var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"small_n/#Small-*n*-checklist","page":"Small-n checklist","text":"Continuous MTP and mediation with tens to low hundreds of units (conservation, ecology, early trials) need different defaults from large-cohort epi software. Theory still comes from LMTP and targeted learning (Díaz et al., 2023; van der Laan & Rose, 2011); the checklist below is about finite-sample practice. See Methods and References.","title":"Small-n checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"small_n/#Before-you-estimate","page":"Small-n checklist","text":"Call recommend_run_options(n; engine, n_mediators) and pass the kwargs into run_lmtp_grid / execute_estimand (or into CausalMediation.run_mediation for mediation; run_mediation_grid is a compatibility façade delegating to CausalMediation.jl).\nPrefer SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS or adaptive_learners(n) over RICH_SL_LEARNERS when n < 80 (Super Learner library must be estimable at the given n; van der Laan, Polley & Hubbard, 2007).\nKeep parallel=false unless you have measured headroom (δ-grid threading can thrash memory on multi-core laptops).\nRun positivity_report (or positivity=true on the grid) and inspect weak_support / unsupported_shift cells before interpreting TE curves (Petersen et al., 2012; Díaz et al., 2023 on MTP-designed positivity).\nTreat the default finite ratio caps as a point-estimation stabiliser: capped fits report unavailable EIF limits. For supported finite-support discrete policies or unclamped, constant additive Gaussian shifts, weight_truncation=false requests the uncapped score and its EIF interval. The small-n profile does not select a cap by minimising empirical variance, and untruncated intervals are not guaranteed to have nominal coverage in small samples.\nFor mediation (owned by CausalMediation.jl, with compatibility façades in CausalTargeted.jl), run mediation_n_mc_sweep and mediation_stability_summary; raise n_mc until TE SE and signs stabilise (nested MC is part of the estimand’s approximation error under continuous MTP mediation; cf. Liu et al., 2024).\nAttach sensitivity_report (tipping-point / partial-R²) for any claim that is policy-relevant under possible omitted confounding (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020).\nTreat discovery graphs as sensitivity, not oracles: discovery_adjustment_sensitivity / merge_discovery_sensitivity! (Pearl, 2009; Spirtes et al., 2000).","title":"Before you estimate"},{"category":"section","location":"small_n/#Policy-library","page":"Small-n checklist","text":"Policies implement scientifically interpretable MTPs (Díaz & van der Laan, 2012; Díaz et al., 2023):\n\nAdditive z-shift: additive_shift_policy (default)\nMultiplicative: multiplicative_shift_policy\nThreshold (“raise if below c”): threshold_shift_policy","title":"Policy library"},{"category":"section","location":"small_n/#Longitudinal","page":"Small-n checklist","text":"Use SequentialPolicy / run_sequential_lmtp for multi-time exposures, with ID from CausalDynamics TemporalEffectQuery when a lag DAG is available (Díaz et al., 2023). For discrete-time event-free probability under MTP, use SurvivalPolicy / run_survival_lmtp (Díaz–Hoffman–Hejazi 2024 spirit; competing risks deferred). Point-treatment LongitudinalPolicy carries temporal ID metadata for single-time pathway→AUC analyses.","title":"Longitudinal"},{"category":"section","location":"small_n/#Sysimage-(optional)","page":"Small-n checklist","text":"See scripts/build_mtp_sysimage.jl in the CDCS monorepo for a PackageCompiler recipe (community convenience; not required for CI).","title":"Sysimage (optional)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Super-Learner-and-schemas","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Super Learner ensemble fitting, candidate libraries, small-n profile helpers, cross-fitted nuisance models, fold-stable covariate schemas, and typed estimand structures.\n\nCategorical adjustment variables are handled automatically across g-computation, LMTP (Gaussian, classification, and hybrid density ratios), LMTP fold-cache, sequential LMTP, survival LMTP, repeated-outcome MSM, and missing-data nuisance pathways. Numeric, Bool, String, and explicit categorical-array columns may be passed without manual dummy coding. A stable internal numeric representation is fitted on the cleaned analysis data and reused across folds. Integer columns are treated as numeric unless explicitly converted to categorical.\n\nMetalearners include :nnls (default for Gaussian), :nnloglik (default for binomial), :cv_selector (discrete Super Learner; alias :winner), and :invmse (:discrete is a deprecated alias for :nnls). Opt-in tree learners :randomforest and :xgboost require loading MLJDecisionTreeInterface and MLJXGBoostInterface (see Methods and estimators).","title":"Super Learner & schemas"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Super-Learner-and-candidate-libraries","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"","title":"Super Learner & candidate libraries"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Small-*n*-profile-and-cross-fitting","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"","title":"Small-n profile & cross-fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Nuisance-models-and-cross-fitted-helpers","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"","title":"Nuisance models & cross-fitted helpers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Covariate-schemas-and-design-matrices","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"","title":"Covariate schemas & design matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"api/super_learner/#Estimand-and-engine-types","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"","title":"Estimand & engine types"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.SuperLearnerFit","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"SuperLearnerFit\n\nTyped SuperLearner ensemble: candidate fits, metalearner weights, library names, and outcome family (:gaussian, :binomial, :multinomial, :poisson, or :negbin).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SuperLearnerFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.fit_super_learner","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"fit_super_learner(X, y; learners, metalearner, folds, rng, family) -> SuperLearnerFit\n\nFit candidate learners and combine with a metalearner.\n\n:nnls — cross-validated nonnegative least squares, normalised to simplex weights with a same-prediction squared-loss check that falls back to the best single candidate when normalisation worsens the blend (default for family=:gaussian). This package-specific version is not equivalent to R SuperLearner::method.NNLS or sl3 Lrnr_nnls.\n:nnloglik — nonnegative Bernoulli (or multinomial) log-likelihood on the logit / probability simplex (default for family=:binomial; R method.NNloglik)\n:cv_selector — discrete Super Learner: one-hot on the CV-best candidate (Phillips dSL / sl3 Lrnr_cv_selector). Alias: :winner. Candidates may include nested_sl_candidate ensembles (eSL-inside-dSL).\n:invmse — inverse training MSE weights (fast fallback; LMTP classifiers)\n:discrete — deprecated alias of :nnls (will become :cv_selector in 0.4)\n\nThe NNloglik prediction rule for binary outcomes is logistic(sum(w[j] * logit(p[j]))), not an arithmetic mean of probabilities. It is independently implemented from the same statistical construction as R SuperLearner::method.NNloglik, which can serve as an external numerical QC benchmark.\n\nSquared error remains a proper scoring rule for binary probabilities; NNloglik is an alternative rather than a universally superior criterion. Its stronger penalty for overconfident errors can be useful when fitted probabilities feed propensity scores, density ratios, censoring or missingness models, or other odds-sensitive calculations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_super_learner"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.predict_super_learner","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"predict_super_learner(sl, X) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.predict_super_learner"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.NestedSLCandidate","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"NestedSLCandidate(name, learners, metalearner)\n\nAn ensemble Super Learner treated as one discrete-SL candidate (Phillips eSL-inside-dSL). Outer fit_super_learner must use metalearner=:cv_selector. Inner ensembles may not themselves be :cv_selector or contain nested candidates. LMTP density-ratio classifiers stay on :invmse and should not use this type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.NestedSLCandidate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.nested_sl_candidate","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"nested_sl_candidate(learners; name=:esl, metalearner=:nnls) -> NestedSLCandidate\n\nBuild a nested ensemble candidate for metalearner=:cv_selector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.nested_sl_candidate"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.DEFAULT_SL_LEARNERS","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Default Super Learner candidate library ((:glm, :mean)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.DEFAULT_SL_LEARNERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.RICH_SL_LEARNERS","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Rich Super Learner candidate library including interaction/quadratic GLMs, glmnet, and tree learners.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.RICH_SL_LEARNERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Lean SuperLearner library for small samples (avoids deep trees / multi-α glmnet / neural nets).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.COUNT_SL_LEARNERS","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Default Super Learner library for count outcomes (:zeroinflated_nb, :glm_nb, :glm_poisson, :mean).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.COUNT_SL_LEARNERS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.SMALL_COUNT_SL_LEARNERS","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Small-sample Super Learner library for count outcomes (:glm_nb, :zeroinflated_nb, :mean).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SMALL_COUNT_SL_LEARNERS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.adaptive_learners","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"adaptive_learners(n; rich=false, include_sieve=false) -> Tuple\n\nCross-validated library selection by sample size:\n\nAlways includes :mean\nAdds :glm when n ≥ 12\nAdds :glmnet when n ≥ 20 and MLJ / MLJLinearModels are loaded\nAdds :evotree when n ≥ 40 and EvoTrees is loaded\nAdds rich glmnet variants / deep trees when rich && n ≥ 80 (if extensions loaded)\nOptional :sieve placeholder flag (include_sieve) maps to extra glmnet α variants when n ≥ 100 (lightweight HAL-like expansion without a HAL dependency)\n\nNeural learners (:mlj_mlp, :mlj_nn_binary) and optional MLJ trees (:randomforest, :xgboost) are never included here — request them explicitly (or pass RICH_SL_LEARNERS, which includes :randomforest) after loading the matching weakdeps. :xgboost remains opt-in even for rich grids because EvoTrees already supplies boosting.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.adaptive_learners"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.validate_contrast_learners","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"validate_contrast_learners(learners; context) -> nothing\n\nRequire at least one treatment-dependent Super Learner candidate. :mean alone predicts a constant and cannot identify binary or shift contrasts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.validate_contrast_learners"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.validate_family_outcome","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"validate_family_outcome(y, family) -> nothing\n\nCheck that family is a supported Super Learner outcome family and that :binomial outcomes lie in 0 1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.validate_family_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.suggest_family_outcome","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"suggest_family_outcome(y) -> Symbol\n\nReturn :binomial when all non-missing values lie in 0 1; :negbin or :poisson for nonnegative integer counts (overdispersion heuristic); otherwise :gaussian. Use with recommend_run_options(...; outcome=) or pass the result as family_outcome on LMTP runners (#34, #36).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.suggest_family_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.recommend_folds","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"recommend_folds(n) -> Int\n\nCross-fitting folds as a function of sample size.\n\nn < 40 → 2\n40 ≤ n < 120 → 3\notherwise → 5 (capped; never exceed max(2, n ÷ 5))\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.recommend_folds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.recommend_learners","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"recommend_learners(n; rich=false) -> Tuple\n\nChoose SuperLearner library. Small n defaults to SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS; rich=true and n ≥ 80 upgrades to RICH_SL_LEARNERS.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.recommend_learners"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.recommend_count_learners","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"recommend_count_learners(n; family) -> Tuple\n\nSuper Learner library for count outcomes (:poisson / :negbin).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.recommend_count_learners"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.recommend_run_options","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"recommend_run_options(n; engine, n_mediators, rich) -> NamedTuple\n\nKwargs suitable for run_lmtp_grid / run_mediation_grid / execute_estimand under a small-n-first policy. Pass outcome= (a column vector) to add family_outcome via suggest_family_outcome.\n\nn folds learners density_ratio n_mc (mediation) parallel\n< 40 2 small gaussian 64–128 false\n40–79 3 default gaussian 64 false\n≥ 80 3–5 default/rich hybrid if rich 32–64 false*\n\n* Parallel remains off by default for memory safety; set parallel=true explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.recommend_run_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.warn_if_folds_too_large","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"warn_if_folds_too_large(n, folds) -> Nothing\n\nEmit a warning when folds > n/5 (unstable cross-fitting at small n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.warn_if_folds_too_large"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.crossfit_indices","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"crossfit_indices(n, folds, rng; cluster=nothing) -> Vector{Vector{Int}}\n\ncluster === nothing: pseudo-random permutation into folds (minimum 2) nearly equal-sized index groups.\n\ncluster given: assign whole clusters to folds (at least two distinct cluster ids required); balances fold sizes by greedily placing sorted-by-size cluster index lists into the currently smallest fold. Test indices for one fold are each returned vector; training rows are the complement across folds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.crossfit_indices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.make_analysis_strata","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"make_analysis_strata(df, stratify_by) -> DataFrame\n\nAdd STRAT column; nothing → single full_population stratum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.make_analysis_strata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.get_target_strata","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"get_target_strata(df; stratum_col=:STRAT) -> Vector{String}\n\nSorted unique string levels of the stratification column (typically STRAT from make_analysis_strata).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.get_target_strata"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.NuisanceModel","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Abstract supertype for cross-fitted nuisance models (OutcomeRegression, ExposureDensity).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.NuisanceModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.OutcomeRegression","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"OutcomeRegression\n\nCross-fitted outcome regression Q(A, W).\n\nStores the full-sample covariate design W (intercept + covariates) so predictions can swap the treatment column without rebuilding covariates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.OutcomeRegression"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.ExposureDensity","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"ExposureDensity\n\nCross-fitted exposure mean model for Gaussian density ratios.\n\nStores the full-sample covariate design W for fold-sliced prediction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ExposureDensity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.fit_outcome_regression","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"fit_outcome_regression(df, outcome, treatment, covariates, folds, rng; learners) -> OutcomeRegression\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_outcome_regression"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.predict_outcome","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"predict_outcome(model, df, treatment_values=nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nPredict using the cached covariate design from fit. df must have the same number of rows as the training frame; treatment may be overridden via treatment_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.predict_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.fit_exposure_density","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"fit_exposure_density(df, treatment, covariates, folds, rng; learners) -> ExposureDensity\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_exposure_density"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.predict_exposure_mean","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"predict_exposure_mean(model, df) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.predict_exposure_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.crossfit_outcome_predictions","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"crossfit_outcome_predictions(df, outcome, treatment, covariates, folds, rng; learners) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.crossfit_outcome_predictions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.crossfit_predict_outcome","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"crossfit_predict_outcome(df, outcome, treatment, covariates, treatment_values, folds, rng; learners) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.crossfit_predict_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.crossfit_propensity","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"crossfit_propensity(df, treatment, covariates, folds, rng; learners, fold_sets) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nCross-fitted propensity scores, combined with inverse-MSE weights and clamped to [1e-3, 1 - 1e-3]. Pass fold_sets to share a partition with other nuisances fitted in the same estimator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.crossfit_propensity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.crossfit_treatment_mean","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"crossfit_treatment_mean(df, treatment, covariates, folds, rng; learners) -> Vector{Float64}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.crossfit_treatment_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.sparse_exposure_diagnostic","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"sparse_exposure_diagnostic(a; modal_prop=0.5, min_nonmodal=20) -> NamedTuple\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.sparse_exposure_diagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.CovariateSchema","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"CovariateSchema\n\nFitted representation of an ordered covariate list. terms are StatsModels terms with categorical levels and dummy contrasts learned from the schema-fit data. The schema defines columns only; it does not impute, standardise, select, or otherwise transform features.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.CovariateSchema"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.fit_covariate_schema","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"fit_covariate_schema(df, covariates) -> CovariateSchema\n\nFit StatsModels terms for the requested covariates in their supplied order. Numeric and Boolean columns remain one column each. String and categorical columns use StatsModels' default DummyCoding, retaining the fitted levels and column order for later subsets or prediction data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_covariate_schema"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.transform_covariates","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"transform_covariates(schema, df) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nEncode df with the exact fitted terms, levels, contrasts, feature order, and matrix width stored in schema. Unseen levels produce an informative error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.transform_covariates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.design_matrix","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"design_matrix(schema, df; treatment=nothing, treatment_values=nothing) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nBuild [intercept | optional numeric treatment | encoded covariates] using a fitted CovariateSchema. Treatment is never encoded by the schema.\n\n\n\n\n\ndesign_matrix(df, covariates; treatment=nothing, treatment_values=nothing) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nConvenience form that fits a covariate schema on df and immediately encodes it. As in earlier releases, requested covariates absent from df are ignored. Cross-fitting code should fit once on the cleaned analysis data and call the schema-aware method for every fold.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.design_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.covariate_design_matrix","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"covariate_design_matrix(df, covariates) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nIntercept plus covariates only (no treatment column). Reused across δ / counterfactual predictions via outcome_design_matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.covariate_design_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.outcome_design_matrix","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"outcome_design_matrix(W, a) -> Matrix{Float64}\n\nAssemble [1 | A | covariates] from a cached covariate design W (intercept + covariates) and a treatment vector a of matching length.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.outcome_design_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.columns_present","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"columns_present(df, cols) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.columns_present"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.Estimand","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"Abstract supertype for causal estimands executed by CausalTargeted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.Estimand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.InterventionalMean","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"InterventionalMean(trt, outcome, adjustment, shift)\n\nModified treatment policy contrast vs natural policy (LMTP TE grid).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.InterventionalMean"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.LongitudinalPolicy","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"LongitudinalPolicy(trt, outcome, adjustment, shift, treat_lag, outcome_lag)\n\nPathway exposure at treat_lag on AUC outcome at outcome_lag (temporal ID).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.LongitudinalPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.MediationContrast","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"MediationContrast(trt, outcome, adjustment, mediators, shift)\n\nInterventional NDE / NIE / TE grid under shifted exposure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MediationContrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.estimand_engine","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"estimand_engine(::Estimand) -> Symbol\n\nDispatch helper: :lmtp, :mediation, :scalar, or :sequential_lmtp. Legacy :crumble is normalised to :mediation via normalize_engine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.estimand_engine"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.estimand_from_query","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"estimand_from_query(query::CausalQuery, adjustment; mediators, shift, policies, treatments, time_vary) -> Estimand\n\nMap a CausalDynamics query to a typed estimand.\n\nTotalEffectQuery → InterventionalMean\nMediationQuery → MediationContrast\nInterventionalPolicyQuery → DiscreteInterventionalMean when query.shift or policies is a DiscreteTreatmentPolicy; otherwise InterventionalMean\nTemporalEffectQuery → LongitudinalPolicy by default (single lagged column). Pass nonempty policies and wide treatments (:A1, :A2, …) for SequentialPolicy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.estimand_from_query"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.estimand_from_pathway_task","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"estimand_from_pathway_task(task, nuisances; shift) -> Estimand\n\nBuild a typed estimand from a registry task and resolved nuisances.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.estimand_from_pathway_task"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.longitudinal_estimand","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"longitudinal_estimand(trt, outcome, adjustment; treat_lag, outcome_lag, shift) -> LongitudinalPolicy\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.longitudinal_estimand"},{"category":"function","location":"api/super_learner/#CausalTargeted.normalize_engine","page":"Super Learner & schemas","text":"normalize_engine(engine) -> Symbol\n\nMap legacy engine names to the canonical symbol.\n\n\n\n\n\nnormalize_engine(engine::AbstractString) -> Symbol\n\nCoerce engine with Symbol and delegate to normalize_engine(::Symbol).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.normalize_engine"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Planning,-certificates-and-plotting","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"Temporal panel estimation plans, MTP execution planning, policy selection, identification certificates, run metadata, MTP effect curves, and synthetic data-generating processes (DGPs).","title":"Planning, certificates & plotting"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Temporal-panel-plans-and-sequential-bridge","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"Use the qualified planner and plan type below when working with temporal identification certificates from CausalDynamics.jl. The planner and type remain unexported during the compatibility period; run_estimation_plan also accepts plans returned by the legacy CausalDynamics planner and its session wrapper. See Methods and estimators for a panel example.","title":"Temporal panel plans & sequential bridge"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#MTP-planning,-execution-and-settings","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"","title":"MTP planning, execution & settings"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Policy-choice","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"","title":"Policy choice"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Identification-certificates-and-run-metadata","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"identification_certificate(...; provenance=CDMProvenance(...)) carries declared graph, mechanism, observation, policy, exogenous, numerical, scale, and coupling identifiers into certificate_dict. Function implementations are not fingerprinted: supply explicit stable identifiers in CDMProvenance.\n\nThe returned metadata includes certificate_schema_version = \"cdcs.certificate.v2\". Semantic identifiers remain distinct from run-time fields such as package version, Julia version, and execution time.","title":"Identification certificates & run metadata"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#MTP-effect-curves","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"These functions visualise effects that have already been estimated; they do not fit an MTP estimator or identify a causal effect. Interval columns can be pointwise or simultaneous bands when the estimator reports available limits; unavailable NaN limits produce a point-only curve. Set ylabel to describe the intervals passed to the function whenever the default \"Estimated effect (95% CI)\" is not accurate.\n\nLoading CairoMakie activates both the vector API and the DataFrame convenience interface:\n\nThe DataFrame defaults match run_lmtp_grid, including the optional clamp strip:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CairoMakie\n\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(data, :A, :Y; baseline = [:W])\nfig, ax = plot_mtp_curve(grid; title = \"Exposure → outcome\")\n\nFor simultaneous intervals produced by the grid estimator, select those columns and label them explicitly:\n\nfig, ax = plot_mtp_curve(\n    grid;\n    lower = :lwr_sim,\n    upper = :upr_sim,\n    ylabel = \"Estimated effect (simultaneous 95% band)\",\n)","title":"MTP effect curves"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Synthetic-TE/NDE/NIE-example","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"using CausalTargeted, CairoMakie, DataFrames\n\nshifts = [-0.75, -0.5, -0.25, 0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75]\nestimands = [\"TE\", \"NDE\", \"NIE\"]\ndf = DataFrame(\n    delta = repeat(shifts, length(estimands)),\n    estimand = repeat(estimands; inner = length(shifts)),\n)\nscale = Dict(\"TE\" => 0.12, \"NDE\" => 0.08, \"NIE\" => 0.04)\ndf.est = [scale[e] * sinpi(x) for (x, e) in zip(df.delta, df.estimand)]\ndf.lwr = df.est .- 0.035\ndf.upr = df.est .+ 0.035\ndf.clamp = repeat([0.42, 0.28, 0.14, 0.03, 0.16, 0.30, 0.45], length(estimands))\n\nfig, ax = plot_mtp_curve(df; title = \"Synthetic exposure → outcome\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFor custom multi-panel layouts, create ordinary Makie axes and compose the curve-level primitive directly:\n\npanel = Figure()\nax = Axis(panel[1, 1]; title = \"Panel A\")\nhandles = mtp_curve!(ax, delta, estimate, lower, upper; clamp, estimand)\n\nThe default canvas uses 120 Makie layout units per inch. Save the exact 8.5 × 4.5 inch curve as PDF or 320-dpi PNG with:\n\nsave(\"mtp.pdf\", fig; pt_per_unit = 72 / 120)\nsave(\"mtp.png\", fig; px_per_unit = 320 / 120)","title":"Synthetic TE/NDE/NIE example"},{"category":"section","location":"api/planning_plotting/#Synthetic-DGPs","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"","title":"Synthetic DGPs"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.EstimationPlan","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"EstimationPlan\n\nCausalTargeted plan linking temporal identification to an estimation engine and wide-panel column names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.EstimationPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation(unrolling, query, column_names; ...)\n\nUse CausalDynamics temporal identification and wide-panel mapping to prepare an CausalTargeted.EstimationPlan for a CausalTargeted engine.\n\nFor same-time contrasts, discrete_treatment=true selects :discrete_lmtp and false selects :lmtp_grid. A query on the same node at later times selects :sequential_lmtp. Other queries select :lmtp_grid.\n\noutcome_specs maps DAG nodes to CausalDynamics NodeOutcomeSpec values. Hurdle outcomes select :two_part_discrete_lmtp and populate presence_col and intensity_col. Binary outcomes use :binomial, and count outcomes use :negbin by default or count_family = :poisson.\n\nPass data as a wide table to attach complete-case support using min_n as the threshold. missingness, skip, and name_fn control identification and panel column mapping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.run_estimation_plan","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"run_estimation_plan(df, plan; arm_hi, arm_ref, levels, policy, kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nRun the CausalTargeted engine named in plan using column names from CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation. Also accepts a legacy CausalDynamics plan.\n\nFor :discrete_lmtp, pass arm_hi, arm_ref, and levels to call run_discrete_lmtp_contrast, or pass policy for a single run_discrete_lmtp fit.\n\nFor :two_part_discrete_lmtp, pass arm_hi, arm_ref, and levels; uses plan.presence_col and plan.intensity_col.\n\nRemaining kwargs are forwarded to the runner (e.g. folds, rng, learners_outcome, cluster). When omitted, recommend_run_options supplies defaults from nrow(df) and plan.engine.\n\nIdentification discipline\n\nA plan whose identifiable flag is false is refused by default (on_unidentified = :refuse): the estimator would otherwise compute a number that the certificate does not license as a causal contrast. Pass on_unidentified = :exploratory to run anyway; the returned NamedTuple then carries identifiable = false and identification_status = :not_identified_by_procedure so the status travels with the estimate instead of living only in a log line. Identified plans return identification_status = :identified (CausalDynamics IDENTIFICATION_STATUSES).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_estimation_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.plan_sequential","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"plan_sequential(spec, id_result; shift) -> SequentialPolicy\n\nCertificate-first planning: merge baseline adjustment from a CausalDynamics IdentificationResult into a SequentialPolicy (mirrors CausalMediation plan_mediation).\n\nEmpty baseline on spec is filled from id_result.adjustment; nonempty fields are kept. Optional shift replaces the numeric policy shift. Factor spec.policies are copied through unchanged. Requires id_result.query isa TemporalEffectQuery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.plan_sequential"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.sequential_spec_from_identification","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"sequential_spec_from_identification(id_result; treatments, outcome, baseline, time_vary, shift, policies, name_fn)\n\nBuild a SequentialPolicy from an IdentificationResult whose query is a CausalDynamics.TemporalEffectQuery.\n\nDefault treatments are name_fn(treatment, t) for t = 1t_outcome; default outcome is the bare query.outcome symbol (terminal column, matching CausalDynamics.simulate_panel terminal=). Pass treatments / outcome / baseline / time_vary explicitly when the wide table uses a different layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.sequential_spec_from_identification"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.MTPSettings","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"MTPSettings\n\nConfiguration container for default MTP shift-grid settings (shift grid deltas, support quantiles, cross-fitting folds, training epochs, shift_scale, and stratum retention thresholds). Construct the package defaults with mtp_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MTPSettings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.mtp_settings","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"mtp_settings() -> MTPSettings\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.mtp_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.default_deltas","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"default_deltas() -> Vector{Float64}\n\nReturn the default shift grid deltas from mtp_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.default_deltas"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.resolved_stratify_by","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"resolved_stratify_by() -> Union{Nothing,Symbol}\n\nRead MTP_STRATIFY_BY environment variable (age, immunisation, sex, or unset).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.resolved_stratify_by"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.MTPPlan","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"MTPPlan\n\nValidated execution plan for one estimand grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MTPPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.plan_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"plan_mtp(estimand, data; id_result, deltas, ...) -> MTPPlan\n\nWhen id_result is provided (from CausalDynamics.identify), it is wrapped into the certificate; otherwise a minimal certificate is built from the estimand fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.plan_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.summarise_plan","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"summarise_plan(plan) -> String\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.summarise_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.execute_estimand","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"execute_estimand(estimand, data; kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nDispatch a structural Estimand to the appropriate targeted estimator and return a DataFrame of point estimates, (where available) standard errors, and intervals, with one or more rows depending on the estimand (e.g. δ grid, mediation effects, MSM coefficients).\n\nPlans nuisances via plan_mtp when plan is nothing. Sequential policies may be updated from id_result via plan_sequential. Keyword arguments are filtered per branch and forwarded to the underlying run_* routine (run_lmtp_grid, run_mediation_grid, run_sequential_lmtp, run_survival_lmtp, run_discrete_lmtp, run_two_part_discrete_lmtp, run_repeated_outcome_msm, …).\n\nMain arguments\n\nestimand: instance of a concrete Estimand type (continuous or discrete LMTP, mediation, sequential, survival, two-part, MSM, …).\ndata: DataFrame containing the columns named in estimand.\n\nCommon keywords\n\nid_result: optional identification result for sequential policies.\nplan: optional pre-built MTPPlan; if omitted, built from estimand and data.\nparallel: parallelise grid or stratified jobs when true (default: true if Base.Threads.nthreads() > 1).\ncache_nuisances: passed through to nuisance estimation.\nmetadata: if true, attach fold/learner/estimator/density-ratio metadata to the frame.\nnuisance_source, temporal_lags, n_mc: passed to plan_mtp and mediation drivers as applicable.\n\nRemaining kwargs are passed only insofar as each branch allows (e.g. folds, rng, learners_outcome, estimator, trunc).\n\nnote: Continuous LMTP contrasts\nPointwise continuous interventions may return rows with limited or no inferential uncertainty; see the specific run_* documentation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.execute_estimand"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.PolicyChoice","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"PolicyChoice\n\nResult of choose_policy: selected label, value table, and optional certificate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.PolicyChoice"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.choose_policy","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"choose_policy(candidates, data; id_result, sense=:max, kwargs...) -> PolicyChoice\n\nEvaluate each labelled estimand via execute_estimand and pick the best scalar TE (first row est column) by sense (:max or :min).\n\ncandidates is a vector of label::Symbol => estimand pairs (or a Dict).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.choose_policy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.IdentificationCertificate","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"IdentificationCertificate\n\nApplication-layer certificate wrapping CausalDynamics.IdentificationResult with column symbols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.IdentificationCertificate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.identification_certificate","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"identification_certificate(result, trt, outcome; adjustment, mediators, nuisance_source, temporal_lags, provenance) -> IdentificationCertificate\n\nWrap a CausalDynamics IdentificationResult in IdentificationCertificate for MTP planning. adjustment and mediators default to Symbol.(result.adjustment) and Symbol.(result.mediators); nuisance_source tags where nuisances were planned from (:graph, temporal, etc.). Optional provenance carries CDM lineage when the result came from a dynamics certificate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.identification_certificate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.certificate_dict","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"certificate_dict(cert::IdentificationCertificate) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nFlatten IdentificationCertificate into string-keyed metadata for CSV join / provenance columns: merges CausalDynamics.certificate_dict(result) with id-specific keys (id_trt, id_adjustment, …) and, when present, cdm_* entries from CausalDynamics.provenance_dict(provenance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.certificate_dict"},{"category":"type","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.RunMetadata","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"RunMetadata\n\nProvenance for a single MTP grid execution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.RunMetadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.build_run_metadata","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"build_run_metadata(estimand, cert; estimator, density_ratio, learners_outcome, learners_trt, n_mc, folds, epochs, parallel, cache_nuisances) -> RunMetadata\n\nCapture one row of execution context: estimand string and estimand_engine, learner stacks (comma-joined strings), MTP settings, UTC timestamp, and embedded cert. Used by metadata_dict / attach_run_metadata!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.build_run_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.attach_run_metadata!","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"attach_run_metadata!(df, meta::RunMetadata) -> DataFrame\n\nAdd one column per entry of metadata_dict to df, each filled with the scalar value on every row (wide provenance block).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.attach_run_metadata!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.metadata_dict","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"metadata_dict(meta::RunMetadata) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\nMerge certificate_dict on meta.certificate with meta_* run keys (engine, folds, epochs, parallel, package version, etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.metadata_dict"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.has_makie","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"has_makie() -> Bool\n\nReturn true when the optional CausalTargetedMakieExt plotting extension is loaded. Loading a Makie backend such as CairoMakie loads Makie and activates the extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.has_makie"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.mtp_curve!","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"mtp_curve!(ax, shift, estimate, lower, upper;\n    clamp=nothing, estimand=nothing, panel_mode=false,\n    ribbon_alpha=0.18, strip_gap_fraction=0.020,\n    strip_height_fraction=0.055)\n\nDraw already-estimated modified-treatment-policy (MTP) effects into an existing Makie Axis. This function performs no causal estimation.\n\nThe four required vectors contain shift values, point estimates, and lower and upper interval limits. estimand may contain TE, NDE, and NIE labels and defaults to TE for every row. clamp contains optional proportions clamped by the treatment policy; finite values are squished to [0, 1]. Missing or non-finite clamp values are omitted. Shifts and estimates must be finite; interval limits must either both be finite with lower <= upper or both be NaN when uncertainty is unavailable. Such points remain visible without an interval ribbon. All vectors must have equal lengths.\n\nRows are copied and sorted by shift within the stable estimand order TE, NDE, NIE. Clamp values repeated across estimands must agree at each shift. The returned named tuple contains plot handles plus series and clamp_geometry metadata, making the result composable and inspectable without mutating inputs.\n\nKeywords\n\npanel_mode=false: use compact line and marker sizes suitable for a manually composed faceted layout when true.\nribbon_alpha=0.18: interval-ribbon opacity.\nstrip_gap_fraction=0.020: gap below the effect range as a fraction of its y span.\nstrip_height_fraction=0.055: clamp-strip height as a fraction of its y span.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.mtp_curve!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.plot_mtp_curve","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"plot_mtp_curve(shift, estimate, lower, upper;\n    clamp=nothing, estimand=nothing, title=nothing,\n    xlabel=\"Shift (SD units)\", ylabel=\"Estimated effect\",\n    figure_size=(8.5, 4.5), base_size=12,\n    ribbon_alpha=0.18, strip_gap_fraction=0.020,\n    strip_height_fraction=0.055) -> Figure, Axis\n\nCreate one complete Makie MTP effect-curve figure and return (figure, axis). The function visualises supplied estimates and intervals; it does not estimate causal effects. Intervals may be pointwise or simultaneous—the configurable ylabel should describe whichever interval the caller supplied.\n\nfigure_size is measured in inches. The default physical canvas is 8.5 × 4.5 inches, represented internally at 120 Makie layout units per inch. title is left aligned. The remaining data and strip keywords have the same meaning and validation as mtp_curve!. If no finite clamp values are supplied, the clamp strip and colourbar are omitted. Only estimands present in the data appear in the top legend, ordered TE, NDE, NIE.\n\nExport at exact physical size\n\nFor a returned fig, PDF uses pt_per_unit = 72 / 120; 320-dpi PNG uses px_per_unit = 320 / 120:\n\nsave(\"mtp_curve.pdf\", fig; pt_per_unit = 72 / 120)\nsave(\"mtp_curve.png\", fig; px_per_unit = 320 / 120)\n\n\n\n\n\nplot_mtp_curve(df;\n    shift=:delta, estimate=:est, lower=:lwr, upper=:upr,\n    clamp=:clamp, estimand=:estimand, kwargs...) -> Figure, Axis\n\nDataFrame convenience interface for plot_mtp_curve. Its defaults match the output of run_lmtp_grid, so plot_mtp_curve(grid) works directly. Required column selectors are shift, estimate, lower, and upper. The estimand column is optional and defaults to TE when absent or when estimand=nothing; the optional clamp column is similarly ignored when absent or disabled. A single error lists every missing required column. Column vectors are never mutated.\n\nLoad a Makie backend such as CairoMakie to activate this method. Other keywords, including title, xlabel, ylabel, and figure_size, are forwarded to the vector API. To plot simultaneous bands returned by run_lmtp_grid, pass lower=:lwr_sim, upper=:upr_sim, and an appropriate ylabel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.plot_mtp_curve"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_linear_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_linear_mtp(n; β_a=0.5, β_w=1.0, σ_y=0.5, σ_a=1.0, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nLinear DGP: W ~ N(0,1), A = W + ε_a, Y = β_a A + β_w W + ε_y. Under an additive shift of δ on clamped A (no clamp if unbounded), E[Y^{A+δ} - Y^{A}] = β_a * δ when clamp is inactive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_linear_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_discrete_survival_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_discrete_survival_mtp(n; T=3, α=-1.2, β_a=-0.5, β_w=0.3, σ_a=1.0, rng)\n\nDiscrete-time survival under longitudinal continuous treatments.\n\nFor t = 1…T:\n\nA_t = W + σ_a ε_t\nhazard λ_t = logit⁻¹(α + β_a A_t + β_w W) among units still event-free\nS_t = 1 if still event-free after occasion t (monotone: once 0, stays 0)\n\ntruth.survival(δ) is a shared-noise Monte Carlo oracle for ES_T^A+δ under a raw additive shift (no clamp) of every A_t by δ. With β_a < 0, larger δ raises survival.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_discrete_survival_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_mixed_baseline_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_mixed_baseline_mtp(n; β_a=0.5, β_w=1.0, σ_y=0.5, σ_a=1.0, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nLinear MTP DGP with mixed-type baseline covariates for CovariateSchema stress:\n\nW::Float64, site::String, vaccinated::Bool, breed::String\nOutcome still depends only on A and W (site / vaccinated / breed are adjustment noise), so under an additive shift E[Y^{A+δ} - Y^{A}] = β_a * δ when clamp is inactive (same as simulate_linear_mtp).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_mixed_baseline_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_binomial_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_binomial_mtp(n; β_a=0.8, β_w=1.0, σ_a=1.0, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nLogistic outcome, additive shift on continuous A. Sample oracle effects(δ) is the mean difference of σ(β_a (A+δ) + β_w W) versus the factual conditional mean on the same (A, W).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_binomial_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_multinomial_outcome","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_multinomial_outcome(n; K=3, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nSoftmax class probabilities of a linear index in W. truth.P is the n × K probability matrix; Y is a sampled integer label in 1:K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_multinomial_outcome"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_categorical_treatment_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_categorical_treatment_mtp(n; rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nThree-level string exposure A ∈ {0,1,2}, Y = β₁ 1{A=1} + β₂ 1{A=2} + β_w W + ε. Default policy recodes 2 → 1. Oracle TE is (β₁ - β₂) P(A=2) on the sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_categorical_treatment_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_sequential_factor_mtp","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_sequential_factor_mtp(n; rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nT=2 string exposures A1, A2 ∈ {0,1,2}, time-varying L1, continuous W. Default MTP recodes 2 → 1 at both times. Oracle psi is the sample structural mean under that recode (g-computation with known coefficients).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_sequential_factor_mtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/planning_plotting/#CausalTargeted.simulate_repeated_outcome_ate","page":"Planning, certificates & plotting","text":"simulate_repeated_outcome_ate(n; T=4, β_a, β_w, ρ, σ_y, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nBinary point treatment with T correlated outcomes (wide layout):\n\nW ~ N(0,1)\nA ~ Bernoulli(logistic(0.5·W))\nshared factor U ~ N(0,1) and independent ε_t\nY_t = β_a[t]·A + β_w·W + √ρ·U + √(1-ρ)·ε_t\n\nTruth: tau = β_a (ATE profile; no treatment–covariate interaction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_repeated_outcome_ate"},{"category":"section","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#Missingness-and-diagnostics","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"Missing-data handling (complete-case, IPCW, mean imputation, and posterior multiple imputation with Rubin pooling), domain transportability weights, positivity diagnostics, and sensitivity calibration.\n\nUnexported diagnostic helpers for hurdle conditional-independence tests (CausalTargeted.test_implied_hurdle_independences, CausalTargeted.local_markov_statements, CausalTargeted.IndependenceStatement, CausalTargeted.default_hurdle_label_to_col) and discovery adjustment-set sensitivity (CausalTargeted.adjustment_set_disagreement, CausalTargeted.discovery_adjustment_sensitivity, CausalTargeted.merge_discovery_sensitivity!) are also available qualified.","title":"Missingness & diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#Missingness-and-IPCW","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"","title":"Missingness & IPCW"},{"category":"section","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#Posterior-imputation-and-pooling","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"","title":"Posterior imputation & pooling"},{"category":"section","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#Domain-transportability","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"","title":"Domain transportability"},{"category":"section","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#Positivity-and-sensitivity-analysis","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"","title":"Positivity & sensitivity analysis"},{"category":"type","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.MissingDataResult","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"MissingDataResult\n\nResult of handle_missing_data: cleaned table, row weights, extra indicator columns, and metadata (strategy, miss rates, optional PCH rung).\n\nDestructuring data, weights, extra = result remains supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MissingDataResult"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.MISSINGNESS_META_KEY","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"Key used in DataFrames metadata for MissingDataResult.meta.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MISSINGNESS_META_KEY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.handle_missing_data","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"handle_missing_data(df, outcome, covariates, strategy; learners, folds, rng, rung, time_indexed)\n    -> MissingDataResult\n\nDispatch missing-data handling according to strategy:\n\n:drop — complete-case analysis (default, preserves current behaviour)\n:ipcw — IPCW for outcome missingness, drop covariate-missing rows\n:impute — mean-impute covariates (with indicators), drop outcome-missing rows\n:ipcw_impute — both: impute covariates, then IPCW for outcome missingness\n\nKeyword rung (default :L2) and time_indexed are recorded in result.meta for stratum × PCH bookkeeping; they do not change the numerical policy. Destructure as data, weights, extra = result or read result.meta.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.handle_missing_data"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.impute_covariates_mean!","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"impute_covariates_mean!(df, cols) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nReplace missing values in each column of cols with the column mean (continuous) or mode (categorical). Adds binary indicator columns col_miss for each imputed column. Returns the names of the added indicator columns.\n\nModifies df in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.impute_covariates_mean!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.complete_numeric_column","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"complete_numeric_column(col; name=:column) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nConvert a complete column to Float64. Throws if any entry is missing (refuses silent coercion; call handle_missing_data first).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.complete_numeric_column"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.ipcw_weights","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"ipcw_weights(df, outcome, covariates; learners, folds, rng) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nFit P(R=1 | W) where R = !ismissing(outcome), and return stabilised inverse-probability-of-censoring weights P(R=1) / P(R=1 | W) for observed units. Missing-outcome units receive weight 0.\n\nUses the existing SuperLearner for the censoring model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ipcw_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.weighted_influence_summary","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"weighted_influence_summary(ic, weights) -> NamedTuple\n\nHajek weighted mean of an influence-function vector, with IF-scale centred ic and matching SE.\n\nEstimate est = sum(w .* ψ) / sum(w). With w_bar = mean(w), the returned curve is ic = (w ./ w_bar) .* (ψ .- est) and se = sqrt(mean(abs2, ic) / n), so se matches the second moment of ic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.weighted_influence_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.attach_missingness_metadata!","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"attach_missingness_metadata!(df, meta) -> DataFrame\n\nStore missingness bookkeeping on a grid DataFrame via DataFrames metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.attach_missingness_metadata!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.missingness_metadata","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"missingness_metadata(x) -> NamedTuple\n\nRead missingness meta from a grid DataFrame or a NamedTuple result that includes a missingness field.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.missingness_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.with_missingness","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"with_missingness(nt, meta) -> NamedTuple\n\nMerge missingness = meta into a NamedTuple estimator result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.with_missingness"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.mar_set","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"mar_set(id::IdentificationResult) -> Vector{Symbol}\n\nReturn the MAR conditioning set from id.missingness, or an empty vector when no missingness certificate is attached. Non-breaking adapter for estimators that prefer reading Structural claims from identify(...; missingness=).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.mar_set"},{"category":"type","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.ImputationDraws","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"ImputationDraws\n\nCollection of completed tables from impute_posterior.\n\nFields\n\ndraws: length-n_draws completed DataFrames (no missing in outcome)\noutcome: imputed column\nn_draws, method, mar_set, meta\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ImputationDraws"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.impute_posterior","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"impute_posterior(data, outcome, covariates; treatment, certificate, n_draws, rng)\n    -> ImputationDraws\n\nDraw completed outcomes under a Gaussian MAR model:\n\nFit Y ~ covariates (+ treatment) by OLS on rows with observed Y.\nFor each draw, sample residual variance from its scaled inverse-chi-square posterior and coefficients from their conditional multivariate normal.\nReplace missing Y with a normal draw at the sampled conditional mean; observed Y are left unchanged.\n\nCategorical predictors use one schema fitted to the outcome-observed rows for both model fitting and missing-row prediction. A category absent from that schema is reported as an unseen-level error.\n\ncovariates should be the MAR conditioning set (or pass certificate / IdentificationResult with missingness so mar_set is taken from the certificate when nonempty). Does not default into estimators — opt in via run_lmtp_grid(...; imputation=draws).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.impute_posterior"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.pool_lmtp_grids","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"pool_lmtp_grids(grids; rubin=true) -> DataFrame\n\nAverage LMTP grid rows across imputation draws. Within-draw se and between-draw variance of est combine via Rubin's rule when rubin=true, with finite-imputation t limits; Rubin pooling requires at least two grids. Input grids must have identical delta, estimand, and stratum keys in the same row order. Because imputed grids do not retain a joint influence-curve covariance, simultaneous-band columns are NaN rather than pointwise limits copied into a simultaneous field:\n\nT = bar U + (1 + 1m) B, mathrmse = sqrtT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.pool_lmtp_grids"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.domain_transport_weights","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"domain_transport_weights(data, domain; target, source=nothing, trunc=0.01) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nStabilised IPTW-style weights for transporting from observed domains to target.\n\nWhen domain is binary / two-level and source is omitted, uses all non-target rows as source. Weights are w_i = P(D=target)  P(D=d_i) clipped away from zero (simple marginal transport; no outcome model).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.domain_transport_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.transport_weighted_mean","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"transport_weighted_mean(values, weights) -> Float64\n\nWeighted mean  w_i y_i   w_i.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.transport_weighted_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.positivity_report","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"positivity_report(data, trt; deltas, stratify_by, lower_q, upper_q, shift_scale, kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nTidy atlas of support / clamp diagnostics across the δ-grid and strata.\n\nUses support_diagnostics and additive_clamp_diagnostics. Statuses: ok, weak_support, unsupported_shift.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.positivity_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.positivity_markdown","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"positivity_markdown(report::DataFrame; title) -> String\n\nCompact markdown summary of a positivity_report table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.positivity_markdown"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.attach_positivity_summary!","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"attach_positivity_summary!(grid, report) -> DataFrame\n\nLeft-join key positivity columns onto an MTP grid by delta and stratum.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.attach_positivity_summary!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.tipping_point_bias","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"tipping_point_bias(est, se; alpha=0.05) -> NamedTuple\n\nSmallest additive bias that would move a Wald CI to include zero (sign flip / nullification). Returns bias_needed, est, se, z_crit, ci_excludes_zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.tipping_point_bias"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.partial_r2_calibration","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"partial_r2_calibration(est, se; r2_du=0.01, r2yu=0.01, n, df_adjust=1) -> NamedTuple\n\nApproximate relative bias from an omitted confounder with partial R² with treatment (r2_du) and outcome (r2yu), following the spirit of Cinelli & Hazlett (2020). Returns adjusted_est, bias, rel_bias.\n\nAssumptions are strong (linear / residualised); treat as a calibration dial.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.partial_r2_calibration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.sensitivity_report","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"sensitivity_report(est, se; n, alpha, r2_grid) -> DataFrame\n\nOne-row tipping point plus a small grid of partial-R² calibrations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.sensitivity_report"},{"category":"function","location":"api/diagnostics_missingness/#CausalTargeted.sensitivity_markdown","page":"Missingness & diagnostics","text":"sensitivity_markdown(report::DataFrame; title) -> String\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.sensitivity_markdown"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Missingness","page":"Missingness","text":"CausalTargeted owns Observable numerical policies for incomplete tables once Structural claims about response indicators R are stated (or deliberately omitted). CausalDynamics owns masks, certificates, and generative dropout; this page covers what estimators do with Julia missing.","title":"Missingness"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Design-rule","page":"Missingness","text":"Same data gap; different object, different assumptions, different API.\n\nNotation (aligned with CausalDynamics / the CDCS book): complete V, response R_V, recorded V^mathrmrec (figure alias V^*), fills tildeV from a documented policy. Counterfactuals stay V^do(cdot); do not overload V^*.\n\nLayer Owner Object\nStructural CausalDynamics MissingnessSpec / MissingnessCertificate / identify(...; missingness=)\nDynamical CausalDynamics + this package Time-indexed gaps; sequential / survival runners\nObservable this package / CausalMediation handle_missing, impute_posterior, metadata\n\nDo not coerce Missing to Float64 without a documented strategy. complete_numeric_column refuses silent promotion.","title":"Design rule"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Strategies-(handle_missing)","page":"Missingness","text":"handle_missing_data implements:\n\nStrategy Outcome missingness Covariate missingness\n:drop (default) Complete-case rows Drop incomplete rows\n:ipcw IPCW on R_Y, then analyse observed Y Drop incomplete covariates first\n:impute Drop incomplete Y Mean/mode impute + *_miss indicators\n:ipcw_impute IPCW after covariate imputation Mean/mode impute + indicators\n\nResults are MissingDataResult: cleaned frame, row weights, extra indicator columns, and meta (strategy, miss rates, optional PCH rung, time_indexed). Grid / NamedTuple runners attach the same meta via missingness_metadata / with_missingness.\n\nFor :ipcw and :ipcw_impute, fitted weights enter the influence-function summary in run_lmtp_grid, run_gcomp, sequential LMTP, discrete LMTP, repeated-outcome MSM, and survival LMTP, so :drop and :ipcw need not coincide under MAR Y.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndf, _ = CausalTargeted.simulate_missing_outcome_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(1))\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y; baseline = [:W], deltas = [0.5],\n    folds = 2, learners_outcome = (:glm, :mean), learners_trt = (:glm, :mean),\n    parallel = false, handle_missing = :ipcw, rng = StableRNG(1),\n)\nmissingness_metadata(grid).strategy  # :ipcw","title":"Strategies (handle_missing)"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Estimand-families","page":"Missingness","text":"Family API Notes\nPoint LMTP / MTP run_lmtp_grid(...; handle_missing=) Default :drop\nG-computation run_gcomp(...; handle_missing=) Same four strategies\nSequential LMTP run_sequential_lmtp(...; handle_missing=) time_indexed=true in meta\nDiscrete LMTP run_discrete_lmtp(...; handle_missing=) Factor A\nRepeated-outcome MSM run_repeated_outcome_msm(...; handle_missing=) Complete-profile R across all Y_t; outcomes are not imputed\nSurvival LMTP run_survival_lmtp(...; handle_missing=) MAR missing terminal S_T ≠ censoring IPCW\nMediation CausalMediation handle_missing Forwards to handle_missing_data\n\nSurvival. Censoring weights for discrete-time survival are a Dynamical object. Do not reuse outcome-missingness IPCW as if it were right-censoring, and do not treat missing terminal S_T as a censoring covariate when weighting MAR event indicators.","title":"Estimand families"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Certificates-and-MAR-sets","page":"Missingness","text":"When identification attaches a missingness certificate,\n\nusing CausalDynamics, Graphs\n\ng = DiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2); add_edge!(g, 1, 3); add_edge!(g, 2, 3)\nid = identify(g, TotalEffectQuery(:A, :Y);\n    node_names = Dict(1 => :W, 2 => :A, 3 => :Y),\n    missingness = MissingnessSpec(:Y; regime = :mar, conditioning_set = [:W]),\n)\nmar_set(id)  # [:W]\n\nmar_set returns Symbol[] when no certificate is present.","title":"Certificates and MAR sets"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Opt-in-posterior-imputation","page":"Missingness","text":"impute_posterior draws completed continuous outcomes under a Gaussian MAR model given predictors (or the certificate's mar_set). Observed Y are preserved. Each completed table draws residual variance and regression coefficients under the Gaussian linear-model posterior with prior p(betasigma^2)propto 1sigma^2, then draws missing outcomes conditionally. The design must have full column rank and positive residual degrees of freedom. Residual-only draws with fitted parameters held constant would omit imputation-model estimation uncertainty. The categorical encoding is fitted once on outcome-observed rows and reused for missing rows, so category columns keep the same meaning; a level absent from the fitted schema is reported as an unseen-level error. Unidentified MNAR certificates throw.\n\nrun_lmtp_grid(...; imputation=draws) runs the grid on each draw (handle_missing=:drop per draw) and pools with Rubin's rule via pool_lmtp_grids, using finite-imputation t limits. Rubin pooling requires at least two draws and identical rowwise estimand, delta and stratum keys; a permuted or mismatched grid is refused. This pooling requires appropriate complete-data uncertainty and a suitable imputation model; it does not validate a clamped-policy score. Simultaneous limits are NaN, because pointwise Rubin pooling does not supply joint bands. This path is opt-in; defaults remain :drop / :ipcw. Turing / RxInfer backends are deferred.","title":"Opt-in posterior imputation"},{"category":"section","location":"missingness/#Stress-notebooks","page":"Missingness","text":"Notebook Content\nMissingness grid Structural / Dynamical / Observable ledger\nPosterior Gaussian MAR → pooled LMTP\nDeep SCM estimation Raw assay missing → drop → encode → LMTP\n\nCDCS harness entry (book repo):\n\njulia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/run_missingness_validation.jl","title":"Stress notebooks"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian/#Bayesian-Gaussian-and-hierarchical-estimation","page":"Bayesian Gaussian & cluster","text":"","title":"Bayesian Gaussian & hierarchical estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian/#Exact-Bayesian-Gaussian-estimation","page":"Bayesian Gaussian & cluster","text":"fit_bayesian_gcomp fits a Gaussian outcome regression with a declared Normal–inverse-Gamma prior. Coefficients have a joint Student-t posterior after integrating residual variance. The prior covariance of coefficients conditional on residual variance is sigma2 * covariance; the constructor's shape > 1 restriction ensures finite marginal prior covariance. Scientific prior scales are supplied explicitly and are never estimated from outcomes. The conjugate parameterisation follows Murphy's Gaussian analysis.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, LinearAlgebra\nprior = ConjugateGaussianPrior(mean = zeros(3),\n    covariance = 4.0 * Matrix{Float64}(I, 3, 3), shape = 3.0, scale = 2.0)\nfit = fit_bayesian_gcomp(data, :Y, :A, [:W]; prior)\npolicy = smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(0.0, 1.0)\neffect = bayesian_policy_contrast(fit, data, policy; deltas = [0.0, 0.2])\n\nThe default numeric design is intercept, treatment, then adjustment columns. Categorical columns use the fitted schema. A custom fixed basis can include interactions. The result describes :policy_vs_observed: the mean fitted outcome under a policy minus that under the observed exposures on the supplied target rows. Target rows, basis and policy constants are conditioned on. For a DiscreteTreatmentPolicy, factual and assigned treatment levels must occur in the fitting data; numeric levels follow the same support rule as categorical levels. A target subset may omit a fitted level without removing that level from the policy's available support.\n\nThe entire grid shares one coefficient posterior. Its covariance therefore retains dependence between shifts, including legitimate rank deficiency. For unknown variance use Student-t quantiles with 2 * shape posterior degrees of freedom. The Student-t scale matrix and covariance are different quantities. These are model-based credible intervals, with no double-robustness claim. Existing EIF inference and its eligibility checks remain separate.","title":"Exact Bayesian Gaussian estimation"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian/#Missingness-and-adjustment","page":"Bayesian Gaussian & cluster","text":"Missing outcomes require explicit Gaussian MAR handling. Fit the observed-data likelihood; predictive draws of missing outcomes are separate from the posterior of a mean effect. Missing treatment or adjustment values are unsupported. Select handle_missing=:mar in the fit and request a draw separately with predictive_missing_outcomes(fit, data; rng). A custom fixed basis has signature (predictor_data, treatment_values) -> matrix, retaining its column meanings. With observation_variance=..., inference conditions on known residual variance and the coefficient posterior is Gaussian rather than Student-t.\n\ngraph_identification_status records whether a matching certificate establishes the query and actual adjustment set; without one it is :user_assumed. outcome_model_status is always :user_assumed, for both default and custom bases. A graph certificate does not establish regression adequacy. Interactions do not themselves change the adjustment set. The conservative summary identification_status stays :user_assumed for a supplied basis, while the validated graph status and certificate remain available. Prior regularisation can make rank-deficient models computable without demonstrating identification.","title":"Missingness and adjustment"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian/#Cross-fitted-nuisance-prediction","page":"Bayesian Gaussian & cluster","text":"BayesianGaussianLearner(; prior) is an optional candidate in the existing Super Learner. It fits each training fold separately and returns posterior mean predictions. Its fitted state is distinct from a full-sample g-computation fit. Coefficient covariance does not enter the existing EIF standard error.","title":"Cross-fitted nuisance prediction"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian/#Validation","page":"Bayesian Gaussian & cluster","text":"Independent analytic fixtures test posterior location, scale, covariance and quantiles. The package test/stress/bayesian_calibration.jl script separately reports prior-predictive calibration and fixed-parameter coverage, with Monte Carlo uncertainty. Neither favourable simulation results nor a proper prior guarantee validity under misspecification or poor overlap.\n\nFor one-level clustered models, Turing NUTS benchmarks confirm that structured VI with correction = :psis closely tracks exact MCMC for clustered Gaussian (K = 30, SD relative error 3.1%, hatk = 047, scaling to K = 300 in < 1 s) and clustered count models (Poisson K = 35, SD relative error 0.68%, hatk = 031; Hurdle-NB2 warms in ~20 s at K = 300 N = 4527). See Bayesian logistic estimation for clustered logistic PSIS scaling limits at large K.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lmtp/#LMTP,-sequential-and-survival","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"Continuous, sequential, and survival longitudinal modified treatment policy (LMTP) estimators implementing Díaz & van der Laan (2012) shifts and Díaz et al. (2023) LMTP variants (see Methods and estimators).\n\nContinuous lmtp_tmle_contrast supports the uncapped influence-curve route only for Gaussian density ratios and constant additive shifts without clamping.","title":"LMTP, sequential & survival"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lmtp/#Continuous-LMTP-grids,-contrasts-and-core-TMLE","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"","title":"Continuous LMTP grids, contrasts & core TMLE"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lmtp/#Shift-policies,-bounds-and-clamping-diagnostics","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"","title":"Shift policies, bounds & clamping diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lmtp/#Fold-nuisance-cache,-targeting-and-inference","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"","title":"Fold nuisance cache, targeting & inference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/lmtp/#Sequential-and-survival-LMTP","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"","title":"Sequential & survival LMTP"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.run_lmtp_grid","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"run_lmtp_grid(data, trt, outcome; baseline, kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nCross-fitted SuperLearner LMTP TMLE grid matching R run_lmtp_grid() (shift_scale = \"z\").\n\nUses clamp-aware hybrid targeting: when many observations hit clamp bounds, the TMLE fluctuation is down-weighted toward g-computation.\n\nDefaults:\n\ndensity_ratio = :gaussian (stable at sheep n; use :hybrid / :classification for large n)\ncv_trunc = false (prespecified trunc; opt-in variance heuristic is not cross-validation)\nestimator = :tmle (score-solving; pass :sdr / :eif / :itmle as needed)\nepochs = 1 (do not inherit mediation-grid epochs=30)\nsimultaneous = true adds multiplier-bootstrap simultaneous bands\nparallel = true when Threads.nthreads() > 1\ncache_nuisances = true reuses fold outcome / exposure models across δ\n\nQuantile clamping creates boundary masses under a continuous treatment law, so ordinary nonparametric EIF intervals are unavailable for nonzero hard-clamped shifts. Use smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(L, U) for a distinct smooth, invertible policy with EIF intervals; bounds must be fixed in advance and uncapped Gaussian ratios are required for those intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_lmtp_grid"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.run_lmtp_contrast","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"run_lmtp_contrast(data, trt, outcome; baseline, q_hi, q_lo, folds, rng) -> NamedTuple\n\nLMTP TMLE contrast: fixed exposure at q_hi vs q_lo quantiles (antibodies / AUC notebooks). For continuous treatment, these point interventions do not have an ordinary nonparametric EIF. Point estimates are retained, but inferential SEs and limits are unavailable; use a justified parametric standardisation model for inference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_lmtp_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_contrast","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"lmtp_tmle_contrast(...) -> NamedTuple\n\nestimator:\n\n:tmle (default) — score-solving separate-policy TMLE\n:eif / :aipw / :sdr — full EIF one-step P_n[Q(d)+H(Y−Q)] (SDR at T = 1)\n:itmle — compatibility label for the same canonical weighted-intercept targeting step\n\ndensity_ratio: :gaussian (default), :classification, or :hybrid.\n\ncv_trunc=true selects the hard truncation among trunc_candidates by clever-covariate variance. targeting_weight scales fluctuations toward g-computation. epochs remains available for compatibility; each arm's canonical score is solved in one step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_from_components","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"lmtp_tmle_from_components(components; estimator, targeting_weight, epochs) -> NamedTuple\n\nTargeting step only (for cached fold nuisances or diagnostics).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_from_components"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_policy_mean","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"lmtp_tmle_policy_mean(...) -> (psi, ic)\n\nSingle-policy interventional mean under the supplied treatment policy. :tmle and :itmle use the canonical weighted-intercept update; :eif, :aipw, and :sdr use the initial one-step score. The centred influence curve is all NaN when fixed weight truncation or another unsupported route gates inference. An identity policy returns the natural sample mean and its centred outcome values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.lmtp_tmle_policy_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.apply_shift_policy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"apply_shift_policy(a, requested_shift, L, U; stratum_mask=nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMatch R .make_shift_LU: clamp(a + δ, L, U) (shift raw exposure, then clamp). Natural policy (δ ≈ 0) is clamp(a, L, U).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.apply_shift_policy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.ShiftPolicy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"ShiftPolicy(scale, lower_q, upper_q)\n\nHow realised shifts map grid delta to exposure changes. scale is one of \"z\", \"raw\", \"global_sd\", \"stratum_sd\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ShiftPolicy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.shift_policy_from_settings","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"shift_policy_from_settings() -> ShiftPolicy\n\nConstruct the default ShiftPolicy from mtp_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.shift_policy_from_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.additive_shift_policy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"additive_shift_policy(; scale=\"z\", lower_q=0.01, upper_q=0.99) -> ShiftPolicy\n\nStandard additive MTP (current default): A ↦ clamp(A + δ·s, L, U).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.additive_shift_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.multiplicative_shift_policy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"multiplicative_shift_policy(; lower_q=0.01, upper_q=0.99) -> ShiftPolicy\n\nMultiplicative MTP encoded as scale = \"multiplicative\": realised shift uses A ↦ clamp(A * (1 + δ), L, U) via apply_policy_values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.multiplicative_shift_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.threshold_shift_policy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"threshold_shift_policy(; lower_q=0.01, upper_q=0.99) -> ShiftPolicy\n\nThreshold / “raise if below” policy (scale = \"threshold\"): for positive δ, observations with A < median(A) are shifted up by δ · sd(A) (clamped).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.threshold_shift_policy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.SmoothBoundedLogitShiftPolicy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"A fixed-bound, smooth logit-scale shift for continuous treatment in (lower, upper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SmoothBoundedLogitShiftPolicy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(lower, upper)\n\nConstruct a smooth, strictly increasing policy on a prespecified bounded support. A grid value δ shifts the logit of the exposure's relative position: L + (U-L) * logistic(logit((A-L)/(U-L)) + δ). Bounds must be fixed before analysing the sample; observed exposures must lie strictly between them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.apply_policy_values","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"Apply the smooth bounded logit-shift policy, optionally within a stratum.\n\n\n\n\n\napply_policy_values(a, delta, policy::ShiftPolicy; stratum_mask=nothing) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nMap grid delta to intervened exposures under additive, multiplicative, or threshold policies. Additive scales (z, raw, …) delegate to apply_shift_policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.apply_policy_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.exposure_bounds","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"exposure_bounds(x, lower_q, upper_q) -> (L, U)\n\nEmpirical quantile bounds for the complete real-valued exposure vector x after Float64 conversion: L = quantile(x, lower_q), U = quantile(x, upper_q). Missing values must be handled before this call. Used for clamping and shift diagnostics in LMTP grids.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.exposure_bounds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.clamp_exposure","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"clamp_exposure(a, L, U)\n\nElementwise clamp of a to [L, U] after Float64 coercion; convenience alias for bounded natural exposure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.clamp_exposure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.shifted_exposure","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"shifted_exposure(a, delta, sd_a, L, U) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nSD-unit shift policy (mediation / z-scale).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.shifted_exposure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.requested_shift","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"requested_shift(delta, a, stratum_mask, shift_scale) -> Float64\n\nMap grid delta to realised shift (R mtp_requested_shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.requested_shift"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.clamp_diagnostics","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"clamp_diagnostics(a, delta, sd_a, L, U) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturns clamp proportion, severity, effective shift mean (SD-scaled shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.clamp_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.additive_clamp_diagnostics","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"additive_clamp_diagnostics(a, requested_shift, L, U) -> NamedTuple\n\nClamp diagnostics for additive (z/raw) shift policies used by LMTP grids. Matches R .effective_shift: shift raw a, then clamp (not clamp-then-shift).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.additive_clamp_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.targeting_weight_from_clamp","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"targeting_weight_from_clamp(clamp_rate; soft=0.25, hard=0.75) -> Float64\n\nDown-weight TMLE fluctuation when clamping is severe (hybrid g-comp / TMLE).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.targeting_weight_from_clamp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.LMTPFoldCache","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"LMTPFoldCache\n\nPre-fitted cross-fitted nuisances reused across δ values in one grid run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.LMTPFoldCache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.build_lmtp_fold_cache","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"build_lmtp_fold_cache(df, trt, outcome, covariates, folds, rng; learners_outcome, learners_trt, family_outcome, weight_truncation) -> LMTPFoldCache\n\nThe cache records family_outcome and weight_truncation for component construction. weight_truncation=true preserves the capped and stabilised weight path; false requests raw static Gaussian ratios and is validated when the policies are supplied to lmtp_components_from_cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.build_lmtp_fold_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.lmtp_components_from_cache","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"lmtp_components_from_cache(cache, a_policy, a_reference; density_ratio, trunc, L, U, shift_amount, shift_reference) -> NamedTuple\n\nRecompute Q and H from cached nuisances (δ-specific policies only).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.lmtp_components_from_cache"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.tmle_score_diagnostics","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"tmle_score_diagnostics(components; λ=1.0) -> NamedTuple\n\nCompute targeting equation diagnostics from shared-fold LMTP components.\n\nReturns score norms for policy 1 and 0, relative to √n, plus a combined score that should be near zero after successful TMLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.tmle_score_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.tmle_fluctuation_objective","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"tmle_fluctuation_objective(ε, Q1, Q0, resid, H1, H0; λ) -> Float64\n\nSum of squared targeting scores after a joint ε fluctuation on both policies. Used for AD-assisted one-dimensional root finding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.tmle_fluctuation_objective"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.optimise_tmle_fluctuation","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"optimise_tmle_fluctuation(Q1, Q0, resid, H1, H0; λ, ε_bounds) -> NamedTuple\n\nFind ε minimising targeting scores via ForwardDiff gradient descent on the one-dimensional fluctuation objective (diagnostic / polish helper).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.optimise_tmle_fluctuation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.diagnostics_dict","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"diagnostics_dict(diag) -> Dict{String, Any}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.diagnostics_dict"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.prepare_clever_covariate","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"prepare_clever_covariate(H; trunc=10, stabilize=true) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nTruncate, optionally stabilise, then re-truncate so weights stay in [1/trunc, trunc].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.prepare_clever_covariate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.truncate_weights","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"truncate_weights(H; trunc=10, q_lo=0.01, q_hi=0.99) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nAdaptive truncation: clamp to the intersection of a hard cap and empirical quantiles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.truncate_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.stabilize_weights","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"stabilize_weights(H) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nRescale so mean(H) == 1 (stabilised IPTW / density-ratio weights).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.stabilize_weights"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.cv_select_truncation","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"cv_select_truncation(H_raw; candidates, stabilize) -> (trunc, H)\n\nChoose hard truncation among candidates by minimising the empirical variance of the stabilised clever covariate (proxy for EIF variance under mild misspecification).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.cv_select_truncation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.wald_ci","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"wald_ci(est, se; alpha=0.05) -> (lwr, upr)\n\nNormal-approximation equal-tailed interval est ± z_{1-α/2} * se with z from the standard normal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.wald_ci"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.simultaneous_wald_bands","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"simultaneous_wald_bands(estimates, ses, critical) -> (lwr, upr)\n\nApply a common multiplier critical value to pointwise SEs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simultaneous_wald_bands"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.multiplier_simultaneous_critical","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"multiplier_simultaneous_critical(ic_cols; n_boot, alpha, rng) -> Float64\n\nMultiplier-bootstrap critical value for simultaneous Wald bands across columns (δ-grid). Each column is an influence curve for one estimand; columns are centred before resampling, as required for a multiplier approximation.\n\nReturns c such that est ± c * se has approximate simultaneous coverage 1 − alpha, where se = std(ic)/√n for each column.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.multiplier_simultaneous_critical"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.robust_residual_sd","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"robust_residual_sd(resid) -> Float64\n\nScale estimate: max of sample SD and MAD-based σ (Gaussian consistency factor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.robust_residual_sd"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.support_diagnostics","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"support_diagnostics(df, trt, stratum, stratify_by, lower_q, upper_q, delta, shift_scale; kwargs...) -> NamedTuple\n\nPer-stratum shift support for MTP grids: restricts to rows with df[!, stratum_col] == stratum, computes requested_shift from delta and shift_scale (z/raw pass delta through; global_sd / stratum_sd scale), compares unclamped vs quantile-clamped shifted exposure, and returns requested_shift, clamp proportions, effective shift mean, retention eff_mean / req, support_status (ok, weak_support, unsupported_shift), counts and L, U.\n\nKeyword guards: min_stratum_n, max_stratum_clamp_prop, min_shift_retention (see body for thresholds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.support_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.cluster_robust_variance","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"cluster_robust_variance(ic; cluster=nothing) -> Float64\n\nSampling variance of the mean of a centred influence vector ic. Unit-level: mathrmVar=ic^2n^2; cluster level: sum within clusters first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.cluster_robust_variance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.resolve_cluster_ids","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"resolve_cluster_ids(df, cluster, n) -> Union{Nothing, Vector}\n\nNormalise cluster to a length-n id vector, or nothing. When cluster is a vector aligned to the input rows, pass input_n=nrow(df) and input_rows indexing into the analysis frame after missingness handling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.resolve_cluster_ids"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.SequentialPolicy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"SequentialPolicy(treatments, outcome, baseline; time_vary, shift, policies)\n\nTime-ordered treatments A_1,…,A_T, baseline covariates L_0, and optional time-varying covariates time_vary[t] observed before A_t.\n\nEmpty policies selects the numeric additive-shift path. A nonempty vector of DiscreteTreatmentPolicy (length T, or length 1 broadcast to every time) selects dummy-coded factor recodes. Mixed continuous and categorical A_t is not supported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SequentialPolicy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.run_sequential_lmtp","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"run_sequential_lmtp(data, treatments, outcome; baseline, time_vary, delta, folds, learners, rng) -> NamedTuple\n\nRecursive g-computation + a cross-fitted sequential one-step score for discrete policies. Numeric exposures share an additive MTP shift delta and retain the existing point estimate, with inference marked unsupported for quantile clamping. Categorical exposures require policies (DiscreteTreatmentPolicy per time, or one policy broadcast to all times). Discrete policy functions receive the baseline covariate context W0; the time-specific density-ratio models condition on the pre-treatment history H_t.\n\nReturns estimate, se, Wald limits, and inference_status. Discrete policies also return per-time density-ratio diagnostics, positivity, and n_changed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_sequential_lmtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.sequential_identification_certificate","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"sequential_identification_certificate(unrolling, query; nuisance_source) -> IdentificationCertificate\n\nRun CausalDynamics identify on a TemporalUnrolling with a TemporalEffectQuery, then wrap the result for plan_mtp / execute_estimand. Application code supplies column names separately via SequentialPolicy; this never invents sheep-specific symbols.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.sequential_identification_certificate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.SurvivalPolicy","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"SurvivalPolicy(treatments, surv, baseline; time_vary, censor, shift, horizon)\n\nDiscrete-time survival estimand: ES_mathrmhorizon^d under an MTP d applied to every treatment time. surv[t] is the event-free indicator at occasion t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.SurvivalPolicy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.run_survival_lmtp","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"run_survival_lmtp(data, treatments, surv; baseline, time_vary, censor, delta, horizon, ...) -> NamedTuple\n\nSequential regression estimator of event-free probability at horizon under a common MTP shift delta on every treatment time through the horizon.\n\nhandle_missing applies to MAR / MCAR missingness in the terminal event-free indicator surv[horizon] (and covariates), via handle_missing_data. That is not the same as survival censoring IPCW: pass censoring indicators in censor so _censor_ipcw reweights the observed risk set. Do not encode right-censoring by setting S_T to missing and hoping :ipcw replaces censoring weights.\n\nReturns a NamedTuple including estimate, se, n, times, horizon, delta, estimand=:survival, inference_status, and missingness metadata. The current quantile-clamped continuous shift retains its point estimate but does not have a reported standard error or interval under the dominated-law EIF.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_survival_lmtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/lmtp/#CausalTargeted.survival_identification_certificate","page":"LMTP, sequential & survival","text":"survival_identification_certificate(unrolling, query; nuisance_source) -> IdentificationCertificate\n\nWrap temporal backdoor ID for a survival / event-time horizon query (TemporalEffectQuery with t_outcome = horizon). Application code supplies column names via SurvivalPolicy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.survival_identification_certificate"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-overview","page":"Overview","text":"Literature mapping lives in Methods and estimators; DOIs in References. Docstrings on exported symbols also carry # References sections where applicable.","title":"API overview"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-sections","page":"Overview","text":"LMTP, sequential & survival — continuous LMTP grids and contrasts, shift policies, fold caching, targeting diagnostics, cluster-robust/simultaneous inference, and sequential/survival LMTP.\nDiscrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD — discrete LMTP, two-part hurdle LMTP, discrete bootstrap, repeated-outcome and parametric MSMs, MMRM, and difference-in-differences.\nParametric & Bayesian g-comp — cross-fitted and parametric GLM/MixedModels g-computation (including NB2 random-intercept fits), non-clustered Bayesian Gaussian and logistic (LRVB) g-computation, and hierarchical Bayesian Gaussian, logistic, count, and hurdle models.\nSuper Learner & schemas — Super Learner fitting and libraries, small-n recommendations, cross-fitted nuisance interfaces, covariate schemas, design matrices, and estimand types.\nMissingness & diagnostics — missing-data handling, IPCW, posterior imputation and pooling, domain transportability, positivity reports, and sensitivity calibration.\nPlanning, certificates & plotting — temporal estimation plans, MTP execution planning, policy choice, identification certificates, run metadata, MTP effect curves, and synthetic DGPs.","title":"API sections"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Module-summary","page":"Overview","text":"","title":"Module summary"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Mediation","page":"Overview","text":"Mediation estimation lives in CausalMediation.jl. After using CausalMediation, prefer CausalMediation.run_mediation, CausalMediation.run_mediation_grid, and CausalMediation.MediationSpec. CausalTargeted.jl retains LMTP, g-computation, MSM, DiD, certificates, and Super Learner workflows.","title":"Mediation"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CausalTargeted.CausalTargeted","page":"Overview","text":"CausalTargeted\n\nCross-fitted targeted inference: LMTP, Super Learner, certificates, and grid execution. Identification is delegated to CausalDynamics.jl. Mediation EIF / TE / NDE / NIE live in CausalMediation.jl (using CausalMediation).\n\nSmall-n profiles, positivity atlases, and sensitivity helpers target conservation and other low-sample causal applications.\n\nDocumentation\n\nMethods ↔ literature: docs/src/methods.md\nFull bibliography (DOIs / BibTeX keys): docs/src/references.md\nSmall-n checklist: docs/src/small_n.md\n\nCanonical papers: Díaz et al. (2023) LMTP; van der Laan & Rose (2011) TMLE; Cinelli & Hazlett (2020) sensitivity. For mediation, see CausalMediation (Díaz & Hejazi 2020; Liu et al. 2024).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.CausalTargeted"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#How-CausalTargeted-compares","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"CausalTargeted.jl estimates continuous and longitudinal causal parameters after identification: LMTP / MTP grids, positivity and omitted-confounder diagnostics, and small-n Super Learner profiles. Mediation TE / NDE / NIE grids live in CausalMediation.jl (soft façades remain here). Graphs and certificates live upstream in CausalDynamics.jl.\n\nR packages lmtp and crumble are the closest conceptual analogues (not API identity; see NAMING.md). Ananke is the main Python LMTP reference. DoubleML is Neyman-orthogonal and related in spirit, not full LMTP parity.\n\nChoose CausalTargeted when you already have (or can obtain) an IdentificationResult, want Julia-native LMTP grids with fold caches, and care about small-n defaults and certificates on the estimate (use CausalMediation.jl for mediation grids).\n\nPrefer lmtp / Ananke when your pipeline is already R or Python end-to-end, or you need a specialised option (e.g. GPU Riesz nets, competing-risks survival LMTP) that we deliberately do not claim.\n\nStack overview: ECOSYSTEM_COMPARISON.md.\n\nflowchart LR\n  subgraph juliaStack [Julia_typed_pipeline]\n    G[Graph_CDM] --> ID[identify_certificate]\n    ID --> SIM[simulate_counterfactual]\n    ID --> EST[LMTP_mediation]\n    ID --> PLOT[DAGMakie]\n    SIM --> EST\n  end","title":"How CausalTargeted compares"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Legend","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Mark Meaning\nYes First-class, documented\nPartial Possible with glue or a limited API\n— Not in that package’s usual scope\nUnique Strong differentiator here","title":"Legend"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Versus-R-and-Python-(targeted-estimation)","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Capability CausalTargeted R Python\nLMTP / continuous MTP δ-grids Yes Yes (lmtp) Yes (Ananke)\nSequential / longitudinal LMTP Yes Yes (lmtp) Yes (Ananke)\nInterventional mediation TE/NDE/NIE Soft façade → CausalMediation Yes (crumble, tmle3 mediation) Partial (Ananke)\nCross-fit Super Learner profiles Yes (lean / rich / small-n) Yes (sl3 + lmtp/tmle3) Partial (DoubleML / EconML nuisances)\nPoint-treatment TMLE (CM/ATE) — (use TMLE.jl) Yes (tmle / tmle3) Partial (Ananke, others)\nConsumes upstream graph ID certificate Unique Partial (separate packages) Partial (DoWhy closer)\nPositivity atlas / diagnostics Yes Partial (lmtp) Partial\nOmitted-confounder sensitivity Yes Yes (sensemakr + glue) Partial (DoWhy)\nDiD / g-computation utilities Yes Yes (did / g-comp pkgs) Partial (CausalML / DoWhy)\nFull parity with every lmtp/crumble option — (deliberate) Yes —","title":"Versus R and Python (targeted estimation)"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#Julia-neighbours","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Package Role\nCausalDynamics.jl identify, IdentificationResult, CDMs (comparison)\nCausalMediation.jl Interventional & natural mediation (TE / NDE / NIE, moc, nested MC)\nTMLE.jl Point-treatment CM / ATE / AIE\nDAGMakie.jl Optional DAG figures (comparison)","title":"Julia neighbours"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#What-is-distinctive-here","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Typed hand-off — plan_mtp / execute_estimand carry identification certificates into estimate metadata\nSmall-n defaults — learner profiles and checklists aimed at tens to low hundreds of units (see Small-n checklist)\nJulia-native EIF grids — no RCall on the estimation path","title":"What is distinctive here"},{"category":"section","location":"comparison/#What-we-deliberately-do-not-claim","page":"Ecosystem comparison","text":"Full parity with every option in R lmtp / crumble (GPU Riesz nets, all mediation estimand flavours, competing-risks survival LMTP). Thin discrete-time survival LMTP is available (SurvivalPolicy). See Methods.\n\nThe CDCS book walks identify → estimate → display end to end.","title":"What we deliberately do not claim"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Bayesian-logistic-estimation-(RxInfer)","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"","title":"Bayesian logistic estimation (RxInfer)"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Logistic-inference-with-RxInfer","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"The optional logistic backend approximates a Bernoulli-logit regression with an explicit Gaussian coefficient prior. GraphPPL specifies Gaussian subproblems, which RxInfer solves inside Pólya–Gamma/Jaakkola updates. The coefficient block retains its full covariance; factorisation separates it from the auxiliary variables. This is approximate logistic inference.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, RxInfer, GraphPPL, DataFrames, LinearAlgebra, Random\ndata = DataFrame(A = repeat([-0.7, -0.3, 0.3, 0.7], 8),\n    Y = repeat([0, 1, 0, 1], 8))\nprior = GaussianCoefficientPrior(mean = zeros(2),\n    covariance = Matrix{Float64}(I, 2, 2))\nfit = fit_bayesian_logistic(data, :Y, :A, Symbol[]; prior)\neffect = bayesian_policy_contrast(fit, data,\n    smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(-1.0, 1.0);\n    deltas = [0.0, 0.5], n_draws = 2000, rng = Xoshiro(42))","title":"Logistic inference with RxInfer"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Covariance-and-convergence","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"The default covariance=:linear_response differentiates the converged variational fixed point. Both variational_covariance and corrected_covariance remain available. The latter is a posterior covariance approximation, not a corrected joint posterior density. In particular, the correction vanishes at a zero-mean symmetric solution; it cannot repair every error in variational uncertainty.\n\nConvergence checks coefficient means, covariance and second moments. Diagnostics also check response conditioning, stability, solve residuals and positive covariance. Failed checks withhold uncertainty: posterior_covariance and policy limits are NaN, while a labelled plug-in effect remains available. covariance=:variational explicitly selects raw moments. It is never silently substituted after a correction fails. Inspect fit.status, fit.diagnostics and effect.metadata.uncertainty. :converged denotes numerical convergence, not certified posterior accuracy. The default maxiter=1000 allows more Jaakkola updates before withholding uncertainty. If status is :maxiter, increasing the cap gives the existing convergence checks more time; it does not establish posterior accuracy.\n\nStandardising continuous predictors while retaining the same coefficient prior changes the Bayesian model. For coefficient order [intercept, treatment, W], let Wz = (W - c) / s, with a declared centre c and scale s > 0. The equivalent coefficients satisfy βz = T * β, where T = [1 0 c; 0 1 0; 0 0 s]. Preserve the specified Gaussian prior by transforming its mean and covariance as μz = T * μ and Vz = T * V * T'. Centring can therefore introduce prior dependence between the intercept and slope even when their original priors were independent. Use the same declared transformation in fitting and target designs. With this prior transformation, the full Gaussian Jaakkola updates preserve logit means and second moments; standardisation does not guarantee faster convergence. Choosing a different prior on the standardised scale is a separate modelling decision.\n\nPolicy effects are nonlinear risk contrasts. A shared set of joint normal coefficient draws is pushed through the fixed target cohort and policy designs. With corrected covariance, this normal moment approximation is an additional approximation. Results include paired grid covariance, pointwise quantiles and Monte Carlo standard errors of the means. Increase n_draws to assess simulation stability; the exact Gaussian release's Student-t formulas do not apply here.\n\nThe mean's mcse does not describe the variability of interval endpoints. effect.endpoint_mc_bounds.lower_endpoint and .upper_endpoint each contain lower and upper vectors: pointwise Monte Carlo confidence bounds for the corresponding ideal Gaussian-law quantile. Set mc_level=0.95 to request 95% bounds (the default). They use binomial order statistics of the same coefficient draws, without additional outcome noise or an extra fit.\n\nlower_endpoint_mc = effect.endpoint_mc_bounds.lower_endpoint\nupper_endpoint_mc = effect.endpoint_mc_bounds.upper_endpoint\n# Compare the bounds after increasing n_draws with a fresh declared RNG.\n\nThese bounds describe simulation precision conditional on the fitted Gaussian moments. They do not measure prior sensitivity, correct posterior approximation error, or provide a second causal confidence interval. They apply separately to each endpoint and policy, rather than simultaneously across the grid. At very small draw counts they can span the full risk-difference support [-1,1]. Unavailable posterior uncertainty also makes these diagnostics unavailable. The distribution-free order-statistic construction is described in NIST Technical Note 2119.","title":"Covariance and convergence"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Prior-sensitivity","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"Convergence and a proper prior do not guarantee useful fixed-truth coverage. Compare scientifically defensible priors on the same data, basis, target and policy before interpreting a sensitive effect. Declare coefficient scales from the scientific units; do not estimate them from the outcome or select a prior because it performs well on a known simulation truth.\n\n# Illustrative scales only; replace them with justified scientific choices.\nprior_scales = [0.5, 1.5, 3.0]\nprior_comparison = map(prior_scales) do scale\n    declared_prior = GaussianCoefficientPrior(mean = zeros(2),\n        covariance = scale^2 * Matrix{Float64}(I, 2, 2))\n    fitted = fit_bayesian_logistic(data, :Y, :A, Symbol[]; prior = declared_prior)\n    contrast = bayesian_policy_contrast(fitted, data,\n        smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(-1.0, 1.0);\n        deltas = [0.5], n_draws = 2000, rng = Xoshiro(42))\n    (; scale, status = fitted.status, contrast)\nend\n\nRetain failed fits and unavailable intervals in that comparison. The common RNG helps separate changes due to prior choice from simulation variability; it does not establish which prior is scientifically correct. Greater numerical precision cannot repair an inappropriate model, prior or identification assumption.","title":"Prior sensitivity"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Assumptions-and-validation","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"Graph identification, model adequacy, basis provenance and MAR assumptions retain separate metadata. Priors never use outcome-derived scales. Missing predictors and hard-clamped policies remain unsupported. A proper prior keeps separated binary regressions computable without guaranteeing accurate posterior tails. Discrete policies require factual and assigned treatment levels to occur in the fitting data, including numeric levels. This check does not establish conditional positivity within covariate strata.\n\nThe independently reviewed derivation and numerical gates are in the logistic contract. The package tests compare Gaussian subproblems with analytic algebra, variational updates with an independent implementation, and response columns with fully reconverged tilted fits. Optional reference scripts compare posterior moments with converged low-dimensional quadrature and separate prior-predictive calibration from fixed-parameter coverage. Independent adaptive integration checks scalar tails and two deterministic nonlinear bivariate policy cases; unchecked grid-tail results remain exploratory. No universal calibration or improvement is claimed. See the validation report, which retains mean errors under separation and the zero-mean correction limit.","title":"Assumptions and validation"},{"category":"section","location":"bayesian_logistic/#Validated-operating-boundaries","page":"Bayesian logistic (RxInfer)","text":"Empirical validation across bivariate (1,000-replication prior-predictive and fixed-parameter studies), multi-covariate (p in 4 5, 500-replication correlated, poor-overlap, interaction, and misspecified studies), untouched held-out scenarios (1,000 replications), and hierarchical cluster benchmarks establishes the following operating limits:\n\nNon-clustered logistic (fit_bayesian_logistic): Prefer the default covariance = :linear_response (LRVB) over :variational. Across 24 multi-covariate cell×prior combinations (n in 80 200) and 6 untouched held-out scenarios (including near-separation and single-control arms), LRVB reduced relative Frobenius covariance error by 8.4–29.8 percentage points over Raw VI (3.0%–6.4% vs 10.9%–36.1%), held exact posterior content at 94.7%–95.3%, and outperformed both Raw VI and matched Laplace MAP on 95% endpoint error. Under very small samples with diffuse priors (n = 20, prior SD 3.0), Gaussian symmetry produces mild conservatism (96.8%–98.1% prior-predictive coverage) rather than undercoverage.\nPrior conflict and misspecification: Tight priors that conflict with the true effect reduce fixed-truth coverage even for exact posterior integration. LRVB maintains posterior fidelity to the fitted model, not robustness against an omitted interaction, omitted nonlinearity, or invalid causal graph.\nClustered logistic (fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic): At moderate cluster counts (K = 40), correction = :psis closely tracks Turing NUTS (k̂ = 0.32, upper-CI error 0.044 SD). At larger cluster counts (K = 100–300), the Student-t PSIS proposal on (beta log sigma_b^2) commonly triggers operational withholding (k̂ > 0.7 or ESS < 400) rather than returning unstable intervals; inspect the returned PSIS diagnostics before relying on correction = :psis at large K.\n\nSources: Durante and Rigon, Giordano, Broderick and Jordan.\n\nSee Parametric & Bayesian g-comp API for the docstrings of GaussianCoefficientPrior, BayesianLogisticFit, and fit_bayesian_logistic.","title":"Validated operating boundaries"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Choosing-an-approach","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"This page maps common biological study designs, exposure types, and sample sizes to the right estimator in CausalTargeted. It assumes you already have a causal graph or adjustment set; if you are still deciding which covariates to adjust for (or exclude), see CausalDynamics.jl.","title":"Choosing an approach"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Where-this-package-fits","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Task Typical question Package\nDraw assumptions How do variables in my system influence one another? DAGMakie.jl\nIdentify & simulate Which covariates must I adjust for (or exclude)? CausalDynamics.jl\nEstimate effects Given data and an adjustment set, what is the effect of shifting an exposure or treatment? CausalTargeted.jl (this package)\nDecompose pathways How much of an effect acts directly versus through an intermediate mechanism? CausalMediation.jl","title":"Where this package fits"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Why-modified-treatment-policies-and-targeted-learning?","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Biological exposures are frequently continuous (parasite load, hormone concentration, nutrient dose, temperature) or repeated over time. Asking \"what if every individual had zero parasites?\" often violates positivity: in some ages, seasons, or habitats, zero exposure never occurs, so any estimator is forced to extrapolate beyond the data (Petersen et al., 2012).\n\nA modified treatment policy (MTP) asks a realistic, data-supported question instead: \"how would the population mean outcome change if each individual's observed exposure were shifted by delta (or scaled, or raised to a minimum threshold)?\" (Díaz & van der Laan, 2012; Díaz et al., 2023).\n\nStandard regression models assume a rigid functional form (such as linearity with no interactions). Targeted learning (TMLE / LMTP) avoids that rigidity by combining flexible cross-fitted machine learning (Super Learner; van der Laan, Polley & Hubbard, 2007; Zheng & van der Laan, 2011; Chernozhukov et al.,\n\nwith a targeting step that removes plug-in bias for the specific causal\n\nestimand of interest (van der Laan & Rubin, 2006; van der Laan & Rose, 2011).","title":"Why modified treatment policies and targeted learning?"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Match-your-study-design-and-data-to-an-estimator","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Study design, exposure, or outcome Causal estimand Entry point in CausalTargeted Basis / details\nSingle time point, continuous exposure (e.g. parasite load, biomarker, dose) Shift curve psi(delta) under additive, multiplicative, or threshold shift run_lmtp_grid, additive_shift_policy, multiplicative_shift_policy, threshold_shift_policy Díaz & van der Laan (2012); Díaz et al. (2023). Getting started\nSingle time point, binary or categorical treatment (e.g. control / low / high dose) Interventional mean or contrast across treatment levels DiscreteTreatmentPolicy, run_discrete_lmtp, run_discrete_lmtp_contrast Díaz et al. (2023). For static binary ATE only, see also TMLE.jl.\nZero-inflated / two-part biological outcome (e.g. infection presence vs intensity; biomass with zeros) Overall, presence, and intensity causal contrasts run_two_part_discrete_lmtp, TwoPartInterventionalMean (or fit_bayesian_cluster_hurdle) API overview\nRepeated exposures over time with time-varying confounders (e.g. seasonal infection, body condition, treatment) Longitudinal policy effect across waves SequentialPolicy, run_sequential_lmtp Robins (1986); Díaz et al. (2023). Getting started\nDiscrete-time survival / time-to-event (e.g. overwinter survival across trapping sessions) Event-free survival probability under an MTP SurvivalPolicy, run_survival_lmtp Díaz, Hoffman & Hejazi (2024). Methods\nBaseline treatment with repeated outcomes across visits (e.g. vaccine trial with longitudinal antibody titres) Marginal treatment–time profile (MSM) RepeatedOutcomeMSM, run_repeated_outcome_msm, msm_contrast (or fit_mmrm with MixedModels) Rosenblum & van der Laan (2010). Getting started\nParametric standardisation (g-computation) (transparent parametric baseline, mixed model, or comparison against LMTP) Standardised interventional mean or contrast fit_parametric_gcomp, gcomp_contrast, run_parametric_gcomp Robins (1986); Hernán & Robins (2020). Getting started\nVery small samples or clustered units (cages, pens, litters, clinics; Gaussian, binary, count, or hurdle outcomes) Posterior marginal policy contrast fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp, fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic, fit_bayesian_cluster_count, fit_bayesian_cluster_hurdle Bayesian Gaussian, Bayesian logistic\nMissing outcomes or attrition (e.g. animals not recaptured, dropout) Complete-population causal effect Observable missingness policies Missingness\nDirect vs mediated biological pathway Direct (NDE / IDE / CDE) and indirect (NIE / IIE) effects Use CausalMediation.jl Vansteelandt & Daniel (2017); Díaz & Hejazi (2020); Liu et al. (2024)","title":"Match your study design and data to an estimator"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Practical-checks-before-interpreting-an-estimate","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Three checks separate a defensible observational estimate from an artefact:\n\nMatch complexity to sample size (recommend_run_options). Biological datasets often have tens to low hundreds of units rather than tens of thousands. recommend_run_options(n; engine = :lmtp) selects fold counts and lean Super Learner libraries (SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS) appropriate for n (Phillips et al., 2023; see Small-n checklist).\nCheck positivity (positivity_report). Even a shift policy can push individuals into exposure regions with few comparable observations. Inspect effective sample size and weak_support / unsupported_shift flags before interpreting large shifts (Petersen et al., 2012; Díaz et al., 2023).\nQuantify sensitivity to unmeasured confounding (sensitivity_report). No adjustment set protects against an unmeasured confounder. Partial-R^2 sensitivity reports how strongly an omitted variable would need to explain both exposure and outcome to overturn the result (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020).","title":"Practical checks before interpreting an estimate"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Minimal-workflow:-small-*n*-shift-curve-with-diagnostics","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"using CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\n# Synthetic cohort: baseline confounder W, continuous exposure A, outcome Y\ndf, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(150; rng = StableRNG(101))\nopts = recommend_run_options(size(df, 1); engine = :lmtp)\n\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W],\n    deltas = [-0.5, 0.0, 0.5],\n    folds = opts.folds,\n    learners_outcome = opts.learners_outcome,\n    learners_trt = opts.learners_trt,\n    parallel = false,\n    positivity = opts.positivity,\n    simultaneous = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(102),\n)\n\ngrid[:, [:delta, :est, :se]]\n\u001b[1m3×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m delta   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m est       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m se      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │    -0.5  -0.255443    NaN\n   2 │     0.0   0.0           0.0\n   3 │     0.5   0.242463    NaN\n\nInspect positivity support and omitted-confounder sensitivity across the grid:\n\nrep = positivity_report(df, :A; deltas = grid.delta)\nsens = sensitivity_report(grid.est[end], grid.se[end]; n = size(df, 1))\n(support = string.(rep.support_status), sensitivity_rows = size(sens, 1))\n(support = [\"ok\", \"ok\", \"ok\"], sensitivity_rows = 4)","title":"Minimal workflow: small-n shift curve with diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#References","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Key works cited on this page (complete list with DOIs and BibTeX keys in References):\n\nChernozhukov, V., et al. (2018). Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters. The Econometrics Journal, 21(1), C1–C68. doi:10.1111/ectj.12097\nCinelli, C., & Hazlett, C. (2020). Making sense of sensitivity: Extending omitted variable bias. JRSS-B, 82(1), 39–67. doi:10.1111/rssb.12348\nDíaz Muñoz, I., & van der Laan, M. J. (2012). Population intervention causal effects based on stochastic interventions. Biometrics, 68(2), 541–549. doi:10.1111/j.1541-0420.2011.01685.x\nDíaz, I., Williams, N., Hoffman, K. L., & Schenck, E. J. (2023). Nonparametric causal effects based on longitudinal modified treatment policies. JASA, 118(542), 846–857. doi:10.1080/01621459.2021.1955691\nDíaz, I., Hoffman, K. L., & Hejazi, N. S. (2024). Causal survival analysis under competing risks using longitudinal modified treatment policies. Lifetime Data Analysis, 30, 213–236. doi:10.1007/s10985-023-09606-7\nHernán, M. A., & Robins, J. M. (2020). Causal Inference: What If. Chapman & Hall/CRC.\nPetersen, M. L., et al. (2012). Diagnosing and responding to violations in the positivity assumption. Statistical Methods in Medical Research, 21(1), 31–54. doi:10.1177/0962280210386207\nPhillips, R. V., et al. (2023). Practical considerations for specifying a super learner. International Journal of Epidemiology, 52(4), 1276–1285. doi:10.1093/ije/dyad023\nRobins, J. (1986). A new approach to causal inference in mortality studies with a sustained exposure period. Mathematical Modelling, 7, 1393–1512.\nRosenblum, M., & van der Laan, M. J. (2010). Targeted maximum likelihood estimation of the parameter of a marginal structural model. International Journal of Biostatistics, 6(2). doi:10.2202/1557-4679.1238\nvan der Laan, M. J., Polley, E. C., & Hubbard, A. E. (2007). Super learner. SAGMB, 6(1).\nvan der Laan, M. J., & Rose, S. (2011). Targeted Learning: Causal Inference for Observational and Experimental Data. Springer.\nvan der Laan, M. J., & Rubin, D. (2006). Targeted maximum likelihood learning. International Journal of Biostatistics, 2(1).\nZheng, W., & van der Laan, M. J. (2011). Cross-validated targeted minimum-loss-based estimation. In van der Laan & Rose (2011).","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"orientation/#Next-steps","page":"Choosing an approach","text":"Executable walk-throughs: Getting started.\nFinite-sample defaults and diagnostics: Small-n checklist.\nFull method-to-API reference: Methods and literature.","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"#CausalTargeted.jl","page":"Home","text":"Given data and a known adjustment set, CausalTargeted estimates the causal effect of shifting a continuous, discrete, or longitudinal exposure, with small-sample defaults, positivity diagnostics, and omitted-confounder sensitivity analysis.\n\nDeciding which estimator fits your study? See Choosing an approach to match your exposure type, study design, outcome distribution, and sample size to the right estimator.\n\nCausalTargeted implements cross-fitted targeted estimators for continuous and longitudinal exposures, including longitudinal modified treatment policies (LMTP; Díaz et al., 2023, JASA), repeated-outcome profiles (Rosenblum & van der Laan, 2010), parametric regression standardisation, positivity diagnostics, and omitted-confounder sensitivity (Cinelli & Hazlett, 2020). Defaults favour small-to-moderate sample sizes. Method-to-API mapping: Methods; DOIs: References.\n\nMediation (TE / NDE / NIE, moc, nested Monte Carlo stability) lives in CausalMediation.jl (using CausalMediation). This package supplies Super Learner and LMTP infrastructure that Mediation reuses.\n\nIdentification is delegated to CausalDynamics.jl. This package estimates parameters once a query and adjustment set are known.\n\nFor longitudinal policy work, CausalDynamics supplies the temporal specification and its edge provenance, while CausalTargeted consumes the resulting panel and declared policy estimand. A policy that persists as one decision rule while its treatment output varies with history should keep that identity in provenance; repeated applications are not independent policy instances. temporal_edge_records is therefore useful for the hand-off and audit trail, but is not an estimator input by itself.","title":"CausalTargeted.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Compared-with-R-and-Python","page":"Home","text":"CausalTargeted provides Julia-native LMTP δ-grids, small-n Super Learner profiles, and typed hand-off from IdentificationResult (comparable to R lmtp and Python Ananke, while interventional mediation lives in CausalMediation.jl). See Comparison for the full feature matrix and ECOSYSTEM_COMPARISON.md.","title":"Compared with R and Python"},{"category":"section","location":"#Related-packages","page":"Home","text":"Package Role\nCausalDynamics Graphs, identify, temporal unrolling, IdentificationResult\nCausalTargeted Nuisances, LMTP grids, certificates, small-n profiles\nCausalMediation Mediation EIF / TE / NDE / NIE (optional weakdep; using CausalMediation)\nDAGMakie Optional DAG figures (via CausalDynamics plotting façades)\nApplication repos Cohort data, registries, concordance (thin)\n\nDesign notes: DESIGN.md · NAMING.md · BOUNDARIES.md · ecosystem principles.","title":"Related packages"},{"category":"section","location":"#Methods-and-literature","page":"Home","text":"Choosing an approach — match study design, exposure, outcome, and sample size to an estimator\nGetting started — executable walk-throughs (LMTP, mediation, sequential, missingness)\nMethods and literature — LMTP, Super Learner, positivity, sensitivity, outcome paths\nBayesian Gaussian & hierarchical estimation — conjugate Gaussian and clustered VI g-computation\nBayesian logistic estimation (RxInfer) — approximate logistic inference and LRVB\nMissingness — Observable policies, certificates, posterior imputation\nSmall-n checklist — defaults for tens to low hundreds of units\nAPI overview — exported types and functions\nComparison — Julia vs R (lmtp, crumble) and Python (Ananke, DoubleML)\nStress validation — catalogued datasets, papers, packages, smoke freeze, and missingness/deep-SCM ledgers\nQuarto notebook: docs/stress/stress_validation.qmd — dataset analyses, expected vs actual, DAGMakie / MTP figures\nReferences — bibliographic list with DOIs\n\nCanonical sources include Díaz et al. (2023) on LMTP; Díaz & Hejazi (2020) and Liu et al. (2024) on stochastic / modern mediation; van der Laan & Rose on TMLE / Super Learner; and Cinelli & Hazlett (2020) on partial-R² sensitivity. BibTeX keys such as diaz2023lmtp live in the CDCS book references.bib.","title":"Methods and literature"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"For step-by-step walk-throughs (identify → LMTP, mediation, sequential LMTP, missing data, positivity), see Getting started.\n\nPoint-treatment LMTP under baseline confounding uses the DAG below (W → A → Y, W → Y). Identification sets come from CausalDynamics; figures use the optional DAGMakie bridge.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = DiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # W → A\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # W → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # A → Y\n\nfig = plot_backdoor_paths(g, 2, 3; node_labels = [\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()\n\ndf, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(200)\nopts = recommend_run_options(size(df, 1); engine = :lmtp)\nδ = collect(-1.0:0.25:1.0)\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W],\n    deltas = δ,\n    folds = opts.folds,\n    learners_outcome = opts.learners_outcome,\n    learners_trt = opts.learners_trt,\n    parallel = false,\n    positivity = opts.positivity,\n    simultaneous = false,\n)\n\nfig, ax = plot_mtp_curve(\n    grid;\n    title = \"LMTP grid (synthetic linear MTP)\",\n    xlabel = \"δ (z-scale shift)\",\n    ylabel = \"TE estimate\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CausalTargeted\")\n# Pkg.develop(path=\"<path-to-local-clone>\")\nusing CausalTargeted","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#See-also","page":"Home","text":"Worked examples appear in the CDCS book and in application repositories. Prefer package APIs over copying application column names.","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Discrete,-hurdle,-MSM-and-DiD","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"Discrete-treatment LMTP (Díaz–Williams classification density ratios), two-part hurdle LMTP, repeated-outcome and parametric marginal structural models (MSMs), mixed-model repeated measures (MMRM), and difference-in-differences (DiD).\n\nDiscrete runners default to capped classifier ratios and return point estimates. Their ordinary EIF standard errors, limits, and influence curves are unavailable with inference_status=:truncated_weights. Pass weight_truncation=false to use uncapped ratios and request EIF inference for supported policies; positivity, missingness, imputation, and targeting-status gates still apply.","title":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Discrete-LMTP","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"","title":"Discrete LMTP"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Two-part-hurdle-LMTP","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"","title":"Two-part hurdle LMTP"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Repeated-outcome-and-parametric-MSMs","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"","title":"Repeated-outcome & parametric MSMs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Mixed-model-repeated-measures-(MMRM)","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"Requires using MixedModels to load the CausalTargetedMixedModelsExt extension.","title":"Mixed-model repeated measures (MMRM)"},{"category":"section","location":"api/discrete_msm/#Difference-in-differences-(DiD)","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"","title":"Difference-in-differences (DiD)"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.DiscreteTreatmentPolicy","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"DiscreteTreatmentPolicy\n\nModified treatment policy on a finite exposure. Recode maps, a static do(A = a*), or a user function (a, wrow) -> a' (R lmtp shift). mtp=true when the rule depends on the natural value of A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.DiscreteTreatmentPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.DiscreteInterventionalMean","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"DiscreteInterventionalMean(trt, outcome, adjustment, policy)\n\nL2 mean under a DiscreteTreatmentPolicy, contrasted with the natural policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.DiscreteInterventionalMean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.discrete_recode_policy","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"discrete_recode_policy(recode; levels=nothing, mtp=true) -> DiscreteTreatmentPolicy\n\nMap observed levels onto new levels (e.g. Dict(\"high\" => \"low\")). Unmapped values are left unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.discrete_recode_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.discrete_static_policy","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"discrete_static_policy(value; levels=nothing) -> DiscreteTreatmentPolicy\n\nStatic assignment do(A = value) (R lmtp with mtp=false).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.discrete_static_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.discrete_shift_policy","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"discrete_shift_policy(f; levels=nothing, mtp=true) -> DiscreteTreatmentPolicy\n\nUser function f(a, wrow) -> a′ analogous to R shift(data, trt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.discrete_shift_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.apply_discrete_policy","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"apply_discrete_policy(a, W, policy) -> Vector\n\nIntervened exposure under policy. W is an n × p numeric matrix whose rows are passed to a user shift function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.apply_discrete_policy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_discrete_lmtp","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_discrete_lmtp(df, trt, outcome; policy, baseline, folds, rng, ...) -> NamedTuple\n\nPoint-treatment LMTP for a categorical exposure. Density ratios use the 2n classification construction of Díaz et al. (2021), with dummy-coded A. Gaussian location-scale ratios are rejected. Missing data follow the same handle_missing strategies as run_lmtp_grid. When cluster is set, folds keep clusters intact and intervals use a finite-cluster correction with G − 1 degrees of freedom. A failed positivity check leaves the point estimate available but marks its interval unavailable (NaN). The default finite ratio cap also leaves the point estimate available but reports :truncated_weights and unavailable EIF uncertainty. Set weight_truncation=false for uncapped ratios when finite-support policy inference is required; positivity, IPCW, and covariate-imputation gates still apply.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_discrete_lmtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_discrete_lmtp_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df, trt, outcome;\n    arm_hi, arm_ref, levels, kwargs...\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nDifference E[Y|do(arm_hi)] - E[Y|do(arm_ref)] from two static discrete LMTP policies. The standard error uses the row-aligned difference of their influence curves, preserving covariance between policy estimates. Each fold's outcome model is shared across both policies; policy-specific treatment models remain separate. Cluster intervals use G − 1 degrees of freedom. Finite default ratio caps gate EIF uncertainty; set weight_truncation=false for supported uncapped inference. Remaining kwargs are forwarded to run_discrete_lmtp.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_discrete_lmtp_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.discrete_positivity","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"discrete_positivity(a_obs, a_policy) -> NamedTuple\n\nLevels assigned by the policy that never occur in the observed treatment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.discrete_positivity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.bootstrap_discrete_lmtp_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"bootstrap_discrete_lmtp_contrast(df, trt, outcome; B, rng, cluster, ...) -> NamedTuple\n\nPercentile bootstrap CI for run_discrete_lmtp_contrast. Uses reduced folds inside replicates by default. Cluster resampling gives every sampled copy a fresh cluster ID. Only finite replicates with positivity supported both at the point fit and in the replicate count as successful. If fewer than max(2, ceil(min_success_fraction*B)) replicates succeed, the interval and bootstrap SE are NaN; the point estimate is retained and failures are reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.bootstrap_discrete_lmtp_contrast"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.TwoPartInterventionalMean","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"TwoPartInterventionalMean(trt, presence, intensity, adjustment, policy)\n\nHurdle estimand pairing a binary presence outcome with a continuous intensity outcome observed only when presence is positive. Presence is estimated with run_discrete_lmtp (family_outcome=:binomial); intensity is estimated on the subsample with observed positive intensity (conditional on Y_mathrmpres  0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.TwoPartInterventionalMean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_two_part_discrete_lmtp","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_two_part_discrete_lmtp(\n    df, trt;\n    presence, intensity, policy, baseline, folds, rng, ...\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nFit presence and conditional-intensity discrete LMTPs under one DiscreteTreatmentPolicy. The intensity part conditions on observed positive presence (presence > 0); the estimand is EY_mathrmint mid Y_mathrmpres  0 mathrmdo(A).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_two_part_discrete_lmtp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df, trt;\n    presence, intensity, arm_hi, arm_ref, levels, kwargs...\n) -> NamedTuple\n\nArm contrast for a two-part hurdle outcome. Returns .presence and .intensity summaries (estimate, se, lower, upper, n). The intensity contrast is conditional on Y_mathrmpres  0 among rows retained after missing-data handling on the full panel. Each component uses the paired arm-score covariance; the intensity component remains a conditional positive-only contrast, not a marginal hurdle mean effect.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.bootstrap_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"bootstrap_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast(df, trt; presence, intensity, ...) -> NamedTuple\n\nBootstrap summaries for presence and intensity arms of run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast. Both parts use the same successful replicates; if the minimum success fraction is not met, returned bootstrap SEs and limits are NaN and the failure summary is retained. Replicates count as successful only when the point fit and both arms of both hurdle components satisfy positivity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.bootstrap_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.RepeatedOutcomeMSM","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"RepeatedOutcomeMSM(trt, outcomes, adjustment)\n\nPoint-treatment profile estimand: binary trt on several outcomes measured on the same units. Engine estimand_engine is :repeated_msm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.RepeatedOutcomeMSM"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_repeated_outcome_msm","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_repeated_outcome_msm(df, treatment, outcomes; baseline, strata, propensity,\n                         folds, learners, rng, handle_missing, estimator, cluster)\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nEstimate tau(t) for binary point treatment and several outcomes with a joint influence-function covariance.\n\nReturns (estimates, se, covariance, ic, outcomes, n, positivity, missingness, …). Use msm_contrast for linear forms c^toptau (e.g. tau(t_3)-tau(t_2)).\n\nMissingness uses a shared sample across times (complete-profile R). estimator=:tmle (default) applies a per-fold clever-covariate fluctuation; :eif is the untargeted AIPW one-step.\n\nstrata may be one Symbol or a vector of symbols. Nuisance models are still fit on the full eligible sample, with these columns added to baseline, while targeting and Hajek standardisation are conditional on each joint stratum. propensity may be a known scalar, column symbol, or input-row-aligned vector; known values bypass treatment fitting but retain positivity diagnostics.\n\ncluster may be a column Symbol in df or a length-nrow(df) id vector. When set, widehatSigma is cluster-robust (sampling hierarchy). This does not change the estimand to BLUP / partial pooling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_repeated_outcome_msm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.msm_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"msm_contrast(result, i, j) -> NamedTuple\n\nWald inference for tau_i - tau_j using the joint covariance in result.\n\n\n\n\n\nmsm_contrast(result, c) -> NamedTuple\n\nWald inference for the linear form c^toptau with mathrmVar=c^topSigma c.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.msm_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.msm_stratum_contrast","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"msm_stratum_contrast(result, high, low) -> NamedTuple\n\nReturn the complete effect-modification profile Gamma(t)=tau_high(t)-tau_low(t) with joint Wald inference. For one stratum column, high and low may be bare values; joint strata accept named tuples or tuples in result.strata.columns order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.msm_stratum_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.identify_repeated_outcomes","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"identify_repeated_outcomes(g, treatment, outcomes; node_names, missingness)\n    -> Vector{IdentificationResult}\n\nIdentify TotalEffectQuery(treatment, y) for each outcome on the same graph. Useful when all Y_t share a backdoor set (static A, baseline W).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.identify_repeated_outcomes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.unstack_repeated_outcomes","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"unstack_repeated_outcomes(long; id, time, outcome, treatment, covariates, prefix)\n    -> DataFrame\n\nPivot a long table (id, time, Y) to wide Y1…YT with unit-constant treatment and covariates. Times are sorted; columns are prefix * 1:T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.unstack_repeated_outcomes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.ParametricRepeatedOutcomeMSM","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"ParametricRepeatedOutcomeMSM(trt, outcomes, adjustment; design=:linear_time, target=:tau)\n\nParametric MSM estimand. Engine estimand_engine is :parametric_msm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ParametricRepeatedOutcomeMSM"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_parametric_repeated_msm","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_parametric_repeated_msm(df, treatment, outcomes; baseline, design, target, ...)\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nEstimate a parametric MSM by GLS projection of the unstructured repeated-outcome IF estimates from run_repeated_outcome_msm.\n\nstrata and propensity are passed through to the unstructured fit. A stratified projection requires an explicit K*T × p design for target=:tau or 2*K*T × p for target=:mean; symbolic T-row designs are rejected.\n\nReturns (coefficients, se, covariance, coef_names, fitted_tau, design, target, outcomes, n, positivity, missingness, …).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_parametric_repeated_msm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.simulate_mean_treatment_time_msm","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"simulate_mean_treatment_time_msm(n; T=3, β, β_w, ρ, σ_y, rng) -> (df, truth)\n\nBinary treatment with outcomes whose conditional means follow a treatment×time MSM:\n\nm(ta)=beta_0+beta_A a+sum_tge 2beta_tI(t=t)\n+sum_tge 2beta_A t aI(t=t)\n\nand Y_t=m(tA)+beta_w W+sigma_y(sqrtrhoU+sqrt1-rhovarepsilon_t).\n\nDefault beta for T=3: 10 05 02 035 015 025. Truth includes beta, tau (implied ATE profile), and m.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.simulate_mean_treatment_time_msm"},{"category":"type","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.MMRMResult","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"MMRMResult\n\nGaussian mixed model for repeated measures (MMRM) reference fit for a static treatment and repeated outcome visits.\n\nWraps a fitted MixedModels.LinearMixedModel and a MixedGComputationResult from run_mmrm. This is a parametric trial-style reference path beside LMTP/MSM; it is not a substitute for discrete longitudinal LMTP.\n\nFields\n\nmodel: fitted LinearMixedModel\ncontrast: MixedGComputationResult (LS-mean-style trajectory contrast)\nformula: applied MixedModels formula\ncovariance: :random_intercept or :unstructured (random-effects approximation)\ntime_categorical: whether :unstructured used an internal categorical visit factor\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MMRMResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.fit_mmrm","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"fit_mmrm(\n    data;\n    outcome, treatment, time, id,\n    baseline=Symbol[], covariance=:random_intercept\n) -> LinearMixedModel\n\nFit a Gaussian MMRM with fixed effects outcome ~ treatment * time + baseline and a subject random-effects structure.\n\nCovariance (covariance=)\n\n:random_intercept — (1 | id) (default)\n:unstructured — (1 + _mmrm_time | id) with an internal categorical visit column _mmrm_time (MixedModels random-effects approximation to unstructured within-subject correlation; not SAS/R mmrm residual Sigma)\n\nRandom slopes are intentionally unsupported in this release; see issue #25.\n\nRequires using MixedModels (loads CausalTargetedMixedModelsExt).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_mmrm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_mmrm","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_mmrm(\n    data;\n    outcome, treatment, time, id,\n    values=(0, 1), baseline=Symbol[], covariance=:random_intercept,\n    random_effects=:zero, strata=nothing\n) -> MMRMResult\n\nFit an MMRM and return visit-specific contrast estimates via mixed_g_computation.\n\nKeywords for g-computation (random_effects, strata, …) are explicit; remaining keywords forward to MixedModels.fit (progress, REML, etc.).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_mmrm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_did_2x2","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_did_2x2(df; unit, time, treatment, outcome) -> NamedTuple\n\nClassic 2×2 difference-in-differences estimator for panel data with two periods.\n\nAssumes:\n\ntime column has exactly two values (sorted: pre, post)\ntreatment is time-invariant group indicator (0/1)\nParallel trends hold\n\nReturns (att, se, ci_lower, ci_upper, n_treated, n_control).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_did_2x2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.run_did_staggered","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"run_did_staggered(df; unit, time, treatment, outcome, cohort) -> DataFrame\n\nStaggered difference-in-differences using cohort-time group ATTs.\n\nFor each treatment cohort, estimates ATT(g, t) for post-treatment periods using never-treated units as controls (Callaway–Sant'Anna style).\n\nReturns a DataFrame with columns: cohort, time, att, se, n_treated, n_control.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_did_staggered"},{"category":"function","location":"api/discrete_msm/#CausalTargeted.aggregate_did","page":"Discrete, hurdle, MSM & DiD","text":"aggregate_did(res::DataFrame) -> NamedTuple\n\nSimple equal-weight ATT from staggered DiD results. When produced by run_did_staggered, the standard error uses its full unit-level influence covariance, including shared never-treated controls. Cell-only tables report unavailable uncertainty. independence_se retains the historical independence approximation as a diagnostic only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.aggregate_did"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Stress-validation","page":"Stress validation","text":"Application-layer stress tests for the owned Julia causal stack. Package test/ suites stay lean; broader functionality, edge cases, recovery Monte Carlo, and wall-clock checks live in the CDCS application harness.\n\nThe distinctive demonstration is integration along the CDCS spine: Structural (identify / GraphSCM / do / shared mathbfu) → Dynamical (sequential / survival) → Observable (LMTP, mediation, real cohorts) → audit, with Turing for small n and RxInfer for larger tables. Capability matrix: ECOSYSTEM_COMPARISON.md.\n\nMethods notebook (Quarto): docs/stress/stress_validation.qmd runs dataset-by-dataset analyses with expected vs actual results, timings, and DAGMakie / plot_mtp_curve / posterior figures. Fixtures: docs/data/.\n\nUnified Bayes audit env: CausalTargeted + CausalDynamics + RxInfer 4+ resolve together from General (PrettyTables 2.x via DataFrames / MLJ; no owned-package pin). The CDCS book Project.toml is the reference stack; package test/ includes an RxInfer load + backdoor smoke test (#30). See CausalDynamics RxInfer integration.\n\nCanonical harness (data + runners): SimonAB/causal-dynamics-book → scripts/stress_harness/ and data/catalog.toml.\n\nHierarchical nesting / cluster sandwich: generative nested U and DAG unrolling live in CausalDynamics (hierarchy stress); MSM cluster= recovery is covered there and in test/test_msm.jl.\n\nZero-inflated / hurdle outcomes: two-part discrete LMTP (hurdle panel stress); Gaussian repeated measures (MMRM stress). Outcome routing table in methods.md.\n\nJulia↔R concordance (known-truth LMTP / mediation vs lmtp / crumble) remains under scripts/synthetic_benchmark/ in the same repository. Do not promote Super Learner defaults from a single Monte Carlo batch.","title":"Stress validation"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Packages-under-test","page":"Stress validation","text":"Package Role in stress suite Repository Docs\nCausalTargeted.jl LMTP / g-comp / sequential / survival grids; Super Learner; missing-data strategies; positivity this package Documenter\nCausalDynamics.jl Graphs, identify, certificates, panel / CDM bridges GitHub Documenter\nCausalMediation.jl Interventional / natural mediation engines on JOBS II and synthetic MTP mediation GitHub Documenter\nDAGMakie.jl Adjustment / DAG display on toy identify scenarios GitHub Documenter","title":"Packages under test"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Related-third-party-packages","page":"Stress validation","text":"Package Use in this programme\nTMLE.jl Complementary point-treatment CM / ATE / AIE; CausalDynamics prepare_for_tmle\nCausalInference.jl Upstream graphical criteria / discovery smoke (e.g. NCI60)\nGraphs.jl DAG representation\nMLJ.jl (+ LinearModels / DecisionTree / EvoTrees, optional) Super Learner candidates\nCairoMakie.jl Optional MTP curve and DAG figures\nR lmtp / crumble Dual-stack concordance only (not in the stress runners)","title":"Related third-party packages"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Design","page":"Stress validation","text":"Profile Intent\nsmoke Minutes; every smoke=true catalog row\nnightly Hours; large n and rich libraries\nfull Exhaustive pre-release\n\nRunners (scripts/stress_harness/):\n\nFunctionality — finite estimates or documented throws per catalog engine\nRecovery MC — multi-seed absolute error and 95% coverage vs oracle (synthetic)\nPerformance — wall time for lean LMTP on mixed baselines and real microdata\n\nEnvironment knobs: STRESS_PROFILE, STRESS_N, STRESS_SEEDS, STRESS_FOLDS, STRESS_T.\n\nscale Typical n Role\ntiny le 40 Conservation / ecology field trials\nsmall le 200 Published sheep cohort, airquality\nmedium le 2times 10^3 IHDP, CPS sample\nlarge ge 2times 10^3 Bird counts, Twins sample (nightly)\n\ndomain Role\nconservation_biology Liu et al. CircVax sheep; synthetic sheep / tiny panels\necology Lizards, Bt corn, bird counts\nci_benchmark IHDP, Twins, Lalonde/CPS, JOBS II\nmethods_synthetic Package DGPs (MTP, schema, missingness)","title":"Design"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Methodological-source-papers","page":"Stress validation","text":"Full bibliographic list: References. Stress design leans on:","title":"Methodological source papers"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Estimation-and-policies","page":"Stress validation","text":"Díaz, Williams, Hoffman & Schenck (2023). Nonparametric causal effects based on longitudinal modified treatment policies. JASA. doi:10.1080/01621459.2021.1955691\nWilliams & Díaz (2023). lmtp: An R package for estimating the causal effects of modified treatment policies. Observational Studies. muse.jhu.edu/article/883479\nDíaz & Hejazi (2020). Causal mediation analysis for stochastic interventions. JRSS-B. doi:10.1111/rssb.12362\nLiu, Williams, Rudolph & Díaz (2024). General targeted machine learning for modern causal mediation analysis. arXiv:2408.14620\nvan der Laan & Rose (2011). Targeted Learning. Springer — IPCW-TMLE and Super Learner practice","title":"Estimation and policies"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Missing-data-and-positivity","page":"Stress validation","text":"Petersen et al. (2012). Diagnosing and responding to violations in the positivity assumption. Stat Methods Med Res. doi:10.1177/0962280210386207\nWeberpals et al. (2024) — missing-data methods in TMLE (see harness notes / ISSUES)\nBerrevoets et al. (AISTATS 2023) — selective imputation for treatment-effect estimation","title":"Missing data and positivity"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Identification-(CausalDynamics)","page":"Stress validation","text":"Pearl (2009). Causality (2nd ed.)\nShpitser & Pearl (2006). Identification of joint interventional distributions… AAAI","title":"Identification (CausalDynamics)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Datasets-and-literature-anchors","page":"Stress validation","text":"Catalog registry: data/catalog.toml in the book repository. Fixtures under data/fixtures/; processed tables under data/processed/ (gitignored raw downloads).","title":"Datasets and literature anchors"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Conservation-biology","page":"Stress validation","text":"Catalog id n Source Link\nsheep_vaccine_liu2022 62 Liu et al. (2022). Vaccine-induced time- and age-dependent mucosal immunity… npj Vaccines. Paper doi:10.1038/s41541-022-00501-0; phenotype workbook SimonAB/Liu2022\nsheep_vaccine_synthetic_tiny 36 Package / harness synthetic —\nconservation_panel_tiny 24 × T=3 Harness sequential panel —","title":"Conservation biology"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Ecology-(public-microdata-via-[Rdatasets](https://vincentarelbundock.github.io/Rdatasets/))","page":"Stress validation","text":"Catalog id n Table Link\necology_lizards_tiny 24 aod::lizards CSV\necology_bt_corn_tiny 16 agridat::gathmann.bt CSV\necology_bird_counts_large ~18k bayesrules::bird_counts (nightly) CSV\ncontinuous_exposure_micro / airquality_with_missing 153 datasets::airquality CSV","title":"Ecology (public microdata via Rdatasets)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Causal-inference-benchmarks","page":"Stress validation","text":"Catalog id Source Link / note\nihdp_npci_1 Hill (2011) IHDP NPCI; CEVAE mirror ihdpnpci1.csv — Louizos et al. (2017) CEVAE\ntwins_mortality_sample US twins / Almond; CEVAE mirror (nightly) TWINS\nlalonde_nsw LaLonde NSW (MatchIt::lalonde) CSV\ncps_mixtape_sample Dehejia–Wahba / CPS mixtape lineage (nightly) CSV\nmediation_jobs Imai et al. JOBS II (mediation::jobs) CSV","title":"Causal-inference benchmarks"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Methods-synthetics-(in-package-DGPs)","page":"Stress validation","text":"Exported or in-module generators exercised by the harness include simulate_linear_mtp, simulate_mixed_baseline_mtp, simulate_weak_positivity_mtp, simulate_missing_outcome_mtp, simulate_missing_covariate_mtp, simulate_discrete_survival_mtp, simulate_continuous_mtp_mediation, and harness factories for high-cardinality sites, wide baselines, and sequential panels. See src/synthetic.jl.","title":"Methods synthetics (in-package DGPs)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Smoke-profile-freeze-(2026-08-13)","page":"Stress validation","text":"Snapshots committed under scripts/stress_harness/results/ in the book repository:\n\nRunner Artefact Headline\nFunctionality functionality_smoke_latest.tsv 31/31 engines ok (including documented expected gaps)\nRecovery MC recovery_mc_smoke_latest.csv Lean SL, two seeds: linear / mixed / missing-outcome recover; weak positivity noisy; missing-covariate impute poor coverage\nPerformance perf_baseline_smoke.tsv Lean LMTP wall times (machine-dependent)\n\nInterpretation notes for recovery:\n\nlinear_mtp / mixed_baseline_mtp / missing_outcome_mtp — small error on this freeze\nweak_positivity_mtp — noisy by design\nmissing_covariate_mtp under :impute — soft failure mode until IPCW / imputation paths harden","title":"Smoke-profile freeze (2026-08-13)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Open-gaps-(tracked-issues)","page":"Stress validation","text":"Gap Issue\nSequential Monte Carlo oracles Notebook audit (some dynamical rows still self-check)\nMIRS spectra, full Twins X, large ecology LMTP causal-dynamics-book#14\n\nClosed in 2026-08-14 cycle: IPCW in LMTP/g-comp (CT#9); sequential missing Y under :drop (CT#10); survival MAR S_T IPCW wiring; mean-only contrast guard (CT#11); fold-stable mediation schema (CT#8 / CM#3); GraphSCM sorted parent order (CD#8); g-comp refitting bootstrap (CT#13); mediation PPL handle_missing (CM#4).\n\nHarness notes: ISSUES.md.","title":"Open gaps (tracked issues)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Reproducing-the-suite","page":"Stress validation","text":"From a checkout of causal-dynamics-book (with owned packages developed under packages/):\n\njulia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/generate_synthetic.jl\njulia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/fetch_real.jl --write-hash\nSTRESS_PROFILE=smoke julia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/run_functionality.jl\nSTRESS_PROFILE=smoke STRESS_SEEDS=5 julia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/run_recovery_mc.jl\nSTRESS_PROFILE=smoke julia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/run_performance.jl\n\nCopy refreshed artefacts into scripts/stress_harness/results/ when freezing a new methods snapshot. Harness README: scripts/stress_harness/README.md.","title":"Reproducing the suite"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Minimal-local-checks-(no-full-catalog)","page":"Stress validation","text":"Without the book tree, package unit tests remain the merge gate:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nFor a hand smoke of mixed baselines and missing strategies:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\ndf, t = simulate_mixed_baseline_mtp(120; rng = StableRNG(1))\nrun_lmtp_grid(df, :A, :Y; baseline = t.baseline, deltas = [0.0, 0.5],\n              folds = 2, learners_outcome = DEFAULT_SL_LEARNERS, parallel = false)\n\nSee also Small-n checklist and Methods and literature.","title":"Minimal local checks (no full catalog)"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Deep-SCM-estimation-stress","page":"Stress validation","text":"Sibling to the main stress validation notebook above: synthetic bump spectra → RepresentationSpec codes → mediation / LMTP / missing Y.\n\nQuarto: docs/stress/deep_scm_estimation_stress.qmd\n\ncd docs/stress && quarto render deep_scm_estimation_stress.qmd\n\nMechanisms and L3 live in CausalDynamics Deep SCM stress. Full MIRS / Twins cohort ingestion remains an open gap (causal-dynamics-book#14).","title":"Deep SCM estimation stress"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Missingness-grid-stress","page":"Stress validation","text":"Cross-stratum ledger for incomplete observation. Narrative API catalogue: Missingness.\n\nStructural — MissingnessSpec / certify_missingness / identify(...; missingness=)\nDynamical — sequential and survival under handle_missing; generative masks via CausalDynamics\nObservable — :drop / :ipcw / :impute / :ipcw_impute on LMTP, g-comp, mediation; opt-in impute_posterior\n\nQuarto notebook: docs/stress/missingness_grid_stress.qmd\n\nPosterior-only sibling: docs/stress/missingness_posterior_stress.qmd\n\ncd docs/stress && quarto render missingness_grid_stress.qmd\n\nFrom the CDCS book repo, run the focused edge suites plus smoke harness:\n\njulia --project=. --threads=auto scripts/stress_harness/run_missingness_validation.jl","title":"Missingness grid stress"},{"category":"section","location":"stress_validation/#Missingness-posterior-stress","page":"Stress validation","text":"Opt-in Gaussian MAR nested-MC imputation (impute_posterior) pooled into run_lmtp_grid(...; imputation=draws) under Rubin's rule. Cheap :drop / :ipcw remain defaults; Turing / RxInfer backends are deferred.\n\nQuarto: docs/stress/missingness_posterior_stress.qmd\n\ncd docs/stress && quarto render missingness_posterior_stress.qmd\n\nStructural claims come from CausalDynamics MissingnessSpec / certify_missingness (see identify(...; missingness=)).","title":"Missingness posterior stress"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting Started","text":"CausalTargeted estimates LMTP and related interventional targets once CausalDynamics.jl has supplied a graph and adjustment set. The walk-throughs below run end to end in Documenter (synthetic data, small folds) so you can copy the pattern onto cohort tables.\n\nInstall from General, or develop a local clone:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"CausalTargeted\")  # resolves CausalDynamics from General\n# Pkg.develop(path=\"<path-to-local-clone>\")  # e.g. after git clone\n\nLoad plotting backends when you want figures:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nMediation examples additionally need using CausalMediation.\n\nEach walk-through below plots DAGs in the style of Cinelli, Forney & Pearl (2024, SMR): a good-control triangle (dagplot_confounding) for total-effect identification, a full mediation DAG (with M on causal paths) for mediation identification, then the estimand path (dagplot_chain or dagplot_mediation) with structural_edge_labels on edges where needed. Custom dagplot layouts use edge_routing / CurvedEdge on skip chords (see CausalMediation Getting started).\n\nPapers implemented in each section are named at the section opening; the full map is in Methods and References.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#1.-Identify,-then-estimate-a-δ-grid-(LMTP)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Implements continuous MTP / LMTP point-treatment grids of Díaz et al. (2023, JASA), with stochastic-shift roots in Díaz & van der Laan (2012, Biometrics). R companion: Williams & Díaz (2023, Observational Studies).\n\nGraph. Baseline confounding of a continuous exposure (W → A → Y, W → Y).\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, Graphs, StableRNGs\n\ng = DiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # W → A\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # W → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # A → Y\nnames = Dict(1 => :W, 2 => :A, 3 => :Y)\n\nid = identify(g, TotalEffectQuery(:A, :Y); node_names = names)\nid.identifiable, id.adjustment, id.strategy\n(true, [:W], :backdoor)\n\nGraph (identification). Good-control triangle: W is adjusted; paths carry no estimates.\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),  # good control W (Cinelli et al. 2024)\n    title = \"Good control W (identification)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nData and defaults. recommend_run_options picks folds and learners for the sample size; pass the kwargs into run_lmtp_grid.\n\ndf, truth = simulate_linear_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(1))\nopts = recommend_run_options(size(df, 1); engine = :lmtp)\nδ = collect(-1.0:0.25:1.0)  # z-scale grid; default_deltas() uses -2:0.1:2\n\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W],\n    deltas = δ,\n    folds = opts.folds,\n    learners_outcome = opts.learners_outcome,\n    learners_trt = opts.learners_trt,\n    parallel = false,\n    positivity = opts.positivity,\n    simultaneous = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(2),\n)\ngrid[:, [:delta, :est, :se]]\n\u001b[1m9×3 DataFrame\u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m Row \u001b[0m│\u001b[1m delta   \u001b[0m\u001b[1m est       \u001b[0m\u001b[1m se      \u001b[0m\n\u001b[1m     \u001b[0m│\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64   \u001b[0m\u001b[90m Float64 \u001b[0m\n─────┼─────────────────────────────\n   1 │   -1.0   -0.502879    NaN\n   2 │   -0.75  -0.375569    NaN\n   3 │   -0.5   -0.246681    NaN\n   4 │   -0.25  -0.121983    NaN\n   5 │    0.0    0.0           0.0\n   6 │    0.25   0.121261    NaN\n   7 │    0.5    0.237855    NaN\n   8 │    0.75   0.351011    NaN\n   9 │    1.0    0.455583    NaN\n\nCertificate. Attach identification provenance before archiving results:\n\nestimand = estimand_from_query(TotalEffectQuery(:A, :Y), [:W])\ncert = identification_certificate(id, :A, :Y; adjustment = [:W])\nmeta = build_run_metadata(estimand, cert; folds = 2)\nmeta.certificate.result.identifiable\ntrue\n\nGraph (total effect). Estimand path A → Y only (confounder omitted from the TE diagram).\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\nδ_show = 0.5\nte = only(grid[abs.(grid.delta .- δ_show) .< 1e-8, :est])\ng_te, _ = chain_graph([\"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_chain([\"A\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\"TE\\n$(round(te; digits = 2))\"]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"Total effect (δ = $δ_show)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFigure. MTP point estimates (plot_mtp_curve needs CairoMakie). The default capped-weight fit reports unavailable inferential limits, so this example draws no uncertainty band:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CairoMakie, StableRNGs\n\ndf, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(3))\nδ = collect(-1.0:0.2:1.0)\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W], deltas = δ,\n    folds = 2, parallel = false, simultaneous = false, rng = StableRNG(4),\n)\nfig, ax = plot_mtp_curve(grid; title = \"LMTP δ-grid (synthetic)\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFurther detail: Methods — LMTP · Small-n checklist.\n\nFor an unclamped, constant additive Gaussian shift, the direct contrast helper also offers an uncapped ratio route with EIF-based uncertainty. This route requires density_ratio=:gaussian and the same constant additive shift for both policy vectors:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndata, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(300; rng = StableRNG(14))\na0 = copy(data.A)\na1 = data.A .+ 0.5\nfit = lmtp_tmle_contrast(\n    data, :A, :Y, [:W], a1, a0, 3, StableRNG(15);\n    density_ratio = :gaussian,\n    weight_truncation = false,\n)\n(fit.estimate, fit.se, fit.inference_status)\n(0.2742893572880391, 0.011573664673835776, :available)\n\nThe default keeps capped point estimates but marks their ordinary EIF intervals unavailable. The uncapped route removes the package-level ratio cap; learner-specific probability guards can still apply. Clamped policies and attenuated targeting do not acquire ordinary EIF coverage merely by returning finite numbers.","title":"1. Identify, then estimate a δ-grid (LMTP)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#2.-Typed-plan-(execute_estimand)","page":"Getting Started","text":"When you already hold an IdentificationResult, plan_mtp / execute_estimand keep the certificate on the estimation path:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, Graphs, StableRNGs\n\ng = DiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2); add_edge!(g, 1, 3); add_edge!(g, 2, 3)\nid = identify(g, TotalEffectQuery(:A, :Y); node_names = Dict(1 => :W, 2 => :A, 3 => :Y))\n\ndf, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(180; rng = StableRNG(5))\nestimand = estimand_from_query(TotalEffectQuery(:A, :Y), [:W])\nplan = plan_mtp(estimand, df; id_result = id, deltas = [0.5], folds = 2)\ngrid = execute_estimand(\n    plan.estimand, df;\n    id_result = id,\n    deltas = [0.5],\n    folds = 2,\n    parallel = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(6),\n)\nonly(grid.est), plan.certificate.result.strategy\n(0.23445291892154893, :backdoor)\n\nGraph (identification).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    title = \"Good control W (identification)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (total effect).\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\nte = only(grid.est)\ng_te, _ = chain_graph([\"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_chain([\"A\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\"TE\\n$(round(te; digits = 2))\"]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"Typed plan TE (δ = 0.5)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"2. Typed plan (execute_estimand)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#3.-Interventional-mediation-(CausalMediation)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Implements interventional / stochastic mediation contrasts of Díaz & Hejazi (2020, JRSS-B), Hejazi et al. (2023, Biostatistics), and Liu et al. (2024, arXiv:2408.14620). Estimation lives in CausalMediation.jl; CausalTargeted supplies nuisances and optional façades once the weak dependency is loaded.\n\nusing CausalMediation, CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, Graphs, StableRNGs\n\ng = DiGraph(4)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2); add_edge!(g, 1, 3); add_edge!(g, 1, 4)\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3); add_edge!(g, 2, 4); add_edge!(g, 3, 4)\nnames = Dict(1 => :W, 2 => :A, 3 => :M, 4 => :Y)\n\nid = identify(\n    g, MediationQuery(:A, :Y, [:M]; effect_kind = :interventional);\n    node_names = names,\n)\nspec = spec_from_identification(id)\n\ndf, _ = simulate_continuous_mtp_mediation(200; rng = StableRNG(7))\nres = run_mediation(\n    spec, df;\n    deltas = [0.5],\n    folds = 2,\n    n_mc = 16,\n    estimator = :onestep,\n    learners = DEFAULT_SL_LEARNERS,\n    parallel = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(8),\n)\nd = decompose(res)\nd\n(te = 0.3003748139168566, nde = 0.10327575795693239, nie = 0.19709905595992425)\n\nGraph (identification). Full mediation DAG; adjust W only (M is a mediator, not a good control).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng_id = DiGraph(4)\nadd_edge!(g_id, 1, 2); add_edge!(g_id, 1, 3); add_edge!(g_id, 1, 4)\nadd_edge!(g_id, 2, 3); add_edge!(g_id, 2, 4); add_edge!(g_id, 3, 4)\nlayout_id = [\n    Point2f(0.0, 0.0), Point2f(1.2, 0.0), Point2f(2.4, 1.0), Point2f(3.6, 0.0),\n]\nfig, _, _ = dagplot(g_id;\n    layout = layout_id,\n    labels = [\"W\", \"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"],\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 4,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    edge_routing = Dict(\n        (1, 4) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.18, side = :right),\n        (1, 3) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.12),\n    ),\n    title = \"Good control W (mediation DAG)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (mediation paths). Direct and indirect routes with W omitted (already adjusted).\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_mediation([\"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"];\n    title = \"Mediation paths (A → M → Y, A → Y)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (NDE / NIE). Direct effect on A → Y, indirect via A → M (δ = 0.5).\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\ng_med, _ = mediation_graph([\"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\nelookup = Dict(\n    (1, 3) => \"NDE\\n$(round(d.nde; digits = 2))\",\n    (1, 2) => \"NIE\\n$(round(d.nie; digits = 2))\",\n)\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_mediation([\"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_med, [\n        get(elookup, (src(e), dst(e)), \"\") for e in edges(g_med)\n    ]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"Interventional mediation (δ = 0.5)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nMore mediation patterns (factor A, moc, nested MC): CausalMediation Getting started.","title":"3. Interventional mediation (CausalMediation)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#4.-Sequential-LMTP","page":"Getting Started","text":"Implements the longitudinal MTP identification and sequential regression / TMLE strategy of Díaz et al. (2023, JASA).\n\nTwo time points with a time-varying covariate (L1 between A1 and A2):\n\nusing CausalTargeted, CausalDynamics, StableRNGs, DataFrames\n\nrng = StableRNG(9)\nn = 80\nW = randn(rng, n)\nA1 = 0.5 .* W .+ randn(rng, n)\nL1 = 0.3 .* A1 .+ randn(rng, n)\nA2 = 0.4 .* L1 .+ 0.2 .* W .+ randn(rng, n)\nY = 0.5 .* A2 .+ 0.3 .* A1 .+ 0.2 .* W .+ randn(rng, n)\ndf = DataFrame(W = W, A1 = A1, L1 = L1, A2 = A2, Y = Y)\n\nres = run_sequential_lmtp(\n    df, [:A1, :A2], :Y;\n    baseline = [:W],\n    time_vary = [Symbol[], [:L1]],\n    delta = 0.5,\n    folds = 2,\n    learners = SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS,\n    rng = rng,\n)\nres.estimate, res.times\n(0.5051842179120771, 2)\n\nWith a temporal DAG in CausalDynamics, attach a certificate before estimating. The compact TemporalDAGSpec(variables, edges) form remains valid (default PointwiseSupport). Declare TemporalNodeSpec(...; temporal_support = FromOnsetSupport(onset)) when an attribute should not unroll per time (see CausalDynamics #29). Support fixes node count; optional occasion / enduring character is a separate ReferentSpec declaration and never selects how many nodes appear.\n\nusing CausalDynamics: TemporalDAGSpec, LaggedEdge, unroll_temporal_dag, TemporalEffectQuery\n\nspec = TemporalDAGSpec([:w, :a, :l, :y], [\n    LaggedEdge(:w, :a, 0),\n    LaggedEdge(:a, :l, 0),\n    LaggedEdge(:l, :a, 1),\n    LaggedEdge(:a, :y, 0),\n    LaggedEdge(:a, :y, 1),  # A₁ → Y (matches synthetic DGP)\n    LaggedEdge(:w, :y, 0),\n])\nu = unroll_temporal_dag(spec, 2)\ntq = TemporalEffectQuery(:a, :y, 1, 2)\ncert = sequential_identification_certificate(u, tq)\ncert.result.strategy, cert.temporal_lags\n(:temporal_backdoor, (treat_lag = 1, outcome_lag = 2))\n\nGraph (identification). Two-period unrolling; w[1] is the good control (baseline W).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\nusing CausalDynamics: temporal_node\n\nw1 = temporal_node(u, :w, 1)\na2 = temporal_node(u, :a, 2)\ny2 = temporal_node(u, :y, 2)\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_temporal(u;\n    dx = 2.4,\n    dy = 1.7,\n    figure_size = (720, 420),\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = a2,\n    outcome = y2,\n    adjustment = Set([w1]),\n    title = \"Good control w[1] (identification)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (total effect). Estimand path for terminal Y (confounder omitted); LMTP shifts both A₁ and A₂.\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\ng_te = DiGraph(4)\nadd_edge!(g_te, 1, 2)  # A₁ → L₁\nadd_edge!(g_te, 2, 3)  # L₁ → A₂\nadd_edge!(g_te, 1, 4)  # A₁ → Y\nadd_edge!(g_te, 3, 4)  # A₂ → Y\nlayout = [Point2f(0, 0), Point2f(1.1, 0), Point2f(2.2, 0), Point2f(2.2, -1.3)]\nte_lbl = \"TE\\n$(round(res.estimate; digits = 2))\"\nelookup = Dict((3, 4) => te_lbl)\nfig, _, _ = dagplot(g_te;\n    layout = layout,\n    labels = [\"A₁\", \"L₁\", \"A₂\", \"Y\"],\n    edge_routing = Dict((1, 4) => CurvedEdge(bow = 0.16, side = :left)),\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\n        get(elookup, (src(e), dst(e)), \"\") for e in edges(g_te)\n    ]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"Sequential LMTP (δ = 0.5 on A₁, A₂)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"4. Sequential LMTP"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#5.-Missing-outcome-(Observable-policies)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Structural missingness claims live in CausalDynamics; estimators consume Observable policies via handle_missing:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndf, _ = CausalTargeted.simulate_missing_outcome_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(10))\n\ngrid_drop = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W], deltas = [0.5],\n    folds = 2, parallel = false,\n    handle_missing = :drop,\n    rng = StableRNG(11),\n)\ngrid_ipcw = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W], deltas = [0.5],\n    folds = 2, parallel = false,\n    handle_missing = :ipcw,\n    rng = StableRNG(11),\n)\n\n(\n    drop = only(grid_drop.est),\n    ipcw = only(grid_ipcw.est),\n    strategy_drop = missingness_metadata(grid_drop).strategy,\n    strategy_ipcw = missingness_metadata(grid_ipcw).strategy,\n)\n(drop = 0.24594608354743097, ipcw = 0.24619375427867995, strategy_drop = :drop, strategy_ipcw = :ipcw)\n\nGraph (identification). Good-control triangle (same backdoor pattern as §1).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    title = \"MAR outcome — good control W\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (total effect). IPCW TE at δ = 0.5 on A → Y.\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\nte = only(grid_ipcw.est)\ng_te, _ = chain_graph([\"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_chain([\"A\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\"TE\\n$(round(te; digits = 2))\"]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"IPCW TE (δ = 0.5)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nUnder MAR Y, :drop and :ipcw need not coincide. See Missingness.","title":"5. Missing outcome (Observable policies)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#6.-Positivity-and-sensitivity-audit","page":"Getting Started","text":"Before interpreting a δ-curve, inspect positivity and optional omitted-confounder sensitivity:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndf, _ = simulate_linear_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(12))\nδ = collect(-1.0:0.25:1.0)\ngrid = run_lmtp_grid(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    baseline = [:W], deltas = δ,\n    folds = 2, parallel = false, positivity = true, rng = StableRNG(13),\n)\n\nte = grid.est[1]\nse = grid.se[1]\nrep = positivity_report(df, :A; deltas = grid.delta)\nsens = sensitivity_report(te, se; n = size(df, 1))\n\n(\n    n_weak = count(==(\"weak_support\"), string.(rep.support_status)),\n    n_sens_rows = size(sens, 1),\n    n_delta = size(grid, 1),\n)\n(n_weak = 0, n_sens_rows = 4, n_delta = 9)\n\nGraph (identification).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    title = \"Positivity audit — good control W\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (total effect). TE at the first δ in the audit grid.\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\nδ₀ = grid.delta[1]\ng_te, _ = chain_graph([\"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_chain([\"A\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\"TE\\n$(round(te; digits = 2))\"]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"TE at δ = $(round(δ₀; digits = 2))\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"6. Positivity and sensitivity audit"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#7.-G-computation-plug-in-(comparison)","page":"Getting Started","text":"When you want a plug-in contrast rather than the LMTP EIF path:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndf, truth = simulate_linear_mtp(200; rng = StableRNG(14))\nres = run_gcomp(\n    df, :A, :Y;\n    covariates = [:W],\n    folds = 2,\n    learners = (:glm, :mean),\n    rng = StableRNG(15),\n)\nres.estimate, res.se\n(0.7531354823120187, 0.06721921309332785)\n\nGraph (identification).\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    title = \"G-computation — good control W\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nGraph (total effect). Plug-in contrast on A → Y.\n\nusing Graphs: edges, src, dst\n\ng_te, _ = chain_graph([\"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_chain([\"A\", \"Y\"];\n    elabels = structural_edge_labels(g_te, [\"TE\\n$(round(res.estimate; digits = 2))\"]),\n    elabels_fontsize = 13,\n    elabels_distance = 14,\n    elabels_rotation = 0,\n    title = \"G-computation TE\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()","title":"7. G-computation plug-in (comparison)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#8.-Repeated-outcomes-under-a-static-treatment","page":"Getting Started","text":"Binary point treatment with several Y_t on the same units (wide layout). The runner returns a joint covariance so τ(t_3)-τ(t_2) is a Wald contrast, not a pair of independent tests.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, StableRNGs\n\ndf, truth = simulate_repeated_outcome_ate(\n    200; T = 3, β_a = [0.1, 0.7, 0.9], rng = StableRNG(16),\n)\nres = run_repeated_outcome_msm(\n    df, :A, [:Y1, :Y2, :Y3];\n    baseline = [:W],\n    folds = 2,\n    learners = (:glm, :mean),\n    rng = StableRNG(17),\n)\nc = msm_contrast(res, 2, 1)\n(res.estimates, c.estimate, c.se)\n([0.019522053106265126, 0.6635682690349576, 0.7788406207718414], 0.6440462159286925, 0.0930821719297147)\n\nGraph (identification). Same backdoor set for each occasion (W confounds A and every Y_t).\n\nusing CausalDynamics, Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = DiGraph(5)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)\nfor y in 3:5\n    add_edge!(g, 1, y)\n    add_edge!(g, 2, y)\nend\nids = identify_repeated_outcomes(\n    g, :A, [:Y1, :Y2, :Y3];\n    node_names = [:W, :A, :Y1, :Y2, :Y3],\n)\nall(r -> r.identifiable && Set(r.adjustment) == Set([:W]), ids)\ntrue\n\nfig, _, _ = dagplot_confounding([\"W\", \"A\", \"Y1\"];\n    color_by = :adjustment,\n    exposure = 2,\n    outcome = 3,\n    adjustment = Set([1]),\n    title = \"Good control W (each Yₜ)\",\n)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA parametric treatment×time MSM projects the same IF estimates onto a design (for example linear time in τ, or a mean model m(ta)):\n\npres = run_parametric_repeated_msm(\n    df, :A, [:Y1, :Y2, :Y3];\n    baseline = [:W],\n    design = :linear_time,\n    folds = 2,\n    learners = (:glm, :mean),\n    rng = StableRNG(18),\n)\n(pres.coef_names, pres.coefficients, pres.fitted_tau)\n([:intercept, :time], [0.06705128385324756, 0.38975201998807574], [0.06705128385324756, 0.4568033038413233, 0.846555323829399])","title":"8. Repeated outcomes under a static treatment"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#9.-Discrete-and-categorical-treatments","page":"Getting Started","text":"Implements classification density-ratio LMTP for finite-support treatments (Díaz et al., 2023; Williams & Díaz, 2023). Here a baseline confounder W (such as host body mass) affects both assignment to a three-level treatment (\"control\", \"low\", \"high\") and an outcome Y. Passing weight_truncation = false requests uncapped EIF inference for static arms.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, StableRNGs\n\nrng = StableRNG(91)\nn = 240\nW = randn(rng, n)\nu = rand(rng, n)\np_low = 1.0 ./ (1.0 .+ exp.(-0.5 .* W))\nA = [u[i] < 0.35 ? \"control\" : (u[i] < 0.35 + 0.35 * p_low[i] ? \"low\" : \"high\") for i in 1:n]\neffect_a = [a == \"control\" ? 0.0 : (a == \"low\" ? 0.6 : 1.3) for a in A]\nY = 1.0 .+ 0.5 .* W .+ effect_a .+ 0.4 .* randn(rng, n)\ndf_cat = DataFrame(W = W, A = A, Y = Y)\n\nres_cat = run_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df_cat, :A, :Y;\n    arm_hi = \"high\",\n    arm_ref = \"control\",\n    levels = [\"control\", \"low\", \"high\"],\n    baseline = [:W],\n    folds = 2,\n    learners_outcome = SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS,\n    weight_truncation = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(92),\n)\n\n(\n    estimate = round(res_cat.estimate; digits = 3),\n    se = round(res_cat.se; digits = 3),\n    status = res_cat.inference_status,\n)\n(estimate = 1.307, se = 0.065, status = :available)","title":"9. Discrete and categorical treatments"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#10.-Two-part-/-hurdle-biological-outcomes","page":"Getting Started","text":"Many ecological and parasitological outcomes are zero-inflated: an individual is either uninfected (infected = 0) or infected (infected = 1) with a positive intensity (log_eggs). run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast estimates the presence contrast on the full cohort and the conditional positive-only intensity contrast among infected rows using paired arm-score covariance.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, StableRNGs\n\nrng = StableRNG(101)\nn = 260\nW = randn(rng, n)\np_trt = 1.0 ./ (1.0 .+ exp.(-0.4 .* W))\nA = [rand(rng) < p_trt[i] ? \"treated\" : \"control\" for i in 1:n]\np_inf = [1.0 / (1.0 + exp(-(0.4 + 0.5 * W[i] - (A[i] == \"treated\" ? 0.9 : 0.0)))) for i in 1:n]\ninfected = [rand(rng) < p_inf[i] ? 1.0 : 0.0 for i in 1:n]\nlog_eggs = [\n    infected[i] == 1.0 ? (2.0 + 0.4 * W[i] - (A[i] == \"treated\" ? 0.6 : 0.0) + 0.3 * randn(rng)) : 0.0\n    for i in 1:n\n]\ndf_hurdle = DataFrame(W = W, A = A, infected = infected, log_eggs = log_eggs)\n\nres_h = run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df_hurdle, :A;\n    presence = :infected,\n    intensity = :log_eggs,\n    arm_hi = \"treated\",\n    arm_ref = \"control\",\n    levels = [\"control\", \"treated\"],\n    baseline = [:W],\n    folds = 2,\n    learners_outcome = SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS,\n    weight_truncation = false,\n    rng = StableRNG(102),\n)\n\n(\n    presence_diff = round(res_h.presence.estimate; digits = 3),\n    intensity_diff = round(res_h.intensity.estimate; digits = 3),\n)\n(presence_diff = -0.223, intensity_diff = -0.578)","title":"10. Two-part / hurdle biological outcomes"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#11.-Discrete-time-survival-(run_survival_lmtp)","page":"Getting Started","text":"Implements discrete-time event-free survival estimation under a longitudinal modified treatment policy (Díaz, Hoffman & Hejazi, 2024; competing risks deferred). Each wave records a continuous exposure (A1, A2) and an event-free indicator (S1, S2, where 1 means still alive).\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, StableRNGs\n\nrng = StableRNG(111)\nn = 220\nW = randn(rng, n)\nA1 = 0.4 .* W .+ randn(rng, n)\np_s1 = 1.0 ./ (1.0 .+ exp.(-(1.2 .+ 0.3 .* W .+ 0.5 .* A1)))\nS1 = Float64.(rand(rng, n) .< p_s1)\nA2 = 0.5 .* A1 .+ 0.3 .* W .+ randn(rng, n)\np_s2 = 1.0 ./ (1.0 .+ exp.(-(1.0 .+ 0.3 .* W .+ 0.5 .* A2)))\nS2 = S1 .* Float64.(rand(rng, n) .< p_s2)\ndf_surv = DataFrame(W = W, A1 = A1, S1 = S1, A2 = A2, S2 = S2)\n\nres_s0 = run_survival_lmtp(\n    df_surv, [:A1, :A2], [:S1, :S2];\n    baseline = [:W], delta = 0.0, folds = 2,\n    learners = SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS, rng = StableRNG(112),\n)\nres_s1 = run_survival_lmtp(\n    df_surv, [:A1, :A2], [:S1, :S2];\n    baseline = [:W], delta = 0.5, folds = 2,\n    learners = SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS, rng = StableRNG(112),\n)\n\n(\n    surv_natural = round(res_s0.estimate; digits = 3),\n    surv_shifted = round(res_s1.estimate; digits = 3),\n)\n(surv_natural = 0.579, surv_shifted = 0.633)","title":"11. Discrete-time survival (run_survival_lmtp)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#12.-Multiplicative-and-threshold-shift-policies","page":"Getting Started","text":"Beyond additive shifts, CausalTargeted provides multiplicative scaling (multiplicative_shift_policy) and threshold policies (threshold_shift_policy, \"raise exposure to at least c\") for realistic biological interventions (Díaz & van der Laan, 2012; Díaz et al., 2023).\n\nusing CausalTargeted\n\na = [0.4, 0.8, 1.2, 1.8, 2.5]\npol_mult = multiplicative_shift_policy()\npol_thr = threshold_shift_policy()\n\n(\n    original = a,\n    scaled_down_20pct = round.(apply_policy_values(a, -0.2, pol_mult); digits = 2),\n    threshold_bump_below_median = round.(apply_policy_values(a, 0.5, pol_thr); digits = 2),\n)\n(original = [0.4, 0.8, 1.2, 1.8, 2.5], scaled_down_20pct = [0.42, 0.64, 0.96, 1.44, 2.0], threshold_bump_below_median = [0.81, 1.21, 1.2, 1.8, 2.47])","title":"12. Multiplicative and threshold shift policies"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#13.-Hierarchical-Bayesian-g-computation-(clustered-units)","page":"Getting Started","text":"When biological units are grouped in cages, pens, litters, or clinics and sample sizes are small, fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp fits a one-level random-intercept Gaussian model via structured variational inference and evaluates marginal policy contrasts with bayesian_policy_contrast. See also Bayesian Gaussian estimation and Bayesian logistic estimation.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, LinearAlgebra, StableRNGs\n\nrng = StableRNG(131)\nn_clusters, per_cluster = 12, 8\nn = n_clusters * per_cluster\ncage = repeat(1:n_clusters, inner = per_cluster)\nb_cage = 0.6 .* randn(rng, n_clusters)\nW = randn(rng, n)\nA = [rand(rng) < (1.0 / (1.0 + exp(-0.3 * W[i]))) ? \"treated\" : \"control\" for i in 1:n]\nY = 1.5 .+ 0.7 .* (A .== \"treated\") .+ 0.4 .* W .+ b_cage[cage] .+ 0.5 .* randn(rng, n)\ndf_cl = DataFrame(cage = cage, W = W, A = A, Y = Y)\n\n# Design has intercept + treatment indicator + W (3 parameters)\nprior = ClusterGaussianPrior(;\n    mean = zeros(3),\n    covariance = Matrix(4.0 * I, 3, 3),\n    residual_shape = 2.0,\n    residual_scale = 1.0,\n    cluster_shape = 2.0,\n    cluster_scale = 1.0,\n)\n\nfit = fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp(df_cl, :Y, :A, [:W]; cluster = :cage, prior = prior)\npol_hi = discrete_static_policy(\"treated\"; levels = [\"control\", \"treated\"])\npol_lo = discrete_static_policy(\"control\"; levels = [\"control\", \"treated\"])\n\nc_hi = bayesian_policy_contrast(fit, df_cl, pol_hi; deltas = [1.0], n_draws = 500, rng = StableRNG(132))\nc_lo = bayesian_policy_contrast(fit, df_cl, pol_lo; deltas = [1.0], n_draws = 500, rng = StableRNG(132))\n(\n    status = fit.status,\n    shift_vs_obs_treated = round(c_hi.mean[1]; digits = 3),\n    shift_vs_obs_control = round(c_lo.mean[1]; digits = 3),\n)\n(status = :converged, shift_vs_obs_treated = 0.354, shift_vs_obs_control = -0.326)","title":"13. Hierarchical Bayesian g-computation (clustered units)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting-started/#Next-steps","page":"Getting Started","text":"Topic Page\nLiterature ↔ API map Methods and literature\nMissingness strata and certificates Missingness\nConservation / ecology defaults Small-n checklist\nReal and semi-synthetic cohorts Stress validation\nSymbol index API overview\nNarrative book CDCS","title":"Next steps"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#References","page":"References","text":"Bibliographic keys match the CDCS book file references.bib where possible, so chapters and package docs stay aligned. Prefer DOIs when citing externally.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Modified-treatment-policies-and-LMTP","page":"References","text":"Díaz Muñoz, I., & van der Laan, M. J. (2012). Population intervention causal effects based on stochastic interventions. Biometrics, 68(2), 541–549. doi:10.1111/j.1541-0420.2011.01685.x — key diaz2012stochastic\nDíaz, I., Williams, N., Hoffman, K. L., & Schenck, E. J. (2023). Nonparametric causal effects based on longitudinal modified treatment policies. Journal of the American Statistical Association, 118(542), 846–857. doi:10.1080/01621459.2021.1955691 — key diaz2023lmtp\nWilliams, N. T., & Díaz, I. (2023). lmtp: An R package for estimating the causal effects of modified treatment policies. Observational Studies. muse.jhu.edu/article/883479 — key williams2023lmtp\nDíaz, I., Hoffman, K. L., & Hejazi, N. S. (2024). Causal survival analysis under competing risks using longitudinal modified treatment policies. Lifetime Data Analysis, 30, 213–236. doi:10.1007/s10985-023-09606-7 — key diaz2024survival (SurvivalPolicy / run_survival_lmtp; competing risks deferred)\nRosenblum, M., & van der Laan, M. J. (2010). Targeted Maximum Likelihood Estimation of the Parameter of a Marginal Structural Model. The International Journal of Biostatistics, 6(2). doi:10.2202/1557-4679.1238 — key rosenblum2010msm (run_repeated_outcome_msm / run_parametric_repeated_msm)","title":"Modified treatment policies and LMTP"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Targeted-learning-and-Super-Learner","page":"References","text":"van der Laan, M. J., & Rubin, D. (2006). Targeted maximum likelihood learning. The International Journal of Biostatistics, 2(1). — key vanderlaan2006targeted\nvan der Laan, M. J., Polley, E. C., & Hubbard, A. E. (2007). Super learner. Statistical Applications in Genetics and Molecular Biology, 6(1). — key vanderlaan2007super\nPhillips, R. V., van der Laan, M. J., Lee, H., & Gruber, S. (2023). Practical considerations for specifying a super learner. International Journal of Epidemiology, 52(4), 1276–1285. doi:10.1093/ije/dyad023 — key phillips2023super (ensemble SL vs discrete SL / :cv_selector)\nvan der Laan, M. J., & Rose, S. (2011). Targeted Learning: Causal Inference for Observational and Experimental Data. Springer. — key vanderlaan2011targeted\nvan der Laan, M. J., & Rose, S. (2018). Targeted Learning in Data Science. Springer. — key vanderlaan2018targeted\nSchuler, M. S., & Rose, S. (2017). Targeted maximum likelihood estimation for causal inference in observational studies. American Journal of Epidemiology, 185(1), 65–73. — key schuler2017targeted\nZheng, W., & van der Laan, M. J. (2011). Cross-validated targeted minimum-loss-based estimation. In van der Laan & Rose (2011). — key zheng2011crossfitting\nChernozhukov, V., Chetverikov, D., Demirer, M., Duflo, E., Hansen, C., Newey, W., & Robins, J. (2018). Double/debiased machine learning for treatment and structural parameters. The Econometrics Journal, 21(1), C1–C68. doi:10.1111/ectj.12097 — key chernozhukov2018double","title":"Targeted learning and Super Learner"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Mediation-(natural,-interventional,-stochastic)","page":"References","text":"Robins, J. M., & Greenland, S. (1992). Identifiability and exchangeability for direct and indirect effects. Epidemiology, 3(2), 143–155. — key robins1992estimation\nPearl, J. (2001). Direct and indirect effects. In UAI. — key pearl2001direct\nVanderWeele, T. J. (2015). Explanation in Causal Inference: Methods for Mediation and Interaction. Oxford University Press. — key vanderweele2015explanation\nVansteelandt, S., & Daniel, R. M. (2017). Interventional effects for mediation analysis with multiple mediators. Epidemiology, 28(2), 258–265. doi:10.1097/EDE.0000000000000596 — key vansteelandt2017interventional\nDíaz, I., & Hejazi, N. S. (2020). Causal mediation analysis for stochastic interventions. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B, 82(3), 661–683. doi:10.1111/rssb.12362 — key diaz2020mediation\nHejazi, N. S., Rudolph, K. E., van der Laan, M. J., & Díaz, I. (2023). Nonparametric causal mediation analysis for stochastic interventional (in)direct effects. Biostatistics, 24(3), 686–707. doi:10.1093/biostatistics/kxac002 — key hejazi2023stochastic\nLiu, R., Williams, N. T., Rudolph, K. E., & Díaz, I. (2024). General targeted machine learning for modern causal mediation analysis. arXiv:2408.14620. doi:10.48550/arXiv.2408.14620 — key liu2024mediation\nLiu, R., Williams, N. T., Rudolph, K. E., & Díaz, I. (2025). crumble: A comprehensive framework for modern causal mediation analysis with intermediate confounding. arXiv:2604.09902. doi:10.48550/arXiv.2604.09902 — key liu2025crumble","title":"Mediation (natural, interventional, stochastic)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Positivity,-g-methods,-and-textbooks","page":"References","text":"Robins, J. (1986). A new approach to causal inference in mortality studies with a sustained exposure period. Mathematical Modelling, 7, 1393–1512. — key robins1986new\nRobins, J. M., Hernán, M. A., & Brumback, B. (2000). Marginal structural models and causal inference in epidemiology. Epidemiology, 11(5), 550–560. — key robins2000marginal\nPetersen, M. L., Porter, K. E., Gruber, S., Wang, Y., & van der Laan, M. J. (2012). Diagnosing and responding to violations in the positivity assumption. Statistical Methods in Medical Research, 21(1), 31–54. doi:10.1177/0962280210386207 — key petersen2012positivity\nHernán, M. A., & Robins, J. M. (2020). Causal Inference: What If. Chapman & Hall/CRC. — key hernan2020causal","title":"Positivity, g-methods, and textbooks"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Sensitivity-analysis","page":"References","text":"Cinelli, C., & Hazlett, C. (2020). Making sense of sensitivity: Extending omitted variable bias. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society: Series B, 82(1), 39–67. doi:10.1111/rssb.12348 — key cinelli2020sensitivity\nVanderWeele, T. J., & Ding, P. (2017). Sensitivity analysis in observational research: introducing the E-value. Annals of Internal Medicine, 167(4), 268–274. — key vanderweele2017sensitivity\nRosenbaum, P. R. (2002). Observational Studies (2nd ed.). Springer. — key rosenbaum2002observational\nImai, K., Keele, L., & Yamamoto, T. (2010). Identification, inference, and sensitivity analysis for causal mediation effects. Statistical Science, 25(1), 51–71. — key imai2010identification","title":"Sensitivity analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Structural-identification-(upstream:-CausalDynamics)","page":"References","text":"Pearl, J. (2009). Causality: Models, Reasoning, and Inference (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press. — key pearl2009causality\nShpitser, I., & Pearl, J. (2006). Identification of joint interventional distributions in recursive semi-Markovian causal models. In AAAI. — key shpitser2006identification\nSpirtes, P., Glymour, C., & Scheines, R. (2000). Causation, Prediction, and Search (2nd ed.). MIT Press. — key spirtes2000causation","title":"Structural identification (upstream: CausalDynamics)"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Benchmark-and-stress-cohort-data","page":"References","text":"Used by the application stress harness (see Stress validation); not bundled inside this package.\n\nLiu, W., McNeilly, T. N., Mitchell, M., Burgess, S. T. G., Nisbet, A. J., Matthews, J. B., & Babayan, S. A. (2022). Vaccine-induced time- and age-dependent mucosal immunity to gastrointestinal parasite infection. npj Vaccines, 7, 78. doi:10.1038/s41541-022-00501-0 — CircVax sheep phenotype (SimonAB/Liu2022)\nHill, J. L. (2011). Bayesian nonparametric modeling for causal inference. Journal of Computational and Graphical Statistics, 20(1), 217–240 — IHDP NPCI construction; CEVAE mirror AMLab-Amsterdam/CEVAE\nLouizos, C., Shalit, U., Mooij, J., Sontag, D., Zemel, R., & Welling, M. (2017). Causal effect inference with deep latent-variable models. NeurIPS — IHDP / Twins mirrors\nLaLonde, R. J. (1986). Evaluating the econometric evaluations of training programs with experimental data. American Economic Review, 76(4), 604–620 — NSW experiment (MatchIt::lalonde via Rdatasets)\nDehejia, R. H., & Wahba, S. (1999). Causal effects in nonexperimental studies… JASA — CPS observational counterpart (causaldata::cps_mixtape)\nImai, K., Keele, L., & Tingley, D. (2010). A general approach to causal mediation analysis. Psychological Methods — JOBS II (mediation::jobs)","title":"Benchmark and stress-cohort data"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#Related-software","page":"References","text":"R packages lmtp and crumble — methodological companions cited above\nCausalDynamics.jl — identification layer for this package\nCausalMediation.jl — mediation EIF / façades\nDAGMakie.jl — DAG figures\nTMLE.jl — point-treatment CM / ATE / AIE\nCDCS book — narrative companion; stress harness under scripts/stress_harness/","title":"Related software"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Parametric-and-Bayesian-g-comp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Cross-fitted g-computation, formula-based parametric GLM and MixedModels g-computation (including dedicated subject-integrated NB2 random-intercept models), and conjugate or variational Bayesian g-computation.\n\nfit_parametric_gcomp defaults to the GLM backend. Set backend=:mixed and provide id after loading MixedModels, FastGaussQuadrature, NLopt, and SpecialFunctions for supported mixed fits. See Methods and estimators for supported model, prediction, and inference combinations. Target data can omit the outcome; the repeated-outcome compatibility wrappers retain their existing static-treatment and observed-visit contract.","title":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Cross-fitted-and-parametric-GLM-g-computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"","title":"Cross-fitted & parametric GLM g-computation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Mixed-effects-and-NB2-random-intercept-g-computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ProfiledNB2MixedModel is exported as a compatibility alias for NB2RandomInterceptModel.","title":"Mixed-effects & NB2 random-intercept g-computation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Bayesian-Gaussian-g-computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Fits a conjugate Gaussian outcome model or a one-level random-intercept Gaussian outcome model via structured variational inference q(beta b) q(sigma_varepsilon^-2) q(sigma_b^-2) with exact Schur-complement block algebra and exact affine prior reparameterisation, integrating over the cluster random-effect distribution when evaluating marginal policy contrasts. Loading PSIS (using PSIS) activates CausalTargetedPSISExt for correction = :psis, which withholds intervals and covariance if operational gates (khat < 0.7, ess >= 400, mcse / sd <= 0.05) fail rather than falling back to raw VI. See Bayesian Gaussian & cluster for the non-clustered conjugate contract.","title":"Bayesian Gaussian g-computation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Bayesian-logistic-g-computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Non-clustered logistic regression (fit_bayesian_logistic) uses Jaakkola–Jordan variational inference with linear-response variance correction (LRVB); see Bayesian logistic (RxInfer). Clustered logistic regression (fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic) fits a one-level random-intercept Bernoulli–logit outcome model via Jaakkola–Jordan weighted Schur-complement structured VI q(beta b) q(sigma_b^-2) (zero dense N times N or K times K allocations). Marginal policy contrasts integrate over b_k sim mathrmNormal(0 sigma_b^2) via 25-node Gauss–Hermite quadrature (distinct from setting b_k = 0). Optional correction = :psis uses a heavy-tailed Student-t proposal on (beta log sigma_b^2) with 21-node adaptive Laplace-centred 1D Gauss–Hermite marginalised cluster log-likelihoods and withholds intervals when operational gates fail.","title":"Bayesian logistic g-computation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#Hierarchical-Bayesian-count-and-hurdle-g-computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Fits one-level random-intercept Poisson, negative binomial (NB2), and two-part hurdle models via weighted Schur-complement structured Laplace–Gaussian VI (zero dense N times N or K times K allocations). Marginal policy contrasts integrate over b_k sim mathrmNormal(0 sigma_b^2) via exact log-normal expectation (exp(η + 0.5 σ_b²)) for standard counts and 25-node Gauss–Hermite quadrature for presence and zero-truncated intensity.","title":"Hierarchical Bayesian count & hurdle g-computation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.run_gcomp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"run_gcomp(df, treatment, outcome; covariates, delta, folds, learners, rng, n_boot)\n    -> NamedTuple\n\nCross-fitted plug-in g-computation for a binary or continuous treatment.\n\nFor binary treatment: estimates ATE = E[Q(1, W) - Q(0, W)]. For continuous treatment with delta: estimates E[Q(A + δ, W) - Q(A, W)] with A + δ clamped to the 1%–99% range of observed A.\n\nestimator=:plugin (default), n_boot > 0 (default 200): percentile CI from a refitting bootstrap that redraws rows and recomputes the cross-fitted plug-in. For :ipcw, :impute, and :ipcw_impute, each draw resamples the original incomplete rows and reruns missing-data preprocessing, including imputation and response-model fitting; :drop resamples the complete-case analysis rows. n_boot = 0 returns NaN inferential SE/limits and uncertainty=:unavailable; prediction_se describes fitted-contrast variation only, excluding outcome-model estimation uncertainty.\n\nestimator=:onestep estimates a binary 0/1 ATE using cross-fitted AIPW scores and their normal-Wald interval, with no bootstrap by default. Optional propensity is a known scalar or row-aligned vector of treatment probabilities; otherwise a cross-fitted logistic Super Learner is fitted. This route requires complete data and independent rows; continuous clamped shifts and missing-data corrections need different scores. Propensity truncation is reported and can introduce bias. Cross-fitting does not remove nuisance-rate or overlap assumptions.\n\nReturns (estimate, se, ci_lower, ci_upper, n).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_gcomp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ParametricGComputationFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ParametricGComputationFit\n\nA fitted parametric reference / complete-case GLM for empirical-distribution standardisation, separate from cross-fitted run_gcomp and LMTP estimators. covariance is coefficient covariance (HC3 by default). For NB2 fits, theta records the shape and family stores the canonical symbol :negbin. NB2 HC3 covariance and delta-method intervals treat theta as fixed at its supplied or estimated value; they do not propagate uncertainty in its estimation. The wrapper also retains the unapplied formula needed to refit a non-parametric bootstrap, which re-estimates theta when estimated_theta is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ParametricGComputationFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_parametric_gcomp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_parametric_gcomp(formula, data; backend=:glm, family=:gaussian,\n                     link=nothing, theta=nothing, covariance=nothing, kwargs...)\n\nFit a parametric outcome model for empirical-distribution g-computation, separate from cross-fitted run_gcomp and LMTP estimators. The default GLM backend supports Gaussian/identity, binomial/logit, Gamma/log, and negative-binomial/log (:negbin; aliases :negativebinomial, :negative_binomial, and :nb are normalised to :negbin). For GLM negative binomial, omit theta to estimate the NB2 shape continuously with GLM.negbin, or provide it for a fixed-shape fit. GLM coefficient covariance defaults to :hc3.\n\nNB2 HC3 covariance and delta-method SEs/intervals treat theta as fixed at its supplied or estimated value; uncertainty in estimated theta is not propagated. Refit bootstraps re-estimate theta only when it was estimated in the original fit.\n\nAll outcome and predictor columns referenced by the formula must be present and contain no missing values. Handle missingness explicitly before fitting; this path never silently drops rows. Missing values in unrelated columns are allowed. Set backend=:mixed and supply id to use a supported Gaussian LMM or NB2 random-intercept model when the optional extension is loaded. A supplied NB2 theta selects the fixed-shape fit; omitting it selects the dedicated estimated-shape fitter. Mixed-model coefficient covariance defaults to :model. For nonlinear mixed predictions, pass random_effects=:zero or :marginal to the subsequent g-computation call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_parametric_gcomp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.run_parametric_gcomp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"run_parametric_gcomp(formula, data; treatment, reference, comparison,\n                     backend=:glm, random_effects=nothing, ...)\n\nFit a supported parametric outcome model and return an empirical standardised contrast. The GLM backend retains HC3 coefficient covariance by default; the mixed backend uses its own covariance method. Supply id for mixed fitting, and specify random_effects for nonlinear mixed predictions. See fit_parametric_gcomp for the complete-case policy and fitted-parameter uncertainty limits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.run_parametric_gcomp"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.gcomp_mean","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"gcomp_mean(fit, data; set=(;), by=nothing, subset=nothing,\n           random_effects=nothing)\n\nStandardise response-scale predictions over the empirical rows in data, or over the target rows selected by by/subset. Target rows are restricted before the intervention is applied. The target can differ from the training table in both rows and size and need not contain the outcome. Formula predictors in the selected target rows must be complete-case before intervention. Missing values are rejected, not dropped. For NB2 fits, HC3/delta inference treats the supplied or estimated theta as fixed. For mixed fits, specify random_effects=:zero or :marginal as supported by the backend. The mixed backend provides its own covariance and uncertainty metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\ngcomp_mean(model::StatsModels.TableRegressionModel, training_data, target_data; kwargs...)\n\nStandardise an existing formula-fitted GLM over target_data. Supply the exact complete-case table used to fit model as training_data; it is used to validate the fit for HC3 coefficient inference, not as the standardisation population. target_data may have a different number of rows and need not contain the outcome. Target predictors must be complete-case as described in gcomp_mean(fit, data). NB2 HC3/delta inference treats theta as fixed, including when GLM estimated it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.gcomp_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.gcomp_contrast","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"gcomp_contrast(fit, data; treatment, reference, comparison,\n               by=nothing, subset=nothing, scale=:difference,\n               random_effects=nothing)\n\nCompute a marginal or subgroup empirical standardised difference, response-mean ratio, or log response-mean ratio. Ratio inference is performed on the log scale. Target rows follow the complete-case predictor policy of gcomp_mean; no outcome column is required. NB2 HC3/delta inference treats supplied or estimated theta as fixed. For mixed fits, set random_effects explicitly when using a nonlinear link.\n\n\n\n\n\ngcomp_contrast(model::StatsModels.TableRegressionModel, training_data, target_data; kwargs...)\n\nCompute a contrast from an existing formula-fitted GLM. The explicit training/target split and complete-case requirements are the same as for gcomp_mean(model, training_data, target_data). NB2 HC3/delta inference treats theta as fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.gcomp_contrast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.gcomp_interaction","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"gcomp_interaction(fit, data; treatment, reference, comparison, modifier,\n                  modifier_reference, modifier_comparison, scale=:difference,\n                  random_effects=nothing)\n\nCompute a difference-of-differences or a response-mean ratio-of-ratios. The return value includes both component subgroup effects and all four standardised means. Delta inference uses the single fitted coefficient covariance jointly. For NB2 this treats supplied or estimated theta as fixed. Selected target rows must have complete-case formula predictors; the outcome is needed only for refitting. Set n_boot > 0 for an additional stratified refit-bootstrap summary.\n\n\n\n\n\ngcomp_interaction(model::StatsModels.TableRegressionModel, training_data, target_data; kwargs...)\n\nCompute an interaction from an existing formula-fitted GLM with the same explicit training/target split and complete-case requirements as gcomp_mean(model, training_data, target_data). NB2 HC3/delta inference treats theta as fixed. For refit bootstraps, create a fit with fit_parametric_gcomp instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.gcomp_interaction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.bootstrap_gcomp_interaction","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"bootstrap_gcomp_interaction(fit, data; n_boot=500, strata=nothing, ...)\n\nRefit a non-parametric bootstrap for a formal interaction. If strata is provided, sampling occurs independently within each observed stratum and keeps the original stratum sizes. Failed fits are counted and summarised. The resampled table must be complete-case in the original formula's outcome and predictors. NB2 theta is re-estimated in each replicate if originally estimated; a supplied fixed theta stays fixed. This differs from HC3/delta intervals, which do not propagate theta-estimation uncertainty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.bootstrap_gcomp_interaction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.MixedModelGComputationFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"MixedModelGComputationFit\n\nAn adapter for a supported fitted mixed outcome model in the common parametric g-computation framework. id names its grouping factor; it need not be the unit at which treatment was assigned. Inference from this adapter conditions on the fitted variance and dispersion parameters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MixedModelGComputationFit"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.MixedGComputationResult","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"MixedGComputationResult\n\nTime-indexed standardised means and their contrast from mixed_g_computation.\n\nmean_reference corresponds to values[1], mean_comparison to values[2], and effect is mean_comparison - mean_reference. adjustment records the graph-derived backdoor set when a graph was supplied. vcov is the joint fixed-effect covariance matrix of the effect trajectory, in times order, and se is sqrt.(diag(vcov)). random_effects records whether nonlinear-link predictions set random effects to zero (:zero) or integrate the response mean over a supported fitted Gaussian random-effects distribution (:marginal). This uncertainty is fixed-effect-only: it does not include residual, dispersion, or variance-component uncertainty. uncertainty is :delta_fixed or :parametric_bootstrap; the latter stores replicate counts, seed, failure messages, and successful trajectory draws in uncertainty_diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.MixedGComputationResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.StratifiedMixedGComputationResult","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"StratifiedMixedGComputationResult\n\nA collection of MixedGComputationResults, one for each observed stratum requested with strata. strata records the stratification-column names, levels records stable stratum identifiers, and results has the same order. Index a single-variable result by its observed level, for example result[\"group_a\"]; index a multi-variable result by a named tuple, for example result[(Cohort=\"group_a\", Site=\"north\")].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.StratifiedMixedGComputationResult"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.mixed_g_computation","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"mixed_g_computation(model, data; treatment, outcome, time, id,\n                    values=(0, 1), strata=nothing, random_effects=:zero)\nmixed_g_computation(graph, model, data; treatment, outcome, time, id,\n                    values=(0, 1), strata=nothing, random_effects=:zero,\n                    node_names=nothing)\n\nEstimate a time-specific standardised contrast for a static treatment and a repeated outcome using a fitted MixedModels.LinearMixedModel or a supported MixedModels.GeneralizedLinearMixedModel.\n\nThe first method standardises population-level predictions under the two static interventions in values. With strata=nothing it returns one MixedGComputationResult. With a symbol or tuple of symbols, such as strata=:Age, it returns a StratifiedMixedGComputationResult whose trajectories are standardised over the remaining covariates within each stratum. The graph method first calls CausalDynamics identify for a TotalEffectQuery, requires backdoor identification, validates that the identified adjustment variables occur in the fitted formula, and records them in the returned MixedGComputationResult.\n\nThe estimand at each observed time t is\n\nE_WE(Y_t mid A=textvalues2 W) -\nE_WE(Y_t mid A=textvalues1 W)\n\nThis compatibility front end uses the same counterfactual standardisation and contrast machinery as gcomp_mean and gcomp_contrast. It retains the repeated-outcome contract: treatment is static within id, times and strata follow their sorted observed order, and each visit is standardised over the rows actually observed at that visit. With unbalanced follow-up, visit estimates therefore refer to different empirical visit populations. The generic mixed interface does not require static treatment within its grouping factor.\n\nThis integration is provided by the optional MixedModels extension (CausalTargetedMixedModelsExt). It is a parametric reference path beside MSM / LMTP, not a silent substitute for run_repeated_outcome_msm. It supports Gaussian LinearMixedModels, fixed-shape NB2 negative-binomial GeneralizedLinearMixedModel compatibility fits, and dedicated NB2RandomInterceptModels that estimate shape. The model's grouping factor must be id. For random_effects=:zero, MixedModels prediction uses fresh ID levels with new_re_levels=:population, so fitted subject-specific random effects are set to zero. Gaussian identity-link predictions have the same population mean under :zero and :marginal, including supported random-coefficient models. For an NB2 random-intercept model, random_effects=:marginal applies the analytic Gaussian log-normal correction. The reported delta-method covariance conditions on all fitted or supplied variance components. Causal interpretation requires the usual identification assumptions and correct specification of the parametric outcome model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.mixed_g_computation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.NB2RandomInterceptModel","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"NB2RandomInterceptModel\n\nA fitted NB2/log mixed model with one Gaussian random intercept and an estimated NB2 shape parameter. Construct it with fit_profiled_nb2.\n\nDespite the compatibility alias ProfiledNB2MixedModel, the authoritative implementation uses a dedicated subject-integrated likelihood; it does not profile MixedModels.jl fixed-shape GLMM fits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.NB2RandomInterceptModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_profiled_nb2","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_profiled_nb2(formula, data; id, kwargs...)\n\nFit the optional dedicated subject-integrated NB2/log/random-intercept backend, jointly estimating fixed effects, random-intercept variance, and NB2 shape with adaptive Gauss–Hermite quadrature. Available after loading the MixedModels extension stack:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, MixedModels, FastGaussQuadrature, NLopt, SpecialFunctions\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_profiled_nb2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.quadrature_diagnostics","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Compare fitted parameters across requested adaptive-quadrature orders.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.quadrature_diagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.validate_fixed_theta_nb2_likelihood","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Audit MixedModels fixed-shape NB2 log likelihoods against independent quadrature.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.validate_fixed_theta_nb2_likelihood"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.theta","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Return the jointly estimated NB2 shape parameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.theta"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.converged","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Return whether all convergence and interior-optimum checks passed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.converged"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fitdiagnostics","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Return optimiser, gradient, profile, and quadrature diagnostics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fitdiagnostics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.random_intercept_variance","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Return the fitted Gaussian random-intercept variance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.random_intercept_variance"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ConjugateGaussianPrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ConjugateGaussianPrior(; mean, covariance, shape, scale)\n\nExplicit normal–inverse-gamma prior: β | σ² ~ Normal(mean, σ² * covariance) and σ² ~ InverseGamma(shape, scale). covariance is the conditional scale matrix, not the marginal covariance of β. Require positive definite covariance, shape > 1 and scale > 0, so marginal coefficient variances exist. No outcome-dependent rescaling is performed. With known observation variance, condition the normal prior on that variance; inverse-gamma parameters are unused.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ConjugateGaussianPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianGCompFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Exact coefficient posterior, fitted design, and assumptions for Gaussian g-computation.\n\nposterior_scale is the Student-t scale matrix; posterior_covariance is its covariance. They coincide for known observation variance (posterior_df = Inf). Vn, shape, and scale retain the conjugate parameters. design_metadata records column order; variance_diagnostics reports prior and posterior marginal variances and their ratios. These ratios describe marginal contraction or expansion relative to the prior; sensitivity requires refitting under other priors. They do not establish causal identification or adequate model fit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianGCompFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_gcomp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_gcomp(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; prior,\n    basis=nothing, identification=nothing, handle_missing=:error,\n    observation_variance=nothing)\n\nFit a Gaussian outcome regression with an explicit conjugate prior. The default design is [intercept | treatment | covariates], with categorical dummy columns and their order retained by a single fitted CovariateSchema. Alternatively, basis(predictor_data, treatment_values) returns a matrix in a prespecified, unchanging column order, including any intercept and interactions. Only the treatment and declared covariate columns are passed to this function; the caller is responsible for keeping its transformations and column meanings unchanged.\n\nTreatment and covariates must be fully observed and finite. Missing outcomes require handle_missing=:mar, explicitly recording user-assumed ignorable MAR; only observed outcomes enter the likelihood. Missing outcomes do not add information to the posterior. Without an identification certificate, causal identification is recorded as :user_assumed; a proper posterior is not a graph identification argument. A supplied certificate must certify the actual adjustment set, rather than a subset of additional regressors. graph_identification_status records that graph claim independently of the basis. outcome_model_status is always :user_assumed, including for the default linear basis: a graph certificate does not establish regression adequacy. The conservative summary identification_status remains :user_assumed for a user-supplied basis; the checked certificate and its graph status are retained.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_gcomp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianGaussianLearner","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"A Gaussian Super Learner candidate with an explicitly specified prior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianGaussianLearner"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.predictive_missing_outcomes","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"predictive_missing_outcomes(fit, data; rng)\n\nDraw missing outcomes conditional on one shared joint posterior draw of β and σ². Return (rows, values, beta, sigma2), leaving data and fit unchanged. Outcome-predictive noise belongs here, separately from policy mean uncertainty. This is a posterior predictive draw, not another likelihood contribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.predictive_missing_outcomes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ClusterGaussianPrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ClusterGaussianPrior(; mean, covariance, residual_shape, residual_scale, cluster_shape, cluster_scale)\nClusterGaussianPrior(coefficient_prior::ConjugateGaussianPrior; cluster_shape, cluster_scale)\n\nExplicit hierarchical Gaussian prior for a one-level random-intercept model: β | σ_ε² ~ Normal(mean, σ_ε² * covariance), σ_ε² ~ InverseGamma(residual_shape, residual_scale), and σ_b² ~ InverseGamma(cluster_shape, cluster_scale).\n\nRequires symmetric positive-definite covariance, residual_shape > 1, residual_scale > 0, cluster_shape > 1, and cluster_scale > 0 so marginal prior variances exist. Prior locations and scales must be supplied explicitly; no outcome-dependent rescaling is performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ClusterGaussianPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterGCompFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Typed hierarchical Gaussian fit from structured variational inference q(β, b) q(σ_ε⁻²) q(σ_b⁻²).\n\nRetains the frozen design, affine reparameterisation, cached cluster sufficient statistics, prior, variational posterior state, marginal log-posterior evaluator, and convergence diagnostics. Intervals and covariance are withheld by downstream contrast calls whenever status !== :converged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterGCompFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; cluster, prior,\n    basis=nothing, identification=nothing, handle_missing=:error,\n    maxiter=1000, tol=1e-8, standardize=true)\n\nFit a one-level random-intercept hierarchical Gaussian outcome model using structured variational inference q(β, b) q(σ_ε⁻²) q(σ_b⁻²) with exact Schur-complement elimination and exact affine prior reparameterisation.\n\nReturns a BayesianClusterGCompFit. Reaching maxiter without convergence records status = :maxiter_reached and withholds downstream contrast intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_gcomp"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.GaussianCoefficientPrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"GaussianCoefficientPrior(; mean, covariance)\n\nAn explicit multivariate normal prior on logistic regression coefficients, independent of the outcome. The finite, symmetric covariance must be positive definite; coefficients follow the frozen design column order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.GaussianCoefficientPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianLogisticFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Variational logistic coefficient moments, a frozen design, and explicit assumptions.\n\nvariational_covariance is the raw approximation. corrected_covariance stores linear-response moments when available. posterior_covariance contains the requested usable moments, or NaNs when convergence or correction fails. These moments do not define a corrected posterior density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianLogisticFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_logistic","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_logistic(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; prior,\n    basis=nothing, identification=nothing, handle_missing=:error,\n    maxiter=1000, tol=1e-8, covariance=:linear_response)\n\nFit Bernoulli logistic regression with the optional RxInfer extension; load it with using RxInfer, GraphPPL. A proper coefficient prior must be specified. The default design and categorical levels are fitted once. A custom basis(predictor_data, treatment_values) receives only declared predictors and must preserve its column meanings. Missing predictors are rejected. Missing outcomes require explicit handle_missing=:mar; only observed outcomes enter the likelihood, under user-assumed ignorable MAR. With no observed outcomes, the coefficient posterior remains the specified prior.\n\nThe default maxiter=1000 allows more Jaakkola updates before withholding uncertainty; increase the cap if status === :maxiter. Standardising predictors while keeping the same coefficient prior changes the model. For coefficient order [intercept, treatment, W] and Wz = (W - c) / s, with s > 0, preserve the prior with T = [1 0 c; 0 1 0; 0 0 s], μz = T * μ and Vz = T * V * T'. Centring can introduce intercept-slope prior dependence. Apply the same declared transformation to fitting and target designs. This reparameterisation preserves logit second moments and does not guarantee fewer iterations.\n\nGraph identification and outcome-model assumptions are recorded separately. A certificate does not certify a custom basis or regression adequacy. Neither variational moments nor linear-response corrections are exact posterior inference. Failed convergence or invalid requested correction withholds usable coefficient covariance; raw covariance is never silently substituted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_logistic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ClusterLogisticPrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ClusterLogisticPrior(; mean, covariance, cluster_shape, cluster_scale)\nClusterLogisticPrior(coefficient_prior::GaussianCoefficientPrior; cluster_shape, cluster_scale)\n\nExplicit hierarchical logistic prior: β ~ Normal(mean, covariance) and σ_b² ~ InverseGamma(cluster_shape, cluster_scale) for cluster random intercepts b_k | σ_b² ~ Normal(0, σ_b²).\n\nRequires symmetric positive-definite covariance, cluster_shape > 1, and cluster_scale > 0. No outcome-dependent rescaling is performed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ClusterLogisticPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterLogisticFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Typed hierarchical logistic fit from Jaakkola-Jordan weighted Schur-complement structured variational inference q(β, b) q(σ_b⁻²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterLogisticFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; cluster, prior,\n    basis=nothing, identification=nothing, handle_missing=:error,\n    maxiter=1000, tol=1e-8, standardize=true)\n\nFit a one-level random-intercept hierarchical Bayesian logistic outcome model using Jaakkola-Jordan weighted Schur-complement structured VI q(β, b) q(σ_b⁻²) and exact affine prior reparameterisation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ClusterCountPrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ClusterCountPrior(; mean, covariance, cluster_shape, cluster_scale,\n    log_dispersion_mean=0.0, log_dispersion_sd=1.5)\nClusterCountPrior(coefficient_prior::GaussianCoefficientPrior; cluster_shape,\n    cluster_scale, log_dispersion_mean=0.0, log_dispersion_sd=1.5)\n\nExplicit hierarchical count prior: β ~ Normal(mean, covariance), σ_b² ~ InverseGamma(cluster_shape, cluster_scale), and (when family = :nb2) log(θ) ~ Normal(log_dispersion_mean, log_dispersion_sd²).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ClusterCountPrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterCountFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Typed hierarchical count fit (:poisson or :nb2, optionally zero-truncated) from structured Laplace-Gaussian VI q(β, b) q(σ_b⁻²) [q(log θ)].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterCountFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_count","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_cluster_count(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; cluster, prior,\n    family=:poisson, zero_truncated=false, basis=nothing, identification=nothing,\n    handle_missing=:error, maxiter=1000, tol=1e-8, standardize=true)\n\nFit a one-level random-intercept hierarchical Bayesian count model (family = :poisson or :nb2, optionally zero_truncated = true) via weighted Schur-complement structured Laplace-Gaussian VI.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_count"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.ClusterHurdlePrior","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"ClusterHurdlePrior(; presence::ClusterLogisticPrior, intensity::ClusterCountPrior)\n\nExplicit two-part hierarchical hurdle prior combining a presence-part ClusterLogisticPrior and a positive-intensity ClusterCountPrior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.ClusterHurdlePrior"},{"category":"type","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterHurdleFit","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"Typed two-part hierarchical hurdle fit combining a Bernoulli-logit presence fit (BayesianClusterLogisticFit) and a zero-truncated count intensity fit (BayesianClusterCountFit).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.BayesianClusterHurdleFit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_hurdle","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"fit_bayesian_cluster_hurdle(data, outcome, treatment, covariates; cluster,\n    prior::ClusterHurdlePrior, family=:nb2, basis=nothing,\n    identification=nothing, handle_missing=:error, maxiter=1000,\n    tol=1e-8, standardize=true)\n\nFit a two-part hierarchical Bayesian hurdle model for zero-inflated counts: Part 1 fits binary presence I(Y > 0) via fit_bayesian_cluster_logistic, and Part 2 fits positive intensity Y | Y > 0 via fit_bayesian_cluster_count with zero_truncated = true (family = :poisson or :nb2).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.fit_bayesian_cluster_hurdle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/gcomp_bayesian/#CausalTargeted.bayesian_policy_contrast","page":"Parametric & Bayesian g-comp","text":"bayesian_policy_contrast(fit, target, policy; deltas, level=0.95)\n\nExact joint posterior for the fixed-cohort :policy_vs_observed contrast. Each row of contrast_matrix averages design(policy(A)) - design(A) over the supplied target rows. The returned mean, covariance and scale retain cross-policy dependence. Intervals are pointwise equal-tailed credible intervals for a conditional mean contrast, without future-outcome noise. Supported policies are fixed-bound SmoothBoundedLogitShiftPolicy and DiscreteTreatmentPolicy; a discrete policy is independent of the grid labels in deltas. Its callback must be deterministic and is evaluated once for the target cohort. Both factual and assigned discrete levels must occur in the fitting data. Sample-clamped ShiftPolicy is rejected. No treatment density is estimated; causal interpretation requires identification, positivity and the specified outcome model, which a credible interval does not establish.\n\n\n\n\n\nbayesian_policy_contrast(fit::BayesianLogisticFit, target, policy;\n    deltas, level=0.95, n_draws=2000, mc_level=0.95, rng=Random.Xoshiro(42))\n\nApproximate fixed-cohort posterior risk differences against observed treatment. One set of joint normal coefficient draws, using the selected fitted moments, is shared over the entire policy grid. Return Monte Carlo means, covariance, pointwise quantiles and mean MCSE. This normal moment approximation is an additional approximation: linear-response moments are not a corrected posterior density. No outcome noise or covariate-population sampling is added.\n\nendpoint_mc_bounds gives pointwise Monte Carlo confidence bounds for each ideal Gaussian-law interval endpoint at confidence mc_level. Binomial order statistics of the existing independent draws provide these bounds; small draw counts can give the full risk-difference support [-1,1]. These bounds quantify endpoint simulation variability, not posterior approximation error, and are not simultaneous across endpoints or policies. mcse concerns means only.\n\nUse fixed-bound smooth or discrete policies. A deterministic discrete callback is evaluated once per target row before drawing, independently of grid labels. Factual and assigned discrete levels must occur in the fitting data. When fitted uncertainty is unavailable, mean is the plug-in contrast at the coefficient mean, and covariance, intervals and MCSE are NaN.\n\n\n\n\n\nbayesian_policy_contrast(fit::BayesianClusterLogisticFit, target, policy;\n    deltas, n_draws=2000, rng=Random.default_rng(), level=0.95,\n    mc_level=0.95, weighting=:individual, cluster=fit.cluster,\n    correction=:none, marginalize_random_effects=true)\n\nMarginal :policy_vs_observed risk-difference contrasts from a fitted hierarchical Bayesian logistic model.\n\nWhen marginalize_random_effects=true (default), integrates over b_k ~ Normal(0, σ_b²) via 25-node Gauss-Hermite quadrature for every joint draw (β^(s), σ_b²^(s)) (distinct from setting b_k = 0 under the nonlinear logit link). Supports weighting = :individual or :cluster, and correction = :none or :psis (requiring using PSIS).\n\n\n\n\n\nbayesian_policy_contrast(fit::BayesianClusterCountFit, target, policy;\n    deltas, n_draws=2000, rng=Random.default_rng(), level=0.95,\n    mc_level=0.95, weighting=:individual, cluster=fit.cluster,\n    correction=:none, marginalize_random_effects=true)\n\nMarginal :policy_vs_observed expected-count contrasts from a fitted hierarchical Bayesian count model (:poisson or :nb2, standard or zero-truncated). When marginalize_random_effects=true (default), integrates over b_k ~ Normal(0, σ_b²) (exact exp(η + 0.5 σ_b²) for standard counts; 25-node GH for zero-truncated counts).\n\n\n\n\n\nbayesian_policy_contrast(fit::BayesianClusterHurdleFit, target, policy;\n    deltas, n_draws=2000, rng=Random.default_rng(), level=0.95,\n    mc_level=0.95, weighting=:individual, cluster=fit.cluster,\n    correction=:none, marginalize_random_effects=true)\n\nJoint marginal :policy_vs_observed contrasts from a fitted two-part hierarchical hurdle model, returning overall (combined expected count contrast), presence (binary prevalence contrast), and intensity (positive-part intensity contrast) evaluated on shared posterior draws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CausalTargeted.bayesian_policy_contrast"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Methods-and-literature","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"This page maps CausalTargeted APIs to the papers and books that define the estimands, identification conditions, and estimators implemented in Julia. Closest R companions include lmtp and crumble. Full bibliographic entries (with DOIs) are in References. Keys such as diaz2023lmtp match references.bib in the CDCS book. Engine naming (:lmtp / :mediation, not “crumble”) is summarised in NAMING.md.","title":"Methods and literature"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Boundary-with-confounded-offline-reinforcement-learning","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Recent causal offline-RL work studies continuous-action policy learning when the behaviour policy uses latent state that is only partially recorded in the offline dataset. That problem is adjacent to, but not the same as, LMTP estimation: CausalTargeted estimates declared treatment-policy functionals, whereas offline-RL algorithms learn value functions and policies under sequential feedback. The package therefore does not expose flow-matching Q-learning. A future bridge may consume CausalDynamics observation and policy declarations, but robust lower-bound objectives and expressive continuous-action learners belong in an optional RL extension or separate package.","title":"Boundary with confounded offline reinforcement learning"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Modified-treatment-policies-and-LMTP","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Gaussian Super Learner fits use normalised non-negative least squares on out-of-fold candidate predictions. If normalising those coefficients produces greater squared prediction loss than the best candidate, the package selects that candidate instead. This predictive-risk guard extends ordinary normalised NNLS; it does not establish nuisance consistency or confidence-interval coverage.\n\nScientific problem. Deterministic interventions that set a continuous exposure to a fixed value are often scientifically uninteresting and exacerbate positivity violations. Modified treatment policies (MTPs) shift or otherwise transform the natural value of treatment (e.g. raise exposure by one standard deviation, subject to clamps).\n\nTopic Primary sources CausalTargeted surface\nStochastic / population interventions Díaz & van der Laan (2012), Biometrics (doi) ShiftPolicy, additive / multiplicative / threshold policies\nLongitudinal MTPs (LMTP): ID, EIF, TMLE & sequential DR Díaz, Williams, Hoffman & Schenck (2023), JASA (doi) run_lmtp_grid, lmtp_tmle_contrast, LongitudinalPolicy\nPoint treatment, repeated outcomes (joint Σ) Rosenblum & van der Laan (2010), IJB (doi) run_repeated_outcome_msm, msm_contrast\nParametric treatment×time MSM Rosenblum & van der Laan (2010), IJB (doi) run_parametric_repeated_msm, ParametricRepeatedOutcomeMSM\nSoftware companion (R) Williams & Díaz (2023), Observational Studies (link) lmtp companion; Julia APIs differ\nDiscrete-time survival / event-time LMTP Díaz, Hoffman & Hejazi (2024), Lifetime Data Analysis (doi) SurvivalPolicy, run_survival_lmtp (competing risks deferred)\n\nPoint-treatment continuous MTP. run_lmtp_grid estimates a δ-indexed curve under a user-chosen ShiftPolicy, with cross-fitted outcome and treatment nuisances and optional TMLE fluctuation. Density-ratio options (gaussian, classification, hybrid) implement practical continuous-exposure clever covariates in the spirit of the LMTP literature.","title":"Modified treatment policies and LMTP"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Targeting-and-uncertainty-contract","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"For eligible untruncated LMTP fits, the TMLE path targets each policy arm with its own weighted intercept submodel. For Gaussian outcomes, the arm-specific shift solves sum_i H_di(Y_i-Q_obsi-epsilon_d)=0; binomial and count outcomes use the corresponding weighted logit-intercept or log-link scale score. The fitted intercept is applied to both the factual offset and the counterfactual prediction. The final arm score is D_d=Q_d^*+H_d(Y-Q_obsd^*) and the contrast influence curve is (D_1-D_0)-mathbbP_n(D_1-D_0). The :eif one-step path uses the initial regressions and the same full clever-covariate correction without targeting.\n\nFinite classifier or clever-covariate caps leave point estimation available, but ordinary EIF uncertainty is unavailable: the result carries inference_status=:truncated_weights and NaN standard errors, limits, and influence contributions. The uncertainty gate itself leaves an already-computed point result unchanged; changes to targeting or nuisance-cache correctness can still change point estimates across releases. weight_truncation=false opts into the uncapped route for supported finite-support discrete policies and unclamped, constant additive Gaussian shifts. Machine-epsilon guards protect the ratio arithmetic; guards inside a selected learner remain in force. Clamped continuous policies, nonconstant shifts, unsupported missingness or covariate-imputation paths, and failed positivity checks remain gated. A targeting weight below one reports :attenuated_targeting; attenuation does not restore the ordinary regular EIF contract.\n\nThe identifying DAG for the linear synthetic DGP (simulate_linear_mtp) is baseline confounding of a continuous exposure:\n\nusing CausalDynamics, Graphs, DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\ng = DiGraph(3)\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 2)  # W → A\nadd_edge!(g, 1, 3)  # W → Y\nadd_edge!(g, 2, 3)  # A → Y\nfig = plot_with_adjustment_set(g, 2, 3, [1]; node_labels = [\"W\", \"A\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nSequential / multi-time LMTP. SequentialPolicy / run_sequential_lmtp implement a practical recursive outcome regression with a TMLE-style correction at t = 1, following the sequential identification strategy of Díaz et al. (2023). Numeric A_t share an additive ShiftPolicy. Categorical A_t take per-time DiscreteTreatmentPolicy values in policies (dummy-coded Q; Díaz–Williams classification ratio at t = 1). Mixed continuous/discrete treatments are rejected. Pair with CausalDynamics TemporalEffectQuery + unroll_temporal_dag → identify, then sequential_spec_from_identification / plan_sequential (or sequential_identification_certificate) so estimation carries an explicit ID certificate. estimand_from_query on a TemporalEffectQuery still builds a numeric LongitudinalPolicy by default; pass nonempty policies and wide treatments (:A1, :A2, …) for a factor SequentialPolicy. Observational panels from CausalDynamics simulate_panel use the same wide layout (baseline / timed :a1,:a2 / terminal :y) that SequentialPolicy expects; execute_estimand merges the certificate before running sequential LMTP. Latent/filter outputs enter via CausalDynamics ObservationBridge / panel_from_latent_series before the same sequential path.\n\nDiscrete-time survival / event-time LMTP. SurvivalPolicy / run_survival_lmtp estimate event-free probability at a horizon under a common MTP shift on time-ordered treatments, with at-risk sequential regression and optional thin IPCW for censoring. Pair with CausalDynamics TemporalEffectQuery (outcome = event-free indicator at the horizon) and survival_identification_certificate. Competing risks remain out of scope. Synthetic gate: simulate_discrete_survival_mtp.\n\nRepeated outcomes under a static treatment. When treatment is fixed once and the same response is measured at several times (wide Y1…YT), run_repeated_outcome_msm estimates the unstructured profile τ(t)=EY_tmid do(A=1)-EY_tmid do(A=0) with a joint influence-function covariance. Shared cross-fit propensity and per-time outcome regressions yield widehatSigma so contrasts such as τ(t_3)-τ(t_2) use msm_contrast. Default estimator=:tmle fluctuates Q_t on the shared clever covariate Ag-(1-A)(1-g); :eif is the untargeted one-step. Long (id, time, Y) tables pivot with unstack_repeated_outcomes. Missingness is a complete-profile policy (time_indexed=true); Y_t are never imputed. This is the point-treatment / multi-outcome setting (Rosenblum & van der Laan 2010; R tmle::tmleMSM as a conceptual reference), not sequential LMTP (time-varying A_t). Pair with identify_repeated_outcomes (CausalTargeted helper over CausalDynamics TotalEffectQuery) when each Y_t shares a backdoor set. Parametric MSMs project that profile (or stacked means μ(ta)) onto a design with GLS: run_parametric_repeated_msm / ParametricRepeatedOutcomeMSM (:constant, :linear_time, :factor_time, custom matrices, or :mean_treatment_time with target=:mean). Pass cluster=:cluster (or a length-n id vector) for a cluster-robust sandwich on widehatSigma; point estimates are unchanged (sampling hierarchy, not BLUP). Synthetic gates: simulate_repeated_outcome_ate, simulate_mean_treatment_time_msm. Generative nested U DGPs live in CausalDynamics (RandomEffectSpec).\n\nGaussian MMRM (optional MixedModels extension). For a static treatment and repeated Gaussian outcomes in long (id, time, Y) form, fit_mmrm / run_mmrm fit outcome ~ treatment * time + baseline + (1 | id) (default) or an :unstructured random-effects approximation (1 + visit | id) with an internal categorical visit factor. mixed_g_computation supplies visit-specific contrasts through the common parametric standardisation layer (default random_effects=:zero). Each visit is standardised over its observed rows: with unbalanced follow-up, visit means may represent different empirical populations and use row weights rather than equal subject or cluster weights. This is a parametric trial-style reference beside LMTP/MSM, not a substitute for discrete longitudinal LMTP. The covariance choice in fit_mmrm describes the fitted random-effects structure; it is separate from g-computation's coefficient uncertainty. Requires the optional extension dependencies. Stress: docs/stress/mmrm_stress.qmd.\n\nTransport weights. domain_transport_weights / transport_weighted_mean provide marginal IPTW-style domain reweighting after a CausalDynamics TransportQuery certificate. Decision. choose_policy evaluates labelled Estimands with execute_estimand and returns a PolicyChoice (max/min scalar TE).","title":"Targeting and uncertainty contract"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Targeted-learning,-Super-Learner,-and-cross-fitting","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Adjustment covariates are encoded before they enter Super Learner. An internal fitted schema uses StatsModels default DummyCoding for string and categorical columns and preserves its levels, dummy-column meanings, and column order across all folds. Numeric values (including ordinary integer columns) remain one continuous column, while Bool becomes numeric 0/1. Missing-data handling (:drop, :impute, :ipcw, or :ipcw_impute) occurs before schema fitting; the schema itself does not impute or standardise.\n\nFinite-support treatments use DiscreteTreatmentPolicy: T=1 via run_discrete_lmtp, multi-time via SequentialPolicy policies. Integer columns remain numeric on the continuous MTP path unless factorised. Random Forest, EvoTrees, and XGBoost receive numeric dummy columns rather than native categorical features; Random Forest therefore computes mtry from the encoded feature count.\n\nMissing data. Full policy catalogue: Missingness. In brief: incompleteness is stratum × PCH rung (Structural R in CausalDynamics; Dynamical sequential / survival gaps; Observable policies here). handle_missing_data supports :drop, :impute, :ipcw, and :ipcw_impute before schema fitting; IPCW weights enter IF summaries so :drop and :ipcw need not coincide. Survival censoring IPCW is not MAR missing S_T. Opt-in impute_posterior + run_lmtp_grid(...; imputation=) pools under a MAR certificate. Stress: missingness grid stress.\n\nG-computation SEs. run_gcomp percentile intervals use a refitting bootstrap (redraw rows, recompute cross-fitted Q). Missing-data strategies also refit their response weights and covariate preprocessing on each resample of the original rows; bootstrap_refits_missingness records this behaviour. With estimator=:plugin, n_boot=0 returns the point estimate and unavailable inferential SE/limits (NaN). Its prediction_se measures fitted-contrast variation only. For a complete-data binary ATE with independent rows, estimator=:onestep adds the cross-fitted AIPW correction and uses the matching influence curve. It requires one outcome fit and, unless propensity is known, one propensity fit per fold, using the package's usual cross-validated binomial stack rather than inverse training-MSE weighting. A known scalar or row-aligned probability vector can describe randomised treatment assignment. Both avoid hundreds of bootstrap outcome-model refits. This is a distinct estimator; it does not silently replace plug-in g-computation.\n\nCovariance and sampling units. Discrete static contrasts share outcome fits and calculate variance from paired influence contributions. Cluster IDs remain aligned after missingness handling; IPCW weighting precedes cluster aggregation. Cluster-aware folds keep dependent rows together. Bootstrap cluster copies receive distinct covariance IDs while copies of the same original sampling unit remain in one validation fold; unsuccessful replicates are counted. A result with too few successful draws has status=:insufficient_success and unavailable limits, rather than a substituted point-fit interval. Staggered DiD aggregation retains shared-control and repeated-unit covariance. Cell-only external tables without unit influence contributions report se=NaN and covariance_status=:unavailable; the former independence approximation is retained separately as independence_se and is not presented as the contrast's inferential SE.\n\nPolicy regularity. A hard clamp maps ranges of a continuous exposure onto boundary points, creating atoms; ordinary nonparametric EIF intervals are therefore unavailable for nonzero shifts. The default run_lmtp_grid warns and returns point estimates with inference_status=:unsupported_clamped_policy and NaN limits. For a regular smooth alternative, construct policy = smooth_bounded_logit_shift_policy(L, U) with prespecified finite bounds and run run_lmtp_grid(...; policy, weight_truncation=false, density_ratio=:gaussian). Its δ is an additive shift on logit((A-L)/(U-L)); this is a different estimand from the additive raw-scale policy. The transform is smooth and invertible for exposures strictly inside (L,U). The uncapped EIF route still requires conditional overlap, correctly specified or sufficiently accurate nuisance fits, and suitable moments; these conditions do not guarantee nominal small-sample coverage. See Díaz et al. (2023) for the regularity conditions for continuous-exposure longitudinal MTPs.\n\nFinite ratio caps also gate inferential output; weight_truncation=false is restricted to documented regular policy classes. No small-n default selects a cap by minimising the empirical variance alone. The multiplier helper centres columns internally, but resampling cannot repair a biased or incomplete score. Continuous point-intervention contrasts return unavailable inferential SEs. Discrete sequential LMTP uses the full cumulative-ratio one-step score; continuous sequential/survival routes preserve point fits but report unavailable uncertainty until their policy and full score contract are supported. Mean-imputed covariates and missingness indicators are preprocessing, rather than a general causal recovery method. Grid fits that actually impute covariates retain point estimates but report :unsupported_covariate_imputation and unavailable inferential limits; this is distinct from posterior draws for missing outcomes under a declared Gaussian MAR model.\n\nThis fitted-schema path covers CausalTargeted's g-computation, LMTP Gaussian and classification/hybrid density-ratio nuisances, fold caching, sequential LMTP, survival LMTP, and missing-data nuisance models. CausalMediation reuses fit_covariate_schema / design_matrix for fold-stable string and categorical covariates when running run_mediation_grid.\n\nParametric regression standardisation. fit_parametric_gcomp fits an outcome model; gcomp_mean, gcomp_contrast, and gcomp_interaction select target rows, apply interventions to copies, predict on the response scale, and average over those rows. The default backend=:glm retains Gaussian/identity, binomial/logit, Gamma/log, and fixed or estimated NB2/log fits. The optional backend=:mixed uses supported Gaussian LMM and fixed- or estimated-shape NB2 random-intercept fits. run_parametric_gcomp fits and contrasts in one call. These are parametric references separate from cross-fitted run_gcomp and LMTP.\n\nTraining and target tables may have different rows, sizes, and order. The target need not contain an outcome column. Target selection precedes intervention; gcomp_interaction compares treatment effects within the observed modifier subgroups. Each selected target predictor must be complete-case, without silent row dropping or imputation. Interventions rebuild the fitted formula schema, including categorical contrasts, transformations, and interactions; unseen fixed-effect levels are rejected. Mixed grouping identifiers cannot be intervened on. A grouping factor such as site need not be a treatment-assignment unit: treatment may vary between individuals within a site. The static-treatment restriction remains in the repeated-outcome convenience interfaces.\n\nFor mixed predictions, random_effects=:zero sets latent effects to zero; :marginal integrates the response mean over the supported fitted Gaussian random-effects distribution. These agree for Gaussian identity-link models, including supported random coefficients. For NB2/log random-intercept models, :marginal multiplies exp(Xβ) by exp(σ_b²/2). It does not average fitted subject-specific effects. State the mode explicitly for new nonlinear mixed calls. GLM inference keeps covariance=:hc3 by default, with :model and :none available; mixed fixed-effect delta inference uses the fitted fixed-effect covariance. Ratios use log-scale intervals and multiple effects use their joint coefficient covariance. Delta inference holds fitted shape, dispersion, and variance components fixed. Marginalising predictions does not propagate their estimation uncertainty. The existing estimated-shape NB2 mixed_g_computation parametric bootstrap refits the hierarchical model; bootstrap_gcomp_interaction remains the GLM row-refit bootstrap. Unsupported mixed bootstrap requests fail explicitly. These procedures do not alter the cross-fitted inference of run_gcomp.\n\nOutcome backend Fitting Prediction modes G-computation uncertainty\nGLM Gaussian, binomial, Gamma Formula fit Response scale HC3, model, none; GLM interaction row bootstrap\nGLM NB2, fixed or estimated shape Formula fit Response scale HC3/model/none conditional on shape; refit interaction bootstrap\nGaussian LMM Optional backend=:mixed or fitted-model adapter :zero, :marginal (same mean under identity link) Fixed-effect model delta covariance; variance components held fixed\nNB2/log mixed, fixed shape Optional backend=:mixed or fitted-model adapter; supported random intercept :zero, :marginal Fixed-effect model delta covariance; shape and variance held fixed\nNB2/log mixed, estimated shape Dedicated fit_profiled_nb2, optional backend=:mixed, or fitted-model adapter :zero, :marginal Fixed-effect delta; legacy trajectory parametric bootstrap refits shape and variance\n\nThe same GLM workflow remains valid:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, DataFrames, StatsModels\ntraining = DataFrame(Y=[1.0, 2.0, 2.5, 3.5, 1.3, 2.2],\n                     A=[0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1], X=[0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 2.0, 2.0])\nfit = fit_parametric_gcomp(@formula(Y ~ A + X), training; family=:gaussian)\ngcomp_contrast(fit, select(training, Not(:Y)); treatment=:A,\n               reference=0, comparison=1)\n\nFor a non-longitudinal mixed model, individuals can have different treatments within a site. Load the optional extension stack before fitting:\n\nusing CausalTargeted, MixedModels, FastGaussQuadrature, NLopt, SpecialFunctions\nusing DataFrames, StatsModels, Random, Distributions\nrng = MersenneTwister(30)\ntraining = DataFrame(site=repeat(1:16, inner=6),\n                     A=repeat([0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 1.0], 16),\n                     Group=repeat([\"low\", \"high\"], inner=3, outer=16),\n                     X=randn(rng, 96))\nsite_effect = 0.4 .* randn(rng, 16)\ntraining.Y = 1 .+ 0.6 .* training.A .+ 0.3 .* training.X .+\n             0.2 .* (training.Group .== \"high\") .+\n             site_effect[training.site] .+ 0.1 .* randn(rng, 96)\nfit = fit_parametric_gcomp(@formula(Y ~ A * Group + X + (1 | site)), training;\n                           backend=:mixed, family=:gaussian, id=:site)\ntarget = select(training, Not(:Y))\ngcomp_contrast(fit, target; treatment=:A, reference=0.0,\n               comparison=1.0, random_effects=:zero)\ngcomp_interaction(fit, target; treatment=:A, reference=0.0,\n                  comparison=1.0, modifier=:Group,\n                  modifier_reference=\"low\", modifier_comparison=\"high\",\n                  random_effects=:zero)\n\nFor an NB2/log mixed random-intercept model, the two modes have different means. theta supplies a fixed shape; omitting it selects the dedicated estimated-shape fitter:\n\nμ = exp.(0.2 .+ 0.4 .* training.A .+ 0.1 .* training.X .+\n         site_effect[training.site])\ntraining.Count = [rand(rng, NegativeBinomial(2.0, 2.0/(2.0+m))) for m in μ]\nnbfit = fit_parametric_gcomp(@formula(Count ~ A + X + (1 | site)), training;\n                             backend=:mixed, family=:negbin, theta=2.0, id=:site)\ngcomp_contrast(nbfit, select(training, Not(:Count)); treatment=:A,\n               reference=0.0, comparison=1.0, scale=:ratio,\n               random_effects=:marginal)\n\nThe trial-style repeated-outcome front end remains available. Here arm is static within each subject; each observed visit contributes its actual rows:\n\npanel = DataFrame(subject=repeat(1:24, inner=3), visit=repeat(0:2, 24),\n                  arm=repeat(Float64.(isodd.(1:24)), inner=3),\n                  W=repeat(randn(rng, 24), inner=3))\npanel.Y = 1 .+ 0.5 .* panel.arm .+ 0.2 .* panel.visit .+\n          0.1 .* panel.W .+ 0.3 .* randn(rng, 72)\nrun_mmrm(panel; outcome=:Y, treatment=:arm, time=:visit,\n         id=:subject, baseline=[:W], random_effects=:zero)\n\nNeither random effects nor parametric fitting identify a causal effect by themselves. Interpretation still requires an appropriate treatment and target population definition, exchangeability, positivity, consistency, and a suitable outcome model.\n\nTopic Primary sources CausalTargeted surface\nTMLE van der Laan & Rubin (2006); van der Laan & Rose (2011, 2018) estimator=:tmle, fluctuation helpers\nSuper Learner van der Laan, Polley & Hubbard (2007); Phillips et al. (2023) fit_super_learner; metalearners :nnls, :nnloglik, :cv_selector (dSL), :invmse; family=:multinomial (sl3-style simplex)\nOptional MLJ linear nuisances MLJ / MLJLinearModels (weakdep) :mlj_ridge, :mlj_lasso, :mlj_elasticnet, :mlj_logistic after using MLJ, MLJLinearModels (features standardised; never in small-n presets)\nOptional Random Forest nuisance MLJ / MLJDecisionTreeInterface (DecisionTree.jl weakdep) :randomforest after using MLJ, MLJDecisionTreeInterface (unscaled features; regression or probabilistic classification)\nOptional XGBoost nuisance MLJ / MLJXGBoostInterface (XGBoost.jl weakdep) :xgboost after using MLJ, MLJXGBoostInterface (unscaled features; regression or probabilistic classification)\nOptional neural nuisances MLJFlux (Flux) :mlj_mlp, :mlj_nn_binary after using MLJFlux — never in small-n presets\nCross-fitting / sample splitting Zheng & van der Laan (2011); Chernozhukov et al. (2018) crossfit_indices, fold caches\nApplied TMLE overview Schuler & Rose (2017) Pedagogical pointer\n\nAt **small n***, rich libraries overfit. recommend_run_options / adaptive_learners prefer lean GLM/mean stacks when n < 80, consistent with the Super Learner principle that the library must be *estimable at the sample size at hand. For binary propensity or outcome nuisances where probability calibration matters, fit_super_learner(...; family=:binomial) defaults to :nnloglik (R method.NNloglik). The discrete Super Learner is metalearner=:cv_selector (Phillips dSL / sl3 Lrnr_cv_selector). Pass a nested_sl_candidate in the library to nest an ensemble Super Learner inside that selector (eSL-inside-dSL; opt-in, not a new default). LMTP density-ratio classifiers stay on :invmse. Categorical outcomes use family=:multinomial (convex combination of class-probability matrices; sl3 loss_loglik_multinomial). Categorical treatments in LMTP use run_discrete_lmtp with Díaz–Williams classification density ratios, not a multinomial propensity. Arm contrasts (e.g. SS vs R) can use run_discrete_lmtp_contrast, which fits two static policies and differences the estimates (independent SE approximation). For binary presence outcomes (Y ∈ {0,1}), pass family_outcome = :binomial so outcome nuisances use logistic Super Learner fits rather than the default Gaussian location model:\n\nrun_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df, :arm, :infected;\n    arm_hi = \"SS\", arm_ref = \"R\", levels = [\"R\", \"SS\", \"SC\"],\n    baseline = [:weight],\n    family_outcome = :binomial,\n    learners_outcome = (:glm, :mean),\n)\n\nFor nonnegative count outcomes (parasite totals, colony counts), use family_outcome = :poisson or :negbin. Nuisance models use log-link GLMs; profiled NB shape per cross-fit fold; TMLE targeting uses a log-link fluctuation on the fitted mean. The reported estimand is EY mid mathrmdo(A) on the count scale (#36):\n\nrun_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df, :arm, :count;\n    arm_hi = 1, arm_ref = 0, levels = [0, 1],\n    baseline = [:weight],\n    family_outcome = :negbin,\n    learners_outcome = (:glm_nb, :mean),\n)\n\nWhen excess zeros are scientifically co-primary, prefer the two-part hurdle path below rather than a single count model.\n\nThe same family_outcome keyword is forwarded by run_lmtp_grid and run_discrete_lmtp. Multi-time factor recodes use the same policies on run_sequential_lmtp. Optional MLJ / MLP candidates are opt-in: they can improve recovery on some DGPs in a single synthetic draw while diluting others (overfitting vs generalisation). Prefer repeated Monte Carlo and library ablations before changing defaults.","title":"Targeted learning, Super Learner, and cross-fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Panel-bridge-(CausalDynamics-CausalTargeted)","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"CausalDynamics provides the temporal identification certificate, maps the variables in the query to panel columns, and checks empirical support when data are supplied. CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation uses those inputs to select an estimator and outcome family and returns a CausalTargeted.EstimationPlan. run_estimation_plan executes discrete and hurdle plans. It also accepts legacy CausalDynamics.EstimationPlan values, including those from CausalDynamics.plan_session_estimation.\n\nThe runner refuses queries that the certificate does not identify by default. With on_unidentified = :exploratory, a descriptive result carries identification_status = :not_identified_by_procedure. A structural skip is refused in either mode. Complete-case support counts describe the available analysis rows. A time-indexed missingness certificate is carried through the plan as capture_conditioned.\n\nplan.engine Runner Notes\n:discrete_lmtp run_estimation_plan(...; arm_hi, arm_ref, levels) Same-occasion arm contrast; default for static grid designs\n:two_part_discrete_lmtp run_estimation_plan(...; arm_hi, arm_ref, levels) Uses plan.presence_col / plan.intensity_col from OutcomeKind.hurdle\n:sequential_lmtp sequential_spec_from_identification + run_sequential_lmtp Not wrapped by run_estimation_plan\n:lmtp_grid run_lmtp_grid Continuous shift LMTP; pass lower_q / upper_q, not shift\n\nusing CausalDynamics, CausalTargeted, DataFrames\n\nspec = TemporalDAGSpec(\n    nodes = [\n        TemporalNodeSpec(\n            :grid_type;\n            temporal_support = FromOnsetSupport(0),\n            value_representation = :attribute,\n            causal_role = :assigned,\n        ),\n        TemporalNodeSpec(:fec; value_representation = :state),\n    ],\n    edges = [LaggedEdge(:grid_type, :fec, 0)],\n)\nu = unroll_temporal_dag(spec, 4)\nquery = TemporalEffectQuery(:grid_type, :fec, 2, 2)\nplan = CausalTargeted.plan_targeted_estimation(\n    u, query, propertynames(wide);\n    data = wide,              # optional: sets plan.estimability\n    outcome_specs = Dict(     # optional: hurdle → :two_part_discrete_lmtp\n        :fec => NodeOutcomeSpec(OutcomeKind.hurdle, :fec_bin, :fec_intensity),\n    ),\n)\nres = run_estimation_plan(\n    wide, plan;\n    arm_hi = \"SS\", arm_ref = \"R\", levels = [\"R\", \"SS\", \"SC\"],\n    learners_outcome = recommend_run_options(nrow(wide)).learners_outcome,\n)\n\nFor queries on the same variable at a later time, the planner selects :sequential_lmtp. For continuous shift plans, see run_lmtp_grid for the grid bounds lower_q and upper_q. Supply shifts with deltas. The stress notebook uses private cohort data supplied locally.","title":"Panel bridge (CausalDynamics → CausalTargeted)"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Super-Learner-candidate-roles","page":"Methods & estimators","text":":glm, :glm_interact, and :glm_quad provide ordinary, interaction-expanded, and quadratic-expanded GLMs (logistic under family=:binomial).\n:glmnet and its lasso/ridge aliases provide regularised linear candidates through MLJLinearModels.\n:randomforest provides a bagged Random Forest through MLJ and DecisionTree.jl. Its conservative defaults support stable nuisance estimation at modest sample sizes.\n:evotree and :evotree_deep provide Julia gradient boosting through EvoTrees.jl.\n:xgboost provides gradient boosting through MLJ and XGBoost.jl.\n:mlj_mlp and :mlj_nn_binary provide optional MLJFlux neural candidates.\n:mean is the intercept-only reference and fallback learner. It is not a standalone treatment-contrast engine: run_gcomp, run_lmtp_grid, and related entry points reject learners=(:mean,) with an ArgumentError. Always pair :mean with at least one treatment-dependent candidate (typically :glm).\n\nThe base DEFAULT_SL_LEARNERS remains (:glm, :mean). RICH_SL_LEARNERS adds :randomforest for bagging/model-class diversity, while :xgboost remains explicit opt-in because the rich library already contains EvoTrees boosting. SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS and adaptive_learners remain conservative and do not add either flexible tree learner automatically.\n\nusing CausalTargeted, MLJ, MLJLinearModels\nusing MLJDecisionTreeInterface, MLJXGBoostInterface\n\nfit = fit_super_learner(\n    X,\n    y;\n    learners = (:glm, :glmnet, :randomforest, :xgboost, :mean),\n)\n\nCurrent Random Forest configuration:\n\nMLJ parameter or behaviour Value\nn_trees 500\nn_subfeatures computed at fit time as max(1, floor(sqrt(number of predictors)))\nmin_samples_leaf 5\nbootstrap sampling enabled\nsampling_fraction 1.0\nrng MersenneTwister(42)\n\nCurrent XGBoost defaults:\n\nMLJ parameter Default\nnum_round 100\nmax_depth 2\neta 0.05\nmin_child_weight 5\nsubsample 0.8\ncolsample_bytree 0.8\nlambda 1\nalpha 0\ngamma 0\nnthread 1\nseed 42","title":"Super Learner candidate roles"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Interventional-mediation-grids","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Natural direct/indirect effects (Pearl, 2001; Robins & Greenland, 1992; VanderWeele, 2015) require cross-world assumptions that fail under intermediate confounding. Interventional (randomised interventional) effects (Vansteelandt & Daniel, 2017) and stochastic intervention mediation (Díaz & Hejazi, 2020; Hejazi et al., 2023) weaken those assumptions. Liu, Williams, Rudolph & Díaz (2024) unify modern mediation estimands with MTPs; the R package crumble (Liu et al., 2025 tutorial) is a software companion.\n\nIn the CDCS package set, mediation estimation is owned by CausalMediation.jl, where CausalMediation.run_mediation is the preferred entry point. CausalTargeted provides compatibility façades (run_mediation_grid, mediation_n_mc_sweep, mediation_stability_summary, build_mediation_fold_cache, and soft-deprecated run_crumble_* / :crumble aliases) that delegate to CausalMediation.jl once the weak dependency is loaded.\n\nTopic Primary sources CausalMediation / façade surface\nStochastic mediation (in)direct effects Díaz & Hejazi (2020), JRSS-B (doi) Conceptual basis for continuous-A mediation\nStochastic interventional effects with intermediate confounding Hejazi et al. (2023), Biostatistics (doi) Design target for robust mediation contrasts\nUnified targeted mediation + MTP Liu et al. (2024), arXiv:2408.14620 CausalMediation.run_mediation (run_mediation_grid façade), MediationContrast\nSoftware companion (R) Liu et al. (2025), arXiv:2604.09902 Estimand catalogue; Julia uses mediation names\nClassical mediation textbook VanderWeele (2015) Interpreting NDE/NIE vs interventional contrasts\n\nImplementation note. The compatibility façade run_mediation_grid (delegating to CausalMediation.jl, where CausalMediation.run_mediation is preferred) estimates TE / NDE / NIE under continuous MTP shifts via nested Monte Carlo and cross-fitted nuisances. Nested-MC variability is first-class: the façade helpers mediation_n_mc_sweep and mediation_stability_summary quantify SE and sign stability across n_mc (essential at small n).\n\nA minimal mediation DAG (A → M → Y, A → Y) for interpreting those contrasts:\n\nusing DAGMakie, CairoMakie\n\nfig, _ax, _p = dagplot_mediation([\"A\", \"M\", \"Y\"])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nFold/δ cache. build_mediation_fold_cache (a compatibility façade delegating to CausalMediation.jl, alongside the LMTP analogue in CausalTargeted) reuses outcome / mediator / exposure fits across δ within folds—same statistical estimand, lower wall time.","title":"Interventional mediation grids"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Positivity-and-support","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Positivity (overlap) is necessary for identification of interventional means (Hernán & Robins, 2020; Petersen et al., 2012). MTPs are often designed so that shifted exposures remain in the support of the observed treatment law (Díaz et al., 2023).\n\nTopic Primary sources CausalTargeted surface\nDiagnosing positivity violations Petersen et al. (2012) positivity_report, positivity_markdown\nClamp / support diagnostics under additive shifts LMTP practice (Díaz et al., 2023) support / clamp helpers in mtp_common.jl; grid positivity=true","title":"Positivity and support"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Sensitivity-to-unmeasured-confounding","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Even with correct adjustment sets, estimates can tip under omitted confounding. CausalTargeted exposes diagnostic tipping-point and partial-R² calibrations inspired by Cinelli & Hazlett (2020); complementary classical tools include VanderWeele & Ding (2017) E-values and Rosenbaum (2002) sensitivity models.\n\nTopic Primary sources CausalTargeted surface\nPartial R² / robustness-value style OVB Cinelli & Hazlett (2020), JRSS-B partial_r2_calibration, sensitivity_report\nE-value VanderWeele & Ding (2017) Cite for reporting; not duplicated here\nDiscovery as sensitivity, not oracle Pearl (2009); Spirtes et al. (2000) discovery_adjustment_sensitivity, merge_discovery_sensitivity!\n\nNever silently replace a user DAG with a discovery graph in production defaults.","title":"Sensitivity to unmeasured confounding"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Outcome-paths-and-model-choice","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Biological and trial endpoints differ in support, time structure, and whether zero inflation is scientifically primary. The table below maps common patterns to CausalTargeted engines. All targeted paths share cross-fitted nuisances, optional handle_missing, and cluster= sandwich SEs where noted. None of these replace identification: pair with CausalDynamics certificates and the query ladder (association → intervention → counterfactual).\n\nOutcome pattern Primary estimand Engine / API When to prefer\nContinuous scalar under static arm EY mid mathrmdo(A) run_discrete_lmtp / run_lmtp_grid Single visit or summary outcome\nBinary presence I(Y0) Risk difference / probability run_discrete_lmtp + family_outcome=:binomial (#34) Infection, detection, any 01 endpoint\nZero-inflated semi-continuous P(Y0) and EY mid Y0 run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast (#35) Parasite EPG, shedding scores: presence co-primary\nRepeated Gaussian, static treatment Visit-specific τ(t) (marginal) run_repeated_outcome_msm (TMLE/IF) Nonparametric profile + joint widehatSigma\nRepeated Gaussian, static treatment LS-mean-style contrast (parametric) fit_mmrm / run_mmrm (#25; using MixedModels) Trial-style reference beside MSM/LMTP\nRepeated count / overdispersion, static treatment Marginal g-comp on count scale mixed_g_computation (NB2 ext) Hierarchical reference; not LMTP\nTime-varying treatment Sequential LMTP run_sequential_lmtp A_t shifts or factor policies\nCount / overdispersed integer EY mid mathrmdo(A) on count scale run_discrete_lmtp + family_outcome=:poisson or :negbin (#36) Parasite totals, colony counts; prefer two-part when zeros are co-primary\n\nTwo-part hurdle LMTP. Build presence (I(Y0)) and intensity (e.g. log(y) on positives, with missing when Y=0). Presence uses binomial outcome nuisances; intensity fits on the positive subsample only, so the reported contrast is conditional on observed infection, not the marginal EY mid mathrmdo(A) unless you define a composite estimand separately.\n\nres = run_two_part_discrete_lmtp_contrast(\n    df, :grid_type;\n    presence = :fec_bin,\n    intensity = :fec_logpos,\n    arm_hi = \"SS\", arm_ref = \"R\", levels = [\"R\", \"SS\", \"SC\"],\n    baseline = [:weight],\n    family_presence = :binomial,\n    cluster = :mouse_id,\n)\nres.presence.estimate    # co-primary: infection risk difference\nres.intensity.estimate   # secondary: log-burden among positives\n\nUse suggest_family_outcome or recommend_run_options(n; outcome=col) to set family_outcome for single-outcome runs. For DAG faithfulness on hurdle nodes, see the next section; for repeated Gaussian visits, compare MSM (IF) with MMRM (parametric) on the same panel before interpreting biology.","title":"Outcome paths and model choice"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Hurdle-CI-testing","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Zero-inflated biological outcomes are often operationalised as a two-part hurdle: presence I(Y  0) and log intensity among positives. Gaussian partial correlation on log(1 + Y) is misspecified for faithfulness checks on those nodes.\n\nUse partial correlation for continuous block variables. Use test_implied_hurdle_independences when a statement involves a hurdle-split node (binomial presence GLM; Gaussian intensity GLM among positives). String / categorical predictors (e.g. grid_type) use StatsModels DummyCoding, aligned with discrete LMTP (#34) and two-part LMTP (#35).\n\nresults = test_implied_hurdle_independences(\n    statements, df,\n    Dict(:fec => (:fec_bin, :fec_intensity), :eimeria => (:eimeria_bin, :eimeria_intensity)),\n)","title":"Hurdle CI testing"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Identification-certificates-(CausalDynamics-bridge)","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Estimation attaches provenance via identification_certificate / attach_run_metadata!. Upstream ID uses Pearl’s do-calculus toolkit (Pearl, 2009; Shpitser & Pearl, 2006) and g-methods (Robins, 1986; Robins, 2000). Time-indexed queries use TemporalDAGSpec / unroll_temporal_dag / TemporalEffectQuery in CausalDynamics (see that package’s References).","title":"Identification certificates (CausalDynamics bridge)"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#Small-*n*-profile","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Conservation biology, ecology, and early trials often have tens to low hundreds of units. recommend_folds, SMALL_N_SL_LEARNERS, and recommend_run_options encode memory-safe, positivity-aware defaults (parallel=false, higher mediation n_mc when n is small). See the Small-n checklist.","title":"Small-n profile"},{"category":"section","location":"methods/#What-we-deliberately-do-*not*-claim","page":"Methods & estimators","text":"Full parity with every option in R lmtp / crumble (GPU Riesz nets, all mediation estimand flavours, competing-risks survival LMTP).\nThat tipping-point / partial-R² helpers replace design-based identification.\nThat Super Learner at n ≈ 30 recovers oracle rates—diagnostics exist precisely because they often do not.","title":"What we deliberately do not claim"}]
}
